{"aliases": ["System → Packages → Runtimes → AI Libraries.", "Each layer depends on the one below it. Install in order, bottom to top."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_09c6d7a06c", "name": "Four-layer AI environment stack", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/4-Layer AI Environment Stack.md", "message": "The Four Layers", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Dev Environment Stack.md", "message": "The Four-Layer Stack", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "AI engineering environments decompose into four layers — system foundation (OS, shell, git, editor, GPU drivers), package managers, language runtimes, and AI/ML libraries — where each layer depends on the one below it and installation proceeds bottom to top."}
{"aliases": ["nvidia-smi OK + torch.cuda False → Runtimes layer issue."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_954ffc105c", "name": "GPU fault isolation via nvidia-smi and torch.cuda", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/4-Layer AI Environment Stack.md", "message": "GPU Diagnosis", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/4-Layer AI Environment Stack.md", "message": "healthy driver + CUDA-enabled torch build + missing CUDA/cuDNN runtime libraries → Runtimes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When nvidia-smi reports a working driver but torch.cuda reports CUDA unavailable, the fault lies in the Runtimes layer — the CUDA toolkit and CUDA/cuDNN runtime libraries or the CUDA-enabled torch build — rather than in the System layer's driver."}
{"aliases": ["Without activations, stacking linear layers collapses into one big linear function — no matter how deep."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_81e4fed9df", "name": "Linear layer stacking collapses without activations", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Activation Functions.md", "message": "intro paragraph", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Stacking linear layers without activation functions collapses into one big linear function no matter how deep the network, so activation functions are the source of the non-linearity that gives neural networks their representational power."}
{"aliases": ["ReLU is the default for hidden layers across almost all architectures"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_bdeb9c7ae8", "name": "ReLU is the default hidden-layer activation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Activation Functions.md", "message": "Common Functions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "ReLU has output range [0, ∞) and is the default activation for hidden layers across almost all architectures."}
{"aliases": ["Softmax converts logits to a probability distribution over C classes"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_6ffc2ae308", "name": "Softmax maps logits to a class distribution", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Activation Functions.md", "message": "Common Functions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Softmax converts a vector of logits into a probability distribution over C classes, giving outputs in (0, 1) that sum to 1."}
{"aliases": ["Do NOT put softmax in forward() when using nn.CrossEntropyLoss() — it applies softmax internally"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_a379bddd81", "name": "No softmax before CrossEntropyLoss", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Activation Functions.md", "message": "Common Functions (Critical note)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Softmax must not be applied in forward() when the loss is nn.CrossEntropyLoss(), because that loss applies softmax internally."}
{"aliases": ["Acutest is a single-header C/C++ unit-testing facility."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_0f3ef9e9d0", "name": "Acutest is a vendored single-header test facility", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Acutest Unit Testing.md", "message": "Overview", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Acutest is a single-header C/C++ unit-testing facility vendored into a project as acutest.h, so it requires no library installation or linker flag, and it generates main() itself while the test file only defines test functions and a registry."}
{"aliases": ["must end with the sentinel `{ NULL, NULL }` so the runner knows where the list stops"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_f71d1ddc0e", "name": "TEST_LIST requires a NULL sentinel", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Acutest Unit Testing.md", "message": "Registering Tests: TEST_LIST", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Acutest test functions are plain C functions with prototype void test_name(void) that are mapped to readable names in the TEST_LIST array, and that array must end with the sentinel { NULL, NULL } so the runner knows where the list stops."}
{"aliases": ["CHECK gathers all failures and lets the test finish; ASSERT stops immediately when continuing would crash."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_3f2656eb18", "name": "TEST_CHECK continues, TEST_ASSERT aborts", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Acutest Unit Testing.md", "message": "The Assertion Macros", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Acutest Unit Testing.md", "message": "Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "TEST_CHECK logs a failure with its file and line and continues the test, whereas TEST_ASSERT logs and immediately aborts the current test; TEST_CHECK is the default choice and TEST_ASSERT is reserved as a shield before crash-prone operations such as NULL-pointer dereferences or array bounds."}
{"aliases": ["by default Acutest runs each test in its own isolated child process"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_5e131ccb54", "name": "Acutest isolates each test in a child process", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Acutest Unit Testing.md", "message": "Why TEST_ASSERT's \"dire\" consequences are acceptable", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "By default Acutest runs each test in its own isolated child process, so when the child aborts the operating system reclaims all of that test's memory and file descriptors instead of letting them leak into other tests."}
{"aliases": ["prefer `TEST_CHECK` \"unless you know exactly what you do and why you chose TEST_ASSERT\""], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_202d966ca9", "name": "Aborting a test is tolerable under process isolation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Acutest Unit Testing.md", "message": "Why TEST_ASSERT's \"dire\" consequences are acceptable", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Skipping destructors via TEST_ASSERT's abort is tolerable in practice because Acutest's per-test process isolation makes the OS reclaim the aborted test's resources and short-lived fixtures make the skipped cleanup irrelevant to results, although the README still advises preferring TEST_CHECK unless the reason for choosing TEST_ASSERT is known exactly."}
{"aliases": ["Adam = RMSProp + momentum + bias correction."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_1e44bffa88", "name": "Adam combines momentum, RMSProp, and bias correction", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Adam (adaptive moments).md", "message": "intro paragraph", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Adam (adaptive moments).md", "message": "Optimizer lineage (Ruder's genealogy)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Adam maintains two running averages per weight — the first moment m (mean gradient, i.e. momentum) and the second moment v (mean squared gradient, as in RMSProp) — and adds a bias-correction step, giving each weight its own adaptive learning rate without manual tuning."}
{"aliases": ["at t=1, m̂ = g (the true gradient, unshrunk)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_dd570ef650", "name": "Adam bias correction removes the zero-init cold start", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Adam (adaptive moments).md", "message": "Why bias correction", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Adam initializes both m and v to zero, which biases early estimates downward and makes the first steps timid; dividing by (1 - β1^t) and (1 - β2^t) cancels that cold-start artifact, so at t = 1 the corrected first moment equals the true gradient and as t grows the correction fades to 1 because β^t → 0."}
{"aliases": ["w ← w − η m̂ / (√v̂ + ε)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_83c18bba5f", "name": "Adam update rule and default hyperparameters", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Adam (adaptive moments).md", "message": "The equations (Kingma & Ba, 2015)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Adam updates each weight as w ← w - η·m̂/(√v̂ + ε), where m̂ and v̂ are the bias-corrected first and second moments, with defaults β1 = 0.9, β2 = 0.999, ε = 10^-8, and η = 0.001."}
{"aliases": ["m̂/√v̂ is dimensionless (gradient units over gradient units) ≈ ±1"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_823bd5e47d", "name": "Adam behaves as scale-invariant sign descent", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Adam (adaptive moments).md", "message": "Retrieval log (2026-09-26) — the units story", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because bias correction makes m̂ ≈ g and v̂ ≈ g^2 early in training, Adam's update is approximately η·sign(g): the ratio m̂/√v̂ is dimensionless (gradient units over gradient units) and near ±1, so Adam asks only which direction to step and moves by roughly a fixed learning rate, which makes it scale-invariant."}
{"aliases": ["SGD + momentum often achieves better final accuracy when tuning budget allows", "The choice is task-dependent, not a fixed hierarchy."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_c4ed7ea7bd", "name": "Adam versus SGD with momentum is task-dependent", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Adam (adaptive moments).md", "message": "When to use", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Adam (adaptive moments).md", "message": "Role summary (handwritten notes, 2026-09-08)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Adam is the robust out-of-the-box default optimizer, but SGD with momentum often reaches better final accuracy when tuning budget allows, because SGD's residual noise prevents settling into sharp minima that generalize worse while Adam's smooth adaptation can land in a sharp narrow valley; the choice is task-dependent rather than a fixed hierarchy."}
{"aliases": ["Many bigrams never appear in training (e.g. \"JQ\" in English names)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_b504ac8f2b", "name": "Unsmoothed bigram models assign zero to unseen bigrams", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Add-1 Smoothing.md", "message": "Why It's Needed", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An unsmoothed bigram model assigns probability zero to every bigram that never appeared in training, for example the bigram \"JQ\" in English names."}
{"aliases": ["NLL loss computes -ln(P) → -ln(0) = +∞ → entire average loss becomes NaN"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_318a62435d", "name": "Negative log-likelihood of a zero-probability token is infinite", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Add-1 Smoothing.md", "message": "Why It's Needed", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The negative log-likelihood loss computes -ln(P), so a token assigned probability zero yields -ln(0) = +∞ and turns the entire average loss into NaN."}
{"aliases": ["Add-1 (Laplace) smoothing adds a small fake count to every possible bigram entry, ensuring no entry is ever zero."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_b3fab74520", "name": "Add-1 smoothing keeps the bigram loss finite", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Add-1 Smoothing.md", "message": "Overview", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Add-1 Smoothing.md", "message": "Effect", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Add-1 (Laplace) smoothing adds a fake count of one to every possible bigram entry so that no entry is ever zero, which keeps the loss finite and trainable — for example P(JQ) ≈ 0.00003 gives -ln(0.00003) ≈ 10.4 instead of ∞."}
{"aliases": ["Adding 1 to every count is equivalent to L2 regularization in the neural network version"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_0af2533386", "name": "Add-1 smoothing equals L2 regularization", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Add-1 Smoothing.md", "message": "Implementation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Adding 1 to every bigram count is equivalent to L2 regularization in the neural-network version, pulling weights toward zero, i.e. toward the uniform distribution."}
{"aliases": ["Higher smoothing → more uniform distribution → lower confidence on seen patterns."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_8ad197335d", "name": "Smoothing strength trades confidence for uniformity", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Add-1 Smoothing.md", "message": "Trade-off", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Higher smoothing produces a more uniform distribution and lowers confidence on seen patterns, so the fake count value used as smoothing strength is tuned on a dev set."}
{"aliases": ["African languages are **agglutinative** — they form words by combining many morphemes."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_f30b88617b", "name": "Agglutination makes word-level tokenization explode", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/African Language NLP.md", "message": "The Core Challenge", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "African languages are agglutinative — words are formed by combining many morphemes, as in \"omulwadde\" = \"omu\" (person prefix) + \"lwadde\" (sick) — so word-level tokenization produces enormous vocabularies because every morpheme combination creates a new word."}
{"aliases": ["**Subword tokenization** (BPE, SentencePiece) splits into meaningful chunks — the best balance for agglutinative languages"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_992585fab1", "name": "Subword tokenization suits agglutinative languages", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/African Language NLP.md", "message": "Solutions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Subword tokenization methods such as BPE and SentencePiece split words into meaningful chunks and are the best balance for agglutinative languages, whose word-level vocabularies would otherwise explode."}
{"aliases": ["**Character-level models** avoid the vocabulary explosion entirely (tiny vocab, but longer sequences)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_6813981966", "name": "Character-level models trade tiny vocab for long sequences", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/African Language NLP.md", "message": "Solutions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Character-level models avoid the vocabulary explosion of agglutinative languages entirely, at the cost of a tiny vocabulary paired with much longer sequences."}
{"aliases": ["Don't abandon the project — the challenge is data, not architecture."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_8a18c688a4", "name": "Low-resource NLP is data-limited, not architecture-limited", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/African Language NLP.md", "message": "Solutions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/African Language NLP.md", "message": "Practical Advice", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When no pre-trained embeddings exist for a target language, the options are training from scratch on available data or using a multilingual model (mBERT, XLM-R, AfroLM) that has seen related languages during pre-training; the challenge in such projects is data, not architecture."}
{"aliases": ["AI bugs are silent — they don't crash, they just train on garbage and produce a beautiful loss curve."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_ac4d1de912", "name": "AI bugs are silent across three inspection levels", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Debugging and Profiling.md", "message": "Core Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "AI bugs usually do not crash — they train on garbage and still produce a clean loss curve — so debugging inspects tensors at three levels (standard Python, tensor operations, and training dynamics), and roughly 80% of AI bugs live in the first two levels."}
{"aliases": ["Causes: LR too high, division by zero, log(0), exploding gradients in RNNs."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_f3a47d7a4c", "name": "Causes of NaN training loss", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Debugging and Profiling.md", "message": "NaN Loss", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A NaN training loss can arise from a learning rate that is too high, division by zero, log(0), or exploding gradients in RNNs, so both the loss and the parameter gradients should be checked for NaN and Inf."}
{"aliases": ["A tensor silently on CPU while model is on GPU just makes training slow (no error)."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_620dd7423a", "name": "Wrong device placement fails silently", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Debugging and Profiling.md", "message": "Wrong Device", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A tensor left on CPU while the model is on GPU raises no error and merely makes training slow, so device placement must be verified explicitly."}
{"aliases": ["Loss not decreasing → LR too low", "Loss oscillating → LR too high", "Train loss ↓, val loss ↑ → overfitting"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_e9c72daf43", "name": "Loss curve patterns diagnose LR and overfitting", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Debugging and Profiling.md", "message": "TensorBoard — Interpretation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A loss that fails to decrease indicates a learning rate that is too low, an oscillating loss indicates a learning rate that is too high, and training loss falling while validation loss rises indicates overfitting."}
{"aliases": ["Linux organizes everything under a single root `/`."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_99452324bf", "name": "Linux single-root filesystem layout", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Linux for AI.md", "message": "File System Layout", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Linux organizes everything under a single root \"/\", where /home/<user> holds user files, /tmp holds temporary files cleared on reboot, /usr holds system programs and libraries, /etc holds configuration files, /var/log holds logs, /mnt and /media hold external volumes, and /proc and /sys are virtual files exposing kernel and hardware information."}
{"aliases": ["Case-insensitive FS | Case-sensitive FS | `file Model.py` ≠ `file model.py`"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_bb465569bf", "name": "Linux filesystems are case-sensitive", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Linux for AI.md", "message": "macOS → Linux Gotchas", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Linux filesystems are case-sensitive, so Model.py and model.py are different files, unlike the default case-insensitive macOS filesystem."}
{"aliases": ["Use `rsync` over `scp` for anything large — it only transfers changed bytes and handles interrupted connections."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_9db4d83f22", "name": "rsync preferred over scp for large transfers", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Linux for AI.md", "message": "Networking Tools", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "rsync is preferable to scp for large or interruption-prone transfers because it transfers only changed bytes and resumes after a broken connection."}
{"aliases": ["GPU passthrough works with NVIDIA drivers installed on **Windows** side (not Linux)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_7bad090c71", "name": "WSL2 GPU passthrough uses Windows-side drivers", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Linux for AI.md", "message": "WSL2 (Windows Subsystem for Linux)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "WSL2 gives Windows users a real Linux environment without dual-booting, and GPU passthrough in WSL2 works with NVIDIA drivers installed on the Windows side, not the Linux side."}
{"aliases": ["**uv** — 10-100x faster pip replacement"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_efe007a693", "name": "uv as a fast pip replacement", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Dev Environment Stack.md", "message": "Key Tools", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "uv is a pip replacement reported as 10-100x faster that handles Python versions, virtual environments, and dependency resolution in one CLI, and it is preferred over raw pip."}
{"aliases": ["read all 2P bytes of weights per token"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_35ff145855", "name": "Decode re-reads all weights per token", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering Roadmap v2.md", "message": "C3 resource 4 (kipply, Transformer Inference Arithmetic): \"read all 2P bytes of weights per token\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Generating one token with an autoregressive transformer requires reading all 2P bytes of the model's weights, where P is the number of parameters."}
{"aliases": ["208:1 FLOPS/bandwidth ratio"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_0a3689f11d", "name": "GPU FLOPS-to-bandwidth ratio is 208:1", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering Roadmap v2.md", "message": "\"bound below the 208:1 FLOPS/bandwidth ratio\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The GPU arithmetic used for the decode analysis has a FLOPS-to-memory-bandwidth ratio of 208:1."}
{"aliases": ["Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_d8495c4773", "name": "Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering Roadmap v2.md", "message": "Learning outcomes table: \"1. Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Transformer decoding is memory-bandwidth-bound because re-reading all weights for a single token gives an arithmetic intensity below the machine's 208:1 FLOPS-to-bandwidth balance point."}
{"aliases": ["PagedAttention exists because KV cache fragmentation wastes HBM.", "Quantization exists because decode is memory-bound.", "FlashAttention exists because SRAM is small."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_f55c3abf15", "name": "Inference optimizations answer hardware limits", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering Roadmap v2.md", "message": "C3, \"Why hardware is a core strand, not optional\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "FlashAttention, PagedAttention, and quantization each exist as a response to a specific hardware constraint: SRAM is small, KV-cache fragmentation wastes HBM, and decode is memory-bound."}
{"aliases": ["the Goodhart tax"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_0e5f5b24dc", "name": "Goodhart tax measures reward hacking", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering Roadmap v2.md", "message": "C2, \"Critical self-designed experiment\": \"The gap between the two scores **is** the Goodhart tax\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Goodhart tax is the gap between a reward model's score and an independent LLM judge's score on the same PPO output."}
{"aliases": ["Piping (`|`) connects commands — send output of one as input to the next."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_65aecea471", "name": "Shell pipe connects stdout to stdin", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Terminal and Shell.md", "message": "Piping & Redirection", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In a Unix shell, the pipe operator connects commands by passing the standard output of one command as standard input to the next, which is how log filtering chains such as tail piped into grep work."}
{"aliases": ["Survives terminal close?"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_fdab6f3620", "name": "Background process survival by method", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Terminal and Shell.md", "message": "Background Processes & Process Management, survival table", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A command started with & dies when the terminal closes, nohup command & survives the terminal closing but cannot be reattached, and tmux both survives and can be reattached."}
{"aliases": ["ssh -L 8888:localhost:8888 user@gpu-box-ip"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_5610e59218", "name": "SSH -L forwards a remote port locally", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineering - Terminal and Shell.md", "message": "SSH & Remote File Transfer: \"Port forward (access remote Jupyter/TensorBoard locally)\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The ssh -L option forwards a port on the remote host to a port on the local machine over the SSH connection, letting a remote Jupyter or TensorBoard be opened locally."}
{"aliases": ["Semantic tool selection: 3,000 → <300 tokens per call"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_658f72dac4", "name": "Semantic tool selection shrinks tool prompts", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — July 13, 2026.md", "message": "By the Numbers; Theme 1, \"Semantic tool selection\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — 2026-07-25.md", "message": "\"29 tools in the demo travel agent → 3 tools after semantic filtering.\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Embedding the user query, retrieving the top-K matching tool descriptions from a vector store, and sending only those tools to the model cuts per-call token usage from thousands of tokens to under 300."}
{"aliases": ["Graph RAG beats text RAG for any question involving counts, aggregations, or multi-hop relationships.", "The graph computes the answer; the LLM only formats it."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_466d54cbb1", "name": "Graph RAG computes aggregation answers", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — July 13, 2026.md", "message": "Theme 1, item 2 \"Graph RAG\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — 2026-07-25.md", "message": "\"On agent reliability\" and \"How to Apply Right Now\" item 5", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For queries involving counts, aggregations, or multi-hop relationships, querying a knowledge graph with Cypher or SPARQL returns the computed answer and leaves the LLM only to format it, whereas text and vector RAG cannot compute such answers."}
{"aliases": ["the gap compounds because unreviewed AI code grounds tomorrow's AI suggestions"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_be051bce27", "name": "Unreviewed AI code compounds review debt", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — July 13, 2026.md", "message": "Theme 4, \"Review Debt Is Real and Nobody's Measuring It\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "AI produces code faster than humans can review it, and the review gap compounds because unreviewed AI code becomes the context that grounds the next round of AI suggestions."}
{"aliases": ["Scaffolding beats model size, every time."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_2ff801f6a7", "name": "State-machine scaffolding beats model size", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — July 20, 2026.md", "message": "The Big Picture; Theme 1 \"Scaffolding > Model Size\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Moving reasoning, state transitions, and decision logic out of the model into a deterministic state machine, and feeding the model only what to say, lowers latency and cost relative to letting the LLM drive."}
{"aliases": ["\"Correctness\" on a 1-5 scale is low signal."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_28d224fbb5", "name": "Binary evaluators are higher signal", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — July 20, 2026.md", "message": "Theme 2, \"High-Signal Evals\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Binary yes/no checks about specific quality criteria are higher-signal evaluators than 1-5 correctness or helpfulness ratings."}
{"aliases": ["You need checkpoints to ask *what if*."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_bd4e0fc63b", "name": "Checkpoint replay enables cohort comparison", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — July 20, 2026.md", "message": "Theme 3, \"Production Observability for Agents\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Saving the full agent state at each step lets a cohort of production runs be replayed from a checkpoint under a different model or prompt and diffed, which single-replay anecdotes cannot establish."}
{"aliases": ["Decouple or die.", "flow, state, durability, retries"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_d5860b0a89", "name": "Execution and context layers must decouple", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — 2026-07-21.md", "message": "The Big Picture, \"Decouple or die\"; Theme 2", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Separating the execution layer (flow, state, durability, retries) from the context layer (models, prompts, tools, memory) lets either be swapped without rewriting the other, whereas tangling prompt logic with retry logic prevents swapping either."}
{"aliases": ["Multi-agent is overrated. Single-agent with delegation wins.", "It failed because context got lost at every handoff."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_1609bbf62a", "name": "Distributed reasoning across agents loses coherence", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — 2026-07-21.md", "message": "The Big Picture, \"Multi-agent is overrated\"; Must Watch talk 2", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — 2026-07-25.md", "message": "\"Start with one agent.\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Distributing reasoning across several agents loses context at each handoff and yields outputs that are factually correct but incoherent, while consolidating judgment into one reasoning agent that delegates investigations to sub-agents preserves coherence."}
{"aliases": ["Multi-agent validation catches failures the primary agent hides.", "A second agent with a rubric in a separate call is cheap insurance."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_d6610bc3eb", "name": "A second agent verifier catches hidden errors", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — July 13, 2026.md", "message": "Theme 1, item 3 \"Multi-agent validation\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — 2026-07-25.md", "message": "\"On agent reliability\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Running a second agent with a rubric in a separate call to check the primary agent's output catches failures the primary agent hides, at low cost."}
{"aliases": ["let the model manage its own memory structure"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_2793539215", "name": "Agents structure their own memory", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — 2026-07-21.md", "message": "Theme 4, \"Build agents that own their memory\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — 2026-07-25.md", "message": "\"On memory and context\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Giving an agent a filesystem or database and letting it structure its own memory outperforms prescribing a memory schema, because higher-capacity models choose what to abstract and save for later sessions."}
{"aliases": ["an offline process that reviews session traces and consolidates/corrects memories"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_bd19e31b93", "name": "Offline dreaming pass corrects memories", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — 2026-07-21.md", "message": "Theme 4: the \"dreaming\" concept", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/AI Engineer Weekly — 2026-07-25.md", "message": "\"Use an offline \"dreaming\" pass to correct incorrect memories.\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An offline review pass that reads session traces and the agent's memory can consolidate and correct memories that the model wrote incorrectly during a run."}
{"aliases": ["Your Code → HTTP Request (with API key) → API Server → HTTP Response (JSON)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_f98885b8f7", "name": "AI API call anatomy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/API Key Security.md", "message": "The Universal Pattern", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Every AI API call consists of an endpoint URL, an API key for authentication, a request body describing what is wanted, and a response body carrying the result."}
{"aliases": ["**Never** put API keys in code. Use environment variables."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_55610a077b", "name": "API keys belong in environment variables", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/API Key Security.md", "message": "Security Rules", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "API keys must never be embedded in code; they should be supplied through environment variables or a gitignored .env file so the credential is not committed."}
{"aliases": ["wrong or missing API key → 401", "too many requests per minute → 429"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_0d1506c1bb", "name": "API error status codes", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/API Key Security.md", "message": "Common Errors", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An AI API returns 401 for a wrong or missing API key, 429 when a rate limit is exceeded, and 400 when the request exceeds the model's context window."}
{"aliases": ["a placeholder variable in the function definition"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_bd3bf6e27e", "name": "Parameter is a definition-site variable", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Arguments and parameters.md", "message": "Definitions; Visual Distinction", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A parameter is the variable listed in a function definition that receives the value passed in at the call site."}
{"aliases": ["the concrete value supplied when calling the function"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_11c6d5e8c5", "name": "Argument is a call-site value", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Arguments and parameters.md", "message": "Definitions; Visual Distinction", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An argument is the actual value supplied to a function at the call site."}
{"aliases": ["closures capture the parameters of outer functions as part of their backpack"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_e016df22b1", "name": "Closures capture enclosing parameters", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Arguments and parameters.md", "message": "Key Context, \"Closures\"; Related", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A closure captures the parameters of its enclosing function as part of its captured environment."}
{"aliases": ["gives tests a consistent three-phase structure so failures are sharp and the test itself reads like a specification"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_a5d259bcb3", "name": "Arrange-Act-Assert test structure", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA).md", "message": "The Three Phases", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Arrange-Act-Assert pattern gives a test three phases: arrange the object and its collaborators into a known state, act with a single targeted operation on the object, and assert claims about the observable outcome."}
{"aliases": ["the test runner stops at the first failed assertion"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_8202bf9a64", "name": "Test runners stop at first failed assertion", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA).md", "message": "What AAA Gives You", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A test runner stops at the first failed assertion within a test."}
{"aliases": ["bundling many independent checks into one test destroys diagnostic precision"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_a68687f3ec", "name": "Bundled checks destroy diagnostic precision", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA).md", "message": "What AAA Gives You, \"Single responsibility per test\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Bundling many independent checks into a single test destroys diagnostic precision, because the run stops at the first failure and later failures are never observed."}
{"aliases": ["**Setup/teardown belong to Arrange**, not a fourth A."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_6279b02f68", "name": "Setup and teardown belong to Arrange", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA).md", "message": "Practical Nuances", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In Arrange-Act-Assert, setup and teardown belong to the Arrange phase rather than to a fourth phase, because unit tests touch only the object under test and need no external cleanup."}
{"aliases": ["decomposing it into basic operations and applying local derivative rules through the chain rule"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_bb6cd910a5", "name": "Automatic differentiation is exact by chain rule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Automatic differentiation.md", "message": "Intro; Core idea", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Automatic differentiation computes exact derivatives of a program by decomposing it into basic operations, attaching exact local derivative rules to each operation, and chaining those rules through the program graph."}
{"aliases": ["Unlike symbolic differentiation, automatic differentiation works on the executed program.", "Unlike finite differences, it does not approximate the derivative."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_c72a94571b", "name": "AD differs from symbolic and numerical differentiation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Automatic differentiation.md", "message": "Core idea; Compared with other methods", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Automatic differentiation works on the executed program, unlike symbolic differentiation which manipulates algebraic expressions, and unlike finite differences it does not approximate, so it stays exact to machine precision."}
{"aliases": ["Sweep left-to-right.", "This is useful when there are few inputs and many outputs."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_12b96f2ac7", "name": "Forward mode propagates sensitivities with values", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Automatic differentiation.md", "message": "Two modes, Forward mode", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Forward-mode automatic differentiation sweeps the program left to right, propagating each intermediate value's sensitivity forward alongside the value, and is therefore efficient when a function has few inputs and many outputs."}
{"aliases": ["Seeding the input tangent with a direction vector"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_64dafd13dc", "name": "Forward-mode tangent seed yields a JVP", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Automatic differentiation.md", "message": "Two modes, Forward mode", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Seeding the input tangent of forward-mode automatic differentiation with a direction vector r yields the Jacobian-vector product J_f(x)r, which is the same quantity as the directional derivative."}
{"aliases": ["First run a forward pass to compute and store intermediates.", "This is useful when there are many inputs and one scalar output"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_4970c5e521", "name": "Reverse mode accumulates gradients backward", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Automatic differentiation.md", "message": "Two modes, Reverse mode; Why it matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Reverse-mode automatic differentiation runs a forward pass that stores intermediates, then a backward pass right to left that accumulates partial derivatives, and is efficient when there are many inputs and one scalar output, which is why it underlies backpropagation."}
{"aliases": ["reverse-mode form of automatic differentiation specialized to layered neural networks"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_56948c76a0", "name": "Backpropagation as reverse-mode chain rule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Backpropagation.md", "message": "Backpropagation is the standard way to compute gradients in layered neural networks by applying the chain rule efficiently from the output back to earlier layers.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Backpropagation computes gradients in layered neural networks by applying the chain rule from the output back to earlier layers, and is the reverse-mode form of automatic differentiation specialized to layered networks."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_b8b906a3c2", "name": "Forward pass stores activations and loss", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Backpropagation.md", "message": "The two passes — Forward pass", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The forward pass of a neural network feeds the input through one layer at a time, computes each layer's pre-activation and activation, stores the intermediate values needed later, and computes the final loss."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_cdbbb57075", "name": "Backward pass combines upstream and local gradients", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Backpropagation.md", "message": "Backward pass — combine the upstream gradient with the layer's local gradient", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The backward pass starts from the loss gradient and moves backward through the network, and at each layer combines the upstream gradient with the layer's local gradient to produce that layer's parameter gradient and the gradient sent to the previous layer."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_37d740e08a", "name": "Backprop reuses accumulated downstream gradients", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Backpropagation.md", "message": "Why it is useful", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Backpropagation avoids expanding the full chain rule separately for every parameter by reusing the gradient already accumulated from later layers, turning the whole network into a sequence of local computations."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_0fefe9408d", "name": "Base rate fallacy defined", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Base Rate Fallacy.md", "message": "Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The base rate fallacy is the mistake of focusing on new evidence such as a test result or description while ignoring how common or rare the event actually is."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_3a5c28c79a", "name": "Accurate test on a rare disease yields a low posterior", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Base Rate Fallacy.md", "message": "Example — A 99% accurate disease test on a 1/1000 prevalence disease", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes' Theorem.md", "message": "Disease Test Example", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For a disease with 1-in-1000 prevalence, a test with 99% sensitivity and a 1% false-positive rate gives P(disease | positive) of about 9%, not 99%."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_5c2bfc8cfc", "name": "Concrete-population method for base-rate problems", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Base Rate Fallacy.md", "message": "The 3-Step Method (concrete population)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A base-rate problem can be computed on a round population by splitting it into target and non-target groups at the prior rate, multiplying each group size by its evidence rate, and dividing the evidence found in the target group by the total evidence found."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_976e80da82", "name": "Frequentist A/B test and the p-value", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayesian A-B Testing.md", "message": "The Frequentist Procedure It Replaces", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Frequentist A/B testing assumes a null hypothesis of zero difference between variants, measures the observed gap, defines the p-value as the probability of a gap at least that large by chance alone, and ships the winner when p < 0.05."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_0ff06e59e0", "name": "Beta prior updated by binomial counts", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayesian A-B Testing.md", "message": "Setup — Start both with a Beta(1,1) prior", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Starting a variant's conversion rate at a Beta(1,1) prior, observing 50 conversions in 1000 views yields a Beta(51,951) posterior with mean about 0.051, and observing 65 in 1000 yields Beta(66,936) with mean about 0.066."}
{"aliases": ["P(B > A) = mean(samples_B > samples_A)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_8e3604a324", "name": "Monte Carlo estimate of P(B beats A)", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayesian A-B Testing.md", "message": "The Decision: P(B > A)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The probability that variant B's true conversion rate exceeds A's is estimated by drawing samples from each variant's Beta posterior and taking the fraction of sampled pairs where B > A."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_8aa531f357", "name": "Bayesian A/B ship thresholds", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayesian A-B Testing.md", "message": "Ship B if P(B>A) > 0.95; ship A if < 0.05; otherwise keep collecting.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Bayesian A/B test ships variant B when P(B > A) > 0.95, ships variant A when P(B > A) < 0.05, and otherwise keeps collecting data."}
{"aliases": ["no peeking problem", "the posterior doesn't depend on when you looked"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_b0867ac520", "name": "Posterior does not depend on when you looked", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayesian A-B Testing.md", "message": "Why Bayesian Beats Frequentist — Early stopping: inflates false positives / safe at any point", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Bayesian posterior does not depend on when the data was inspected, so checking results at any point and stopping early is safe, whereas early stopping in frequentist testing inflates false positives."}
{"aliases": ["P(H \\mid D) \\propto P(D \\mid H) P(H)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_3dfa6996a3", "name": "Bayes' theorem formula", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes' Theorem.md", "message": "Core Formula", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes rule.md", "message": "Core statement", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Bayes' theorem gives the posterior probability of a hypothesis given evidence as the likelihood times the prior divided by the evidence: P(H|E) = P(E|H)·P(H)/P(E)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_95a2ceff83", "name": "Prior, likelihood, and posterior meanings", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes rule.md", "message": "What the parts mean", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes' Theorem.md", "message": "Components", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In Bayes' rule the prior is what was believed before seeing the data, the likelihood is how probable the data are under the hypothesis, and the posterior is what is believed after seeing the data."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_1a7fa49808", "name": "Evidence as total probability", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes' Theorem.md", "message": "Components — Evidence P(E) (Law of Total Probability)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The evidence term P(E) in Bayes' theorem is the total probability of the evidence under all possibilities: P(E) = P(E|H)·P(H) + P(E|¬H)·P(¬H)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_8630af0c26", "name": "Conditional probability definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes' Theorem.md", "message": "Derivation (from Conditional Probability)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Conditional probability is defined as P(A|B) = P(A∩B)/P(B), and symmetrically P(B|A) = P(A∩B)/P(A)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_4080ab53bf", "name": "Two conditional-probability expressions share one joint probability", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes' Theorem.md", "message": "Both expressions share the same numerator — P(A∩B). Set them equal and isolate P(A|B)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because P(A|B)·P(B) and P(B|A)·P(A) are both expressions for the same joint probability P(A∩B), they are equal: P(A|B)·P(B) = P(B|A)·P(A)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_60d06b9907", "name": "Bayes' theorem needs no independence assumption", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes' Theorem.md", "message": "No independence assumption is needed", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Bayes' theorem requires no independence assumption, because its derivation only equates two expressions for the same joint probability."}
{"aliases": ["The prior dominates when a condition is rare — even accurate tests produce mostly false positives."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_1a66e305f3", "name": "Rare conditions make the prior dominate", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes' Theorem.md", "message": "Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Base Rate Fallacy.md", "message": "Worked Example: Library Cards — the prior (few children) dominates the evidence", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When a condition is rare, the prior dominates the posterior and even an accurate test produces mostly false positives."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_8f6abfc7e5", "name": "Bernoulli distribution definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bernoulli Distribution.md", "message": "Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Bernoulli distribution models one trial with exactly two outcomes, with P(X = k) = p^k (1−p)^(1−k) for k in {0, 1}, where p is the probability of success (X = 1)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_2a25a24089", "name": "Bernoulli mean and variance", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bernoulli Distribution.md", "message": "Key statistics", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Bernoulli random variable with success probability p has mean E[X] = p and variance Var(X) = p(1−p)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_9baf51bdea", "name": "Binary classification is Bernoulli", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bernoulli Distribution.md", "message": "Why it matters in ML — Binary classification is Bernoulli", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Binary classification is a Bernoulli problem: the model outputs a single probability p for the positive class, and the negative class probability is 1 − p."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_205874ea66", "name": "Bernoulli as building block of Binomial and multi-label", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bernoulli Distribution.md", "message": "The Bernoulli distribution also forms the building block for", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Bernoulli distribution is the building block of the Binomial distribution, which is n independent Bernoulli trials, and of multi-label classification, which is multiple independent Bernoulli outputs."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_ba0b88ce4c", "name": "Bigram model conditions on one token", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bigram Language Model.md", "message": "Overview — Context length = 1", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A bigram language model predicts the next character or word using only the immediately preceding token, so its context length is one."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_da26f8bbac", "name": "Bigram counts normalized into row probabilities", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bigram Language Model.md", "message": "Key Idea", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A count-based bigram model counts every consecutive token pair into a vocab_size × vocab_size matrix and normalizes each row to obtain P(next | current)."}
{"aliases": ["No memory beyond the last character", "A bigram model has no memory beyond the last character"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_1ef1b40665", "name": "Bigram model has no long-range memory", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bigram Language Model.md", "message": "Limitations", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A bigram model has no memory beyond the last token, so it cannot capture long-range dependencies and generates mostly unpronounceable output."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_d751df4107", "name": "Single linear layer reproduces bigram counts", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bigram Language Model.md", "message": "Relation to Neural Nets — One-hot × W = W[row, :]", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A single linear layer with no bias and no activation, fed one-hot inputs, learns exactly the same token-pair table that the counting approach computes directly, because multiplying a one-hot vector by W selects a row of W."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_5219540094", "name": "BiLSTM text classifier architecture", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/BiLSTM Text Classifier.md", "message": "Architecture", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A BiLSTM text classifier feeds word embeddings through a bidirectional LSTM, concatenates the last hidden states, applies dropout, and passes the result to a fully connected layer that outputs class logits."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_c1641aa912", "name": "Bidirectional LSTM reads in both directions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/BiLSTM Text Classifier.md", "message": "Bidirectional = Two LSTMs", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A bidirectional LSTM is two LSTMs, one reading left-to-right and one reading right-to-left, whose concatenated final states capture context from both sides of a position."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_e845c2839b", "name": "Bidirectional output doubles the fully connected input width", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/BiLSTM Text Classifier.md", "message": "nn.Linear(hidden_dim * 2, num_classes)  # ×2 for bidirectional", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because the forward and backward final hidden states are concatenated, a BiLSTM classifier's fully connected layer takes twice the hidden dimension as its input width."}
{"aliases": ["Bits and nats are the same information quantity in different log bases"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_42e9186683", "name": "Bits, nats, and hartleys as log bases", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bits vs Nats.md", "message": "The three units", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Bits, nats, and hartleys measure the same information quantity in different logarithm bases — log base 2, natural log, and log base 10 respectively — and machine-learning cross-entropy losses are reported in nats by default."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_c37e764554", "name": "Nat–bit conversion factor", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bits vs Nats.md", "message": "Conversion", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "One nat equals 1/ln 2 bits, about 1.4427 bits, and one bit equals about 0.6931 nats."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_3179407f5e", "name": "Log base only rescales information quantities", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bits vs Nats.md", "message": "The base is a constant factor on every surprise term", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Changing the logarithm base multiplies every surprise term by a constant, so it rescales entropies and divergences without changing their order, their zero points, or identities such as KL = CE − H."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_77838c5c8a", "name": "Perplexity exponent must match the entropy unit", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bits vs Nats.md", "message": "The detector — cross-entropy in bits → Perplexity = 2^H; in nats → PPL = e^H", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Perplexity is 2^H when cross-entropy H is measured in bits and e^H when H is measured in nats, so the unit must be read before choosing the exponent."}
{"aliases": ["Black-box/white-box = visibility; unit/integration/E2E = scope"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_9843e14110", "name": "Visibility and scope are separate testing axes", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Black-box vs White-box Testing.md", "message": "Overview / The Two Axes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Black-box versus white-box describes test visibility, whether the test can see inside the implementation, while unit, integration, and end-to-end describes test scope, how much of the system is exercised; the two are separate axes that can be combined."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_48fac4df3b", "name": "Process-I/O black-box integration tests", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Black-box vs White-box Testing.md", "message": "The Process-I/O Pattern (cstack Part 4)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A process-I/O test is a black-box integration test: it spawns the real binary, pipes commands to its stdin, and asserts on exact stdout lines, so internals can be refactored freely as long as the CLI contract holds."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_dd9129c965", "name": "Testing pyramid", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Black-box vs White-box Testing.md", "message": "The Testing Pyramid", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A healthy test suite keeps many fast, isolated unit tests plus a small set of integration tests, because an integration-only suite is slow, runs less often, and its failures are ambiguous."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_888f119ead", "name": "One-in-1000 test accounting on a population of 1000", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bayes' Theorem.md", "message": "Intuitive: In a group of 1000 people, 1 is sick ... ~10 healthy people false-positive", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In a population of 1000 people with 1-in-1000 disease prevalence and a test that is 99% sensitive with a 1% false-positive rate, about 1 true positive and about 10 false positives yield 11 positive results, so about 1 in 11 positives is truly sick."}
{"aliases": ["one \"broken window\" (messy code, ignored bug, hacky workaround) signals that nobody cares, which invites more neglect and accelerates decay"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_a1adbcd019", "name": "Broken window signals neglect", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Broken Window Theory.md", "message": "# Broken Window Theory", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In a codebase, one visible \"broken window\" such as messy code, an ignored bug, or a hacky workaround signals that nobody cares, which invites more neglect and accelerates decay."}
{"aliases": ["If you can't fix it now, at least acknowledge it (comment, ticket, note)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_0bb176e28d", "name": "Acknowledge unfixed defects visibly", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Broken Window Theory.md", "message": "## Key rules", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When a defect cannot be fully fixed immediately, a visible acknowledgement such as a comment, ticket, or flag marks it as known and counters the broken-window signal that nobody cares."}
{"aliases": ["a portable intermediate representation between source code and machine code"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_06133d18f1", "name": "Bytecode is a portable IR", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bytecode.md", "message": "# Bytecode", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Bytecode is a portable intermediate representation between source code and machine code that is not tied to any specific CPU architecture."}
{"aliases": ["A [[Virtual Machine]] reads the bytecode and executes it (or [[JIT Compilation|JIT-compiles]] it to machine code)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_da99b036c7", "name": "Virtual machine executes bytecode", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bytecode.md", "message": "## How it's used", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Source code is compiled to bytecode, and a virtual machine then reads that bytecode and either executes it directly or JIT-compiles it to machine code."}
{"aliases": ["same bytecode runs on any device that has the right VM"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_2fe5a86977", "name": "Bytecode portability across VMs", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bytecode.md", "message": "## Why bytecode?", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because bytecode is architecture-independent, the same bytecode runs on any device that has the right virtual machine."}
{"aliases": ["bytecode is closer to machine code than source, so the VM can execute it faster than interpreting raw source"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_d4bb61338f", "name": "Bytecode is faster than raw source", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Bytecode.md", "message": "## Why bytecode?", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Bytecode executes faster than interpreting raw source because bytecode is closer to machine code."}
{"aliases": ["it points in the direction of **steepest ascent**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_159c3713c1", "name": "Gradient points to steepest ascent", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Calculus for ML.md", "message": "### Partial Derivatives & Gradient", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A partial derivative holds all variables constant except one, and the gradient collects every partial derivative into one vector that points in the direction of steepest ascent; minimizing therefore means moving in the opposite, negative-gradient direction."}
{"aliases": ["w = w - lr · dL/dw"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_d75bfba7de", "name": "Gradient descent update rule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Calculus for ML.md", "message": "### Gradient Descent", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Gradient descent updates every weight by w = w - lr · dL/dw, where the learning rate controls step size so that too large a step overshoots and too small a step converges slowly."}
{"aliases": ["All eigenvalues > 0 | Local minimum"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_48ff3953a9", "name": "Hessian eigenvalues classify critical points", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Calculus for ML.md", "message": "### Hessian Matrix", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Hessian is the matrix of second-order partial derivatives, and its eigenvalues classify a critical point: all eigenvalues positive means a local minimum, all negative means a local maximum, and mixed signs mean a saddle point."}
{"aliases": ["its derivative is an m×n **Jacobian** matrix containing every partial derivative of every output w.r.t. every input"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_e307e7c813", "name": "Jacobian for vector-valued functions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Calculus for ML.md", "message": "### Jacobian Matrix", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A function mapping R^n to R^m has an m×n Jacobian matrix of every partial derivative of every output with respect to every input, and backpropagation sends gradients backward through the transpose of that matrix."}
{"aliases": ["**Backpropagation is the chain rule applied repeatedly from output to input.**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_0ad7579ac2", "name": "Backpropagation is repeated chain rule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Calculus for ML.md", "message": "### Chain Rule & Backpropagation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Chain Rule for Neural Networks.md", "message": "Backpropagation IS the chain rule applied layer by layer, from loss backward to each weight.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Backpropagation is the chain rule applied repeatedly from the output back to the input, which is the entire algorithm for training a neural network."}
{"aliases": ["P(X = i) = p_i, \\quad i \\in \\{1, 2, \\ldots, k\\}"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_81173caa9a", "name": "Categorical distribution definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Categorical Distribution.md", "message": "## Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Categorical distribution generalizes the Bernoulli to k mutually exclusive outcomes in one trial, with P(X = i) = p_i, sum of p_i equal to 1, and every p_i greater than or equal to 0."}
{"aliases": ["After the softmax, you have a vector of k probabilities that sum to 1 — that's a Categorical PMF."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_3004dd5411", "name": "Softmax output is a categorical PMF", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Categorical Distribution.md", "message": "## Why it matters in ML", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The output of a multi-class classifier after softmax is a vector of k probabilities summing to 1, which is a Categorical probability mass function."}
{"aliases": ["**Bernoulli** = Categorical(2)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_7a4b08f066", "name": "Bernoulli is Categorical with k equals 2", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Categorical Distribution.md", "message": "## Relationship to other distributions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Bernoulli distribution is the two-outcome special case of the Categorical distribution."}
{"aliases": ["**Multinomial** = n independent Categorical trials (not a Categorical!)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_ed7995b115", "name": "Multinomial is not a Categorical", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Categorical Distribution.md", "message": "## Relationship to other distributions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Multinomial distribution is n independent Categorical trials, so it is not itself a Categorical distribution."}
{"aliases": ["Sampling from a Categorical is inverse transform sampling"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_39f6649a1a", "name": "Inverse transform sampling from a Categorical", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Categorical Distribution.md", "message": "## Implementation insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Sampling from a Categorical distribution uses inverse transform sampling: build cumulative probabilities, draw u from Uniform(0,1), and return the index of the first cumulative value greater than or equal to u."}
{"aliases": ["dy/dx = f'(g(x)) · g'(x)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_96cf708f7d", "name": "Chain rule as product of local derivatives", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Chain Rule Decomposition.md", "message": "# Chain Rule Decomposition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The chain rule decomposes the derivative of a composition y = f(g(x)) into a product of simple local derivatives, dy/dx = f'(g(x)) · g'(x)."}
{"aliases": ["each node in the computation only needs to know two things"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_5ee90a67ea", "name": "Autograd nodes need only local derivative and upstream gradient", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Chain Rule Decomposition.md", "message": "# Chain Rule Decomposition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Each node in a computation graph needs only its own local derivative and the upstream gradient flowing from the output side, and multiplying those two and passing the result downstream is why autograd engines scale to arbitrary computation graphs without global knowledge of the expression."}
{"aliases": ["∂Loss/∂w1 = ∂Loss/∂a3 × ∂a3/∂a2 × ∂a2/∂a1 × ∂a1/∂w1"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_d48a98c370", "name": "Multi-layer gradients multiply local derivatives", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Chain Rule for Neural Networks.md", "message": "## Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In a multi-layer network with layers a1 → a2 → a3 → loss, the gradient of the loss with respect to a weight in the first layer is the product of the local derivatives along the path, ∂Loss/∂w1 = ∂Loss/∂a3 × ∂a3/∂a2 × ∂a2/∂a1 × ∂a1/∂w1."}
{"aliases": ["Gradients multiply across layers (not add) because intermediate variables are dependent."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_9a88b11310", "name": "Layer gradients multiply because variables are dependent", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Chain Rule for Neural Networks.md", "message": "## Why Multiplication, Not Addition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Gradients through neural-network layers multiply rather than add because the intermediate variables are dependent and chained, with effects compounding multiplicatively; addition would be correct only if the variables were independent."}
{"aliases": ["A language model that predicts the next character given the previous ones."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_90b9c52edd", "name": "Character-level RNN predicts next character", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Character-Level RNN (CharRNN).md", "message": "## What It Is", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A character-level RNN is a language model that predicts the next character given the preceding characters, and after training on a corpus it can generate new text that mimics the corpus style."}
{"aliases": ["Character IDs → Embedding → LSTM → FC → vocab_size logits → softmax → sample"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_b391731fb3", "name": "CharRNN architecture pipeline", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Character-Level RNN (CharRNN).md", "message": "## Architecture", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A CharRNN pipeline maps character IDs to an embedding, feeds the embedding through an LSTM into a fully connected layer that produces vocab_size logits, then applies softmax and samples."}
{"aliases": ["`torch.multinomial` samples from the probability distribution — introduces randomness."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_ee0aff03b5", "name": "Sampling versus greedy text generation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Character-Level RNN (CharRNN).md", "message": "## Generation via Sampling", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Sampling the next character from the softmax distribution with torch.multinomial introduces randomness, whereas greedy decoding that always picks the highest-probability character produces repetitive, boring text."}
{"aliases": ["**Character:** tiny vocab (~50-200 chars), can generate unseen words"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_1e510f1ff9", "name": "Character-level vocabulary trade-off", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Character-Level RNN (CharRNN).md", "message": "## Character vs Word Level", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Character-level models use a tiny vocabulary of roughly 50 to 200 characters and can generate unseen words, at the cost of sequences about six times longer than word-level sequences."}
{"aliases": ["unsigned integer underflow"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_eee0fd1d5c", "name": "Unsigned subtraction underflows and wraps", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Integer Mechanics (Underflow & Type Promotion).md", "message": "## Trap 1 — Unsigned Underflow (Not Negatives)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An unsigned long cannot hold a negative sign and represents only 0 to 2^64−1 on 64-bit machines, so when available_kb exceeds total_kb the expression total_kb - available_kb underflows and wraps to a massive positive value near the maximum instead of becoming negative."}
{"aliases": ["the corrupt state must be caught *before* the subtraction or division"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_9c81ca9b1a", "name": "Guard unsigned inputs before arithmetic", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Integer Mechanics (Underflow & Type Promotion).md", "message": "## Trap 1 — Unsigned Underflow (Not Negatives)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The corrupt state of available_kb > total_kb must be checked before the subtraction or division, so the program prints an error to stderr and aborts with return code 1 instead of wrapping and reporting a garbage percentage."}
{"aliases": ["the *usual arithmetic conversions* kick in and promote the integer to `double` **before** the operation"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_183d4bd75b", "name": "Usual arithmetic conversions promote to double", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Integer Mechanics (Underflow & Type Promotion).md", "message": "### Why `100.0` (not `100`)?", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When C mixes an unsigned long with a double, the usual arithmetic conversions promote the integer to double before the operation, so 100.0 * used_kb yields a double and the following division preserves precision."}
{"aliases": ["C integer division **truncates the fractional part toward zero**."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_410ba29728", "name": "Integer division truncates the fraction to zero", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Integer Mechanics (Underflow & Type Promotion).md", "message": "### The subtle bug in `(used_kb / memory.total_kb) * 100`", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "C integer division truncates the fractional part toward zero, so in (used_kb / memory.total_kb) * 100 both operands are integers and the quotient becomes 0 for any usage under 100%, making the final result 0.0%."}
{"aliases": ["`100.0 * used_kb / memory.total_kb` is evaluated left-to-right"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_08e5617a65", "name": "Leading float literal preserves percentage precision", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Integer Mechanics (Underflow & Type Promotion).md", "message": "### The subtle bug in `(used_kb / memory.total_kb) * 100`", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Writing 100.0 * used_kb / memory.total_kb evaluates left to right: 100.0 * used_kb becomes a large double, and dividing that double by total_kb yields the correct percentage rather than zero."}
{"aliases": ["**closure** is the combination of a function bundled together with references to its surrounding state"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_58396ad07f", "name": "Closure bundles function with outer state", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Closures.md", "message": "# Closures", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A closure is a function bundled together with references to its surrounding state, allowing an inner function to access variables from an outer function even after that outer function has finished executing."}
{"aliases": ["it is only a closure if it **actually grabs and holds onto a variable from outside its own body**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_ae45c99e9b", "name": "Closure requires actual capture", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Closures.md", "message": "## Technical Precision", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A function is a closure only if it actually grabs and holds onto a variable from outside its own body; a function that uses only its own parameters and local variables is just a function, not a closure."}
{"aliases": ["Closures depend on lexical scoping."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_5bbb6a1be7", "name": "Closures require lexical scope and first-class functions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Closures.md", "message": "## Key Prerequisites", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Closures depend on lexical or block scoping, first-class functions that can be assigned, passed, and returned, and nested functions defined inside other functions."}
{"aliases": ["**Data hiding / private state** — The `count` variable in the example above is private"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_09a869c012", "name": "Closures provide private state", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Closures.md", "message": "## Why Closures Matter", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Closures enable data hiding and private state because a captured variable such as a counter is not directly modifiable from outside the closure."}
{"aliases": ["Stack | Fast automatic scratchpad for function locals"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_9701ba3d81", "name": "Stack frame lifetime and size", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Memory Regions (Stack vs Heap vs Swap).md", "message": "## The Stack (and the Caller's Stack)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The stack is a fast scratchpad of function-local variables managed automatically by the compiler and CPU: each call pushes a frame that is wiped when the function returns, and its size is fixed at compile time to typically only a few megabytes."}
{"aliases": ["heap memory stays allocated until you explicitly `free()` it"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_19c2589613", "name": "Heap memory persists until freed", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Memory Regions (Stack vs Heap vs Swap).md", "message": "## Heap Memory", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The heap is dynamic storage obtained with malloc and realloc that stays allocated until the program explicitly calls free, can grow on demand, and leaks when the program forgets to free it."}
{"aliases": ["**Swap** is an overflow emergency room on your hard drive"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_e950f87bbd", "name": "Swap is disk overflow for full RAM", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Memory Regions (Stack vs Heap vs Swap).md", "message": "## Swap", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Swap is overflow space on disk that the Linux kernel uses when real RAM runs low, moving cold inactive pages out of RAM so the system does not crash, at the cost of disk access being exponentially slower than RAM."}
{"aliases": ["**`buf` (the pointer variable) lives on the stack**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_a9ded943c0", "name": "Malloc pointer lives on stack, data on heap", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Memory Regions (Stack vs Heap vs Swap).md", "message": "### Mental model: `char *buf = malloc(4096);`", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For char *buf = malloc(4096), the pointer variable buf is an 8-byte slot on the stack holding the returned address, while the 4096-byte data block itself lives on the heap."}
{"aliases": ["a stack array **cannot leak**, because stack memory disappears on exit"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:35Z", "id": "n_be2502c4e9", "name": "Stack arrays dangle, heap blocks leak", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Memory Regions (Stack vs Heap vs Swap).md", "message": "## Dangling Pointers vs Memory Leaks (the distinction)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Returning the address of a stack array is a dangling-pointer bug because the frame is destroyed on return and later reads are undefined behavior, whereas a stack array cannot leak at all; memory leaks only happen on the heap when free is never called."}
{"aliases": ["Conv → ReLU → MaxPool", "flattened and fed into a small fully-connected classifier"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_99fe346ead", "name": "Standard CNN architecture pattern", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/CNN Architecture Pattern.md", "message": "The standard CNN pattern: **Conv → ReLU → MaxPool**, repeated 2–4 times, then flattened and fed into a small fully-connected classifier.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The standard CNN architecture pattern repeats Conv then ReLU then MaxPool 2-4 times, then flattens the output and feeds it into a small fully-connected classifier."}
