{"aliases": ["Source code is translated to machine code ahead of time", "The resulting binary executable is self-contained"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_334923338b", "name": "Compiled language definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Compiled vs Interpreted.md", "message": "Compiled languages (e.g., Go, Rust, C++)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A compiled language translates source code to machine code ahead of time, producing a self-contained binary that runs directly on the CPU with no external software needed."}
{"aliases": ["Requires a permanent piece of software on the user's computer"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_968dbabf31", "name": "Interpreted language definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Compiled vs Interpreted.md", "message": "Interpreted languages (e.g., Python, JavaScript)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An interpreted language has its source code read and executed at runtime by an interpreter, which requires a permanent interpreter installed on the user's computer."}
{"aliases": ["Most modern languages use a hybrid approach"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_7e769fe096", "name": "Most modern languages use hybrid execution", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Compiled vs Interpreted.md", "message": "The middle ground", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Most modern languages use a hybrid execution approach: Java is compiled to bytecode run on the JVM with JIT compilation, Python is compiled to .pyc bytecode then interpreted by the Python VM, and JavaScript is interpreted or JIT-compiled by the browser engine."}
{"aliases": ["instantaneous for the human iterating at the keyboard", "the output is a lightning-fast binary for end users"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_da063b9f8f", "name": "Interpreters favour iteration, compilers favour shipping", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Compiled vs Interpreted.md", "message": "Dev vs Production workflow", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In development an interpreter runs each typed line instantly, which is slow for the computer but immediate for the human iterating, whereas for production a compiler takes a moment to build but yields a fast binary for end users."}
{"aliases": ["A compiler is a pipeline that translates source code"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_687de468f8", "name": "Compiler definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Compiler.md", "message": "A **compiler** is a pipeline that translates source code (C++, Rust, Go) into machine code that a CPU can execute directly.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A compiler is a pipeline that translates source code into machine code that a CPU can execute directly."}
{"aliases": ["source-language-specific", "target-architecture-specific"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_3b2efcfe9d", "name": "Compiler frontend and backend split", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Compiler.md", "message": "Pipeline stages", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A compiler has a source-language-specific frontend that handles lexing, parsing, and semantic analysis and a target-architecture-specific backend that handles optimization and code generation."}
{"aliases": ["a compiler sees the entire program at once before any code runs", "Math simplification", "Dead code elimination", "Register allocation"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_5948b9d37c", "name": "Compilers see the entire program at once", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Compiler.md", "message": "Why compilers can optimize heavily", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A compiler sees the entire program at once before any code runs, which enables optimizations such as math simplification, dead code elimination, and register allocation."}
{"aliases": ["it trades optimization for instant feedback"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_d068e86316", "name": "Interpreter trades optimization for feedback", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Compiler.md", "message": "An interpreter, executing line-by-line, simply doesn't have time for this analysis — it trades optimization for instant feedback.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An interpreter executes code line by line, which leaves it no time for whole-program analysis, so it trades optimization for instant feedback."}
{"aliases": ["Machine code", "Bytecode", "Three-address code"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_81848afe61", "name": "Compiler output forms", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Compiler.md", "message": "Output forms", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A compiler can emit machine code for a specific CPU, portable bytecode that runs on a virtual machine, or three-address code as an internal intermediate representation."}
{"aliases": ["directed acyclic graph (DAG)", "Nodes represent values", "Edges represent data flow"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_fa82971c7c", "name": "Computational graph definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Computational Graphs (Autodiff).md", "message": "A computational graph is a **directed acyclic graph (DAG)**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A computational graph is a directed acyclic graph whose nodes represent values (inputs, intermediate results, and the final output) and whose edges represent data flow, each edge carrying a value forward and a gradient backward."}
