{"aliases": ["Early layers detect low-level features; deeper layers detect higher-level patterns (cell morphology, lesion shapes)."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_805a7487da", "name": "Convolution filter feature hierarchy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Convolution Operation.md", "message": "Each filter learns to detect a different pattern", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Each convolution filter learns to detect a different pattern, with early layers detecting low-level features and deeper layers detecting higher-level patterns such as cell morphology and lesion shapes."}
{"aliases": ["How many different filters to learn", "Size of the sliding window", "Extra border pixels to preserve spatial dimensions"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_b2dbd04934", "name": "Conv2d layer parameters", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Convolution Parameters.md", "message": "The key parameters that define a Conv2d layer", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Conv2d layer is specified by in_channels (the number of input channels, 3 for RGB and 1 for grayscale), out_channels (how many filters to learn), kernel_size (the size of the sliding window), stride (how many pixels to slide each step), and padding (extra border pixels that preserve spatial dimensions)."}
{"aliases": ["output_size = (input_size + 2×1 − 3)/1 + 1"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_e3db26d4d2", "name": "Conv2d output size formula", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Convolution Parameters.md", "message": "With `padding=1` and `kernel_size=3`: output_size = (input_size + 2×1 − 3)/1 + 1 = input_size", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The output size of a Conv2d layer is output_size = (input_size + 2×padding − kernel_size)/stride + 1."}
{"aliases": ["`kernel_size // 2` (\"same\" padding)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_241208c48a", "name": "Same padding equals half the kernel", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Convolution Parameters.md", "message": "padding | Extra border pixels to preserve spatial dimensions | `kernel_size // 2` (\"same\" padding)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "\"Same\" padding, which preserves spatial dimensions, is set to padding = kernel_size // 2."}
{"aliases": ["output_size = (input_size + 2×1 − 3)/1 + 1 = input_size (dimensions preserved)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_e205c40ae4", "name": "Kernel 3 padding 1 preserves size", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Convolution Parameters.md", "message": "With `padding=1` and `kernel_size=3`", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "With kernel_size = 3, padding = 1 and stride = 1, a Conv2d layer's output size equals its input size."}
{"aliases": ["u · v = |u| · |v| · cos(θ)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_c12949b0e6", "name": "Dot product in terms of cosine", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cosine Similarity.md", "message": "## Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The dot product of two vectors satisfies u · v = |u| · |v| · cos(θ), where θ is the angle between them."}
{"aliases": ["For unit vectors u·v = cos(θ)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_fb80142b5e", "name": "Unit-vector dot product is cosine", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cosine Similarity.md", "message": "**Insight:** For unit vectors u·v = cos(θ)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For unit vectors u and v, the dot product u · v equals cos(θ), the cosine of the angle between them."}
{"aliases": ["Measures alignment −1 to +1. Orthogonality is special case.", "Confusing general meaning (continuous alignment) with special case (orthogonality)."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_6ec076acd6", "name": "Cosine similarity scale and orthogonality", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cosine Similarity.md", "message": "## Scale", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cosine Similarity.md", "message": "## Common Mistake", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Cosine similarity ranges from −1 (180°, opposite) through 0 (90°, orthogonal) to +1 (0°, same direction); orthogonality is the zero-value special case of a continuous alignment measure, not the general meaning of the measure."}
{"aliases": ["Each row `i` of `N` contains counts of what character followed character `i`. Dividing by the row sum gives P(j | i)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_7d38e49d90", "name": "Row normalization yields conditional probabilities", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Counts-to-Probabilities (keepdim trap).md", "message": "## Overview", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Counts-to-Probabilities (keepdim trap).md", "message": "## The Operation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Dividing each row of a character-count matrix by that row's total converts raw counts into P(j | i), the probability that character j follows the current character i, so each row sums to 1."}
{"aliases": ["This **normalizes columns instead of rows**", "Silent bug, no error raised for square matrices"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_b67fed0513", "name": "Missing keepdim normalizes columns", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Counts-to-Probabilities (keepdim trap).md", "message": "## The keepdim=True Trap", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Without keepdim=True, N.sum(1) returns shape (27,) which PyTorch broadcasts as (1, 27), so the division normalizes columns instead of rows; no error is raised for square matrices because (27, 27) / (1, 27) is a valid operation."}
{"aliases": ["**With `keepdim=True`:** shape is `(27, 1)` (column vector), copied horizontally across columns."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_b86ef17355", "name": "Row normalization requires keepdim=True", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Counts-to-Probabilities (keepdim trap).md", "message": "## The keepdim=True Trap", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Correct row normalization in PyTorch requires keepdim=True, which makes the row sums have shape (27, 1) so they broadcast horizontally and each row is divided by its own sum."}
{"aliases": ["Loss calculations downstream will be silently wrong."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_ad0945a33a", "name": "Wrong-axis normalization corrupts downstream loss", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Counts-to-Probabilities (keepdim trap).md", "message": "## Why It Matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A count matrix normalized along the wrong axis does not carry valid conditional probability distributions, so downstream loss calculations are silently wrong even though no error is raised."}
{"aliases": ["Covariance measures whether two variables tend to increase and decrease together."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_f28badc0c5", "name": "Covariance sign meaning", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Covariance and correlation.md", "message": "## Covariance", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Covariance measures whether two random variables tend to move together: positive covariance means they tend to move in the same direction, negative covariance means opposite directions, and near-zero covariance means little linear relationship."}
{"aliases": ["Correlation is a scaled version of covariance.", "It is normalized so the result always lies between −1 and 1."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_d66d667a99", "name": "Correlation is scaled covariance", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Covariance and correlation.md", "message": "## Correlation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Correlation is a normalized (scaled) version of covariance whose value always lies between −1 and 1, which makes relationships easier to compare across different units."}
{"aliases": ["The divisor is one less than the number of rows you summed"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_74bcb75dbb", "name": "np.cov uses n−1 normalization", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Covariance and correlation.md", "message": "## In NumPy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "np.cov computes the sample covariance with 1/(n−1) normalization because centering has already consumed one degree of freedom; dividing by n instead yields a plausible-looking but wrong covariance."}
{"aliases": ["The **default is `rowvar=True`**: each *row* is a variable.", "`np.cov(Xc, rowvar=False)` — each **column** is a variable"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_52e83e2796", "name": "np.cov rowvar default trap", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Covariance and correlation.md", "message": "## In NumPy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "np.cov defaults to rowvar=True, so each row is treated as a variable and a points-as-rows matrix silently yields an n×n object of point-pairings; np.cov(Xc, rowvar=False) instead makes each column a variable and returns the d×d matrix the PCA recipe assumes."}
{"aliases": ["The result is square and symmetric; a single variable comes back as a 0-d scalar, not a `1×1` matrix."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_236ba9f4ee", "name": "Covariance matrix is square symmetric", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Covariance and correlation.md", "message": "## In NumPy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A covariance matrix is square and symmetric, and np.cov on a single variable returns a 0-d scalar rather than a 1×1 matrix."}
{"aliases": ["Plausibility values are real numbers.", "The algebra of plausibility must be consistent"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_b50d7a6d47", "name": "Cox-Jaynes premises", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cox-Jaynes view.md", "message": "## Core claim", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Cox-Jaynes argument requires degrees of plausibility to be represented by real numbers that update in agreement with common sense and whose algebra is consistent, so that the same information pattern always produces the same numbers."}
{"aliases": ["**Non-contradiction**: if two routes lead to the same conclusion, they must give the same plausibility."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_9bb0771142", "name": "Consistency as non-contradiction, honesty, reproducibility", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cox-Jaynes view.md", "message": "## What consistency means here", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In the Cox-Jaynes view, consistency of plausible reasoning means non-contradiction (two routes to the same conclusion give the same plausibility), honesty (all available data are used), and reproducibility (the same state of knowledge produces the same plausibility assignment)."}
{"aliases": ["the rules that emerge are the standard probability rules, especially the sum rule and product rule, up to a monotone re-labeling of the scale"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_96e7532f7d", "name": "Cox-Jaynes derives the probability rules", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cox-Jaynes view.md", "message": "## Core claim", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cox-Jaynes view.md", "message": "## Why it matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Under the Cox-Jaynes consistency requirements, the algebra of plausibility is forced to be the standard probability rules, especially the sum rule and the product rule, up to a monotone re-labeling of the scale."}
{"aliases": ["A monotone re-labeling is allowed, so you can work in another scale such as log-odds without changing the underlying logic."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_4bfb8ae99d", "name": "Monotone relabeling preserves probability logic", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cox-Jaynes view.md", "message": "## Why it matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because the Cox-Jaynes derivation allows a monotone re-labeling of the scale, probability logic can be carried out in another scale such as log-odds without changing the underlying logic."}
{"aliases": ["placing `&` before an existing variable yields its **memory address**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_3aa8fd6ad3", "name": "Address-of operator yields an address", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Pointers (&, *, ->).md", "message": "## The Address-of Operator (&)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Placing & before an existing variable yields that variable's memory address (its box number), not the value stored in it."}
{"aliases": ["The `*` symbol wears **two hats** depending on placement"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_dd8e4fd781", "name": "Two roles of the asterisk", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Pointers (&, *, ->).md", "message": "## The Pointer / Dereference Operator (*)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In a declaration the * symbol means the variable holds a memory address rather than data, while in executable code before an existing pointer it means dereference: follow the stored address and read or write the value located there."}
{"aliases": ["`out->total_kb` is shorthand for `(*out).total_kb`"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_d01c3891a8", "name": "Arrow operator is dereference-then-field", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Pointers (&, *, ->).md", "message": "## The Arrow (->)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For a pointer to a struct, p->field is shorthand for (*p).field, following the address to modify the original struct back in the caller."}
{"aliases": ["C passes function arguments **by value**.", "Passing the address (`&memory`) is how C simulates pass-by-reference."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_22d3fb48ed", "name": "Passing an address simulates pass-by-reference", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Pointers (&, *, ->).md", "message": "## Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Pointers (&, *, ->).md", "message": "calling `parse_meminfo(input, &memory)` passes the *address* of the caller's `struct memory`", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "C passes function arguments by value, so a plain struct argument would be copied and its mutations would vanish on return; passing the struct's address (&memory) and reaching it through * or -> is how C simulates pass-by-reference and lets the function modify the caller's struct."}
{"aliases": ["Returning the address of a **stack** array is the classic dangling-pointer bug"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_e9b93c52e3", "name": "Returning a stack address dangles", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Pointers (&, *, ->).md", "message": "## Pointers & Memory Lifetime (dangling pointers)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Returning the address of a local stack array produces a dangling pointer, because the function's stack frame is destroyed on return and any later use of the pointer is undefined behaviour."}
{"aliases": ["an automatic Find & Replace that runs right before the compiler sees your source"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_d1bfda9343", "name": "Macros are preprocessor text substitution", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Preprocessor Macros.md", "message": "## Overview", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Preprocessor Macros.md", "message": "## Two Flavors", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A macro is a named piece of code that the C preprocessor replaces textually at every use before compilation; object-like macros act as constants and function-like macros as inline code generators."}
{"aliases": ["**No type checking** — the preprocessor operates before the compiler parses types"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_ae5cb9a7b3", "name": "Macros bypass type checking", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Preprocessor Macros.md", "message": "## The Catch: Dumb Text Replacement", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because macro expansion happens before the compiler parses types, macros receive no type checking and accept any argument."}
{"aliases": ["`SQUARE(x++)` expands to `((x++) * (x++))` — x increments **twice**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_060893b72b", "name": "Repeated macro arguments evaluate twice", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Preprocessor Macros.md", "message": "## The Catch: Dumb Text Replacement", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A function-like macro that uses its argument more than once evaluates that argument more than once, so SQUARE(x++) expands to ((x++) * (x++)) and increments x twice."}
{"aliases": ["Macros can capture things ordinary functions can't — `__FILE__` and `__LINE__`."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_dc89020292", "name": "Macros capture source metadata", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Preprocessor Macros.md", "message": "## The Power: Source Metadata", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Macros can capture __FILE__ and __LINE__, which ordinary functions cannot see, and that is how TEST_CHECK expands into code reporting the file and line of a failed check."}
{"aliases": ["Variadic macros became standard in **C99** (1999)."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_7c73003647", "name": "Variadic macros are C99", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C Preprocessor Macros.md", "message": "## Variadic Macros (C99+)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Variadic macros accept a variable number of arguments using ... and forward them via __VA_ARGS__, and they became standard in C99, so only pre-C99 compilers lack support."}
{"aliases": ["H(P,Q) = Σ q(x)·(−log₂ p(x)) — the model's surprise, weighted by the truth", "the average surprise the model assigns to the truth"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_3d450a852a", "name": "Cross-entropy definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cross-Entropy from NLL.md", "message": "## Definition (two distributions)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Cross-entropy H(P,Q) = Σ_x q(x)·(−log₂ p(x)) is the average surprise −log₂ p(x) that the model distribution P assigns to events, weighted by the true distribution Q, and it is defined for any two distributions over the same events without needing labels."}
{"aliases": ["L = −Σ_j y_j log(ŷ_j) = −log(ŷ_correct)", "the CE loss *is* the NLL"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_9b7cce5777", "name": "Cross-entropy equals NLL under one-hot labels", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cross-Entropy from NLL.md", "message": "## Loss view (Rohit P1 L06)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "With one-hot labels the truth is 1 on the correct class and 0 elsewhere, so cross-entropy loss reduces to −log(ŷ_correct), which is exactly the negative log-likelihood."}
{"aliases": ["∂L/∂z_j = softmax(z)_j − y_j"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_3b440e26bc", "name": "Softmax gradient of cross-entropy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cross-Entropy from NLL.md", "message": "Gradient through softmax", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The gradient of cross-entropy loss through a softmax is ∂L/∂z_j = softmax(z)_j − y_j."}
{"aliases": ["H(P,Q) = H(Q) + KL(Q∥P)", "minimizing cross-entropy is exactly minimizing KL(Q∥P)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_253bfdeea6", "name": "Cross-entropy equals data entropy plus KL", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cross-Entropy from NLL.md", "message": "## Why minimizing cross-entropy works", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cross-Entropy from NLL.md", "message": "## KL bridge — [[KL Divergence]]", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Cross-entropy decomposes as H(P,Q) = H(Q) + KL(Q∥P) with KL ≥ 0, and because the data's entropy H(Q) does not depend on the model's parameters, minimizing cross-entropy is exactly minimizing KL(Q∥P)."}
{"aliases": ["H(P,Q) ≥ H(Q), with equality **iff P = Q**", "The floor is set by the data, not by the model."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_e5c3a3640c", "name": "Cross-entropy floor is the data's entropy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cross-Entropy from NLL.md", "message": "## Floor rule", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cross-Entropy from NLL.md", "message": "Misconception to avoid: calling the floor \"the model's own entropy H(P)\".", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "H(P,Q) ≥ H(Q), with equality if and only if P = Q, so the cross-entropy floor is the entropy of the distribution supplying the weights — the data's entropy — not the model's own entropy H(P)."}
{"aliases": ["every buffer must reserve one extra byte for the `'\\0'`"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_d4d3fd1ab3", "name": "Null terminator needs an extra byte", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C String Buffer Boundaries.md", "message": "## Overview", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "C strings are null-terminated, so every buffer must reserve one extra byte for the '\\0' beyond the maximum number of characters it stores."}
{"aliases": ["Inserting a username of exactly 32 characters wrote `'\\0'` **out of bounds**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_f8cefed4b8", "name": "Exact-maximum insert writes out of bounds", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C String Buffer Boundaries.md", "message": "## The +1 Null-Terminator Bug", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A buffer sized exactly to the column maximum, such as char username[32], writes its '\\0' out of bounds when a 32-character value is inserted, corrupting the next field or crashing, and the fix is to size it COLUMN_USERNAME_SIZE + 1."}
{"aliases": ["`sscanf()` into fixed buffers can overflow on long inputs — it copies without bound checks.", "Validate first, copy second."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_7e743df650", "name": "sscanf copies without bounds", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C String Buffer Boundaries.md", "message": "## sscanf → strtok", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "sscanf into fixed buffers copies input without bound checks and can overflow on long input, so bounded parsing must tokenize and check each field's strlen before copying it into a struct."}
{"aliases": ["Happy-path tests miss all four; boundary tests prove the code fails *cleanly*."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_335d52aab1", "name": "Boundary tests surface four failure classes", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/C String Buffer Boundaries.md", "message": "## What Boundary Tests Surface (from Part 4)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Boundary tests at maximum lengths and beyond surface four classes of bug that happy-path tests miss: a missing +1 null terminator at exact maximum payload size, overflow beyond the maximum (which should give a clean parser error), capacity exhaustion (a table-full error), and invalid input types such as negative IDs (which should give a clean validation error)."}
{"aliases": ["F_X(x) = P(X \\leq x)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_e3085b8455", "name": "CDF definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cumulative distribution function.md", "message": "The **cumulative distribution function (cdf)** of a random variable $X$ gives the probability that $X$ is less than or equal to some value $x$", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The cumulative distribution function F_X(x) = P(X ≤ x) gives the probability that a random variable X is less than or equal to x, and unlike the pdf it exists for both discrete and continuous random variables."}
{"aliases": ["F_X(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{x} f(z) \\, dz", "f(x) = \\frac{d}{dx} F_X(x)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_585c069517", "name": "CDF integrates PDF and PDF differentiates CDF", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cumulative distribution function.md", "message": "## For continuous variables", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For a continuous random variable the cdf is the integral of the pdf from −∞ to x, and equivalently the pdf is the derivative of the cdf with respect to x."}
{"aliases": ["$F_X(x)$ is non-decreasing", "\\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} F_X(x) = 0"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_7174ff27cc", "name": "CDF properties", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cumulative distribution function.md", "message": "## Key properties", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A cdf always lies between 0 and 1, is non-decreasing in x as more probability mass is accumulated, and tends to 0 as x → −∞ and to 1 as x → +∞."}
{"aliases": ["**PDF $f(x)$**: the density (relative concentration) of probability near $x$.", "**CDF $F_X(x)$**: the accumulated probability from the left edge up to $x$. This *is* a probability."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:18:36Z", "id": "n_0dbf44d500", "name": "PDF is density, CDF is probability", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Cumulative distribution function.md", "message": "## PDF vs CDF", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A pdf value is a density of probability near x that must be integrated and is not itself a probability (f(1.7) = 2.5 is admissible), whereas a cdf value is an accumulated actual probability (F_X(1.7) = 0.4 means 40% are at most 1.7 m)."}
{"aliases": ["the family of failures that show up as the number of features d grows", "the geometry that makes low-dimensional intuition work stops holding"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_8f520154c3", "name": "Curse of dimensionality", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Curse of Dimensionality.md", "message": "opening definition paragraph", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The curse of dimensionality is the family of failures that appear as the number of features d grows: the geometry that makes low-dimensional intuition work stops holding, even though every individual coordinate still behaves perfectly well."}
{"aliases": ["the ratio of the largest to the smallest pairwise distance", "max/min ≈ 1.8 at d = 10 → ≈ 1.02 at d = 1000"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_8ff94211a1", "name": "Distance concentration collapses relative contrast", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Curse of Dimensionality.md", "message": "## Distance concentration", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In high dimensions pairwise distances become more alike: the relative contrast, defined as the ratio of the largest to the smallest pairwise distance, collapses toward 1 (about 1.8 at d = 10 and about 1.02 at d = 1000 in the lesson's working numbers)."}
{"aliases": ["The distances are not getting smaller; they are getting more similar.", "A claim that high dimensions \"shrink distances\" has the direction wrong."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_4d37ccc2fd", "name": "High dimensions make distances similar, not smaller", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Curse of Dimensionality.md", "message": "Distance concentration bullet list", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "High dimensionality does not shrink pairwise distances; it makes them more similar, so nearest and farthest neighbours become nearly equidistant and 'closest' stops being informative."}
{"aliases": ["This is the failure mode behind distance-based methods (k-NN, k-means, plain Euclidean clustering) degrading in high dimensions"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_e3e0df570a", "name": "Distance-based methods degrade under concentration", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Curse of Dimensionality.md", "message": "**Detector:** read a max/min ratio as a **contrast** measure", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because relative contrast approaches 1 in high dimensions, distance-based methods such as k-NN, k-means, and plain Euclidean clustering degrade, which motivates dimensionality reduction before any distance-based work."}
{"aliases": ["PCA redistributes variance; it never removes it", "Σλ = trace(C)", "PCA can keep k < d axes only when the spread is unequal across directions"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_3e90a86d9c", "name": "PCA redistributes variance but requires anisotropy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Curse of Dimensionality.md", "message": "## Why this bites PCA: compressibility needs anisotropy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "PCA can keep k < d axes only when the spread is unequal across directions, and since the total spread Σλ = trace(C) is invariant under rotation, PCA redistributes variance rather than removing it."}
{"aliases": ["not one axis can be dropped", "one axis holds 99% of the pie and the rest can go at ~1% loss"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_d11011ebd5", "name": "Isotropic versus anisotropic variance spectra", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Curse of Dimensionality.md", "message": "Isotropic case / Anisotropic case bullets", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When the covariance is isotropic (C = λI) every direction is an eigenvector and the scree is flat, so no axis can be dropped, whereas an anisotropic spectrum such as λ = (99, 1) lets one axis hold 99% of the variance and the rest be discarded at about 1% loss."}
{"aliases": ["Data augmentation artificially expands your dataset by applying random but realistic transformations."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_45580ac906", "name": "Data augmentation definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Data Augmentation (torchvision).md", "message": "opening paragraph", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Data augmentation artificially expands a small dataset by applying random but realistic transformations to its examples."}
{"aliases": ["Augmentation is for training only.", "Never apply augmentation to test/validation data."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_eca9826ad6", "name": "Never augment test or validation data", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Data Augmentation (torchvision).md", "message": "## Critical Rule", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Augmentation must never be applied to test or validation data; the test set must represent the real, unmodified images the model would encounter in production, so test pipelines apply only normalization."}
{"aliases": ["mean=[0.485, 0.456, 0.406], std=[0.229, 0.224, 0.225]"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_f810e54bf8", "name": "ImageNet normalization statistics", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Data Augmentation (torchvision).md", "message": "The ImageNet normalization stats", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The ImageNet normalization statistics mean=[0.485, 0.456, 0.406] and std=[0.229, 0.224, 0.225] are the standard for pre-trained models and should be used when doing transfer learning."}
{"aliases": ["Adding functionality to things without changing them."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_5831f4997a", "name": "Decorator pattern definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Decorator Pattern.md", "message": "## Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Decorator Pattern is a structural design pattern that adds functionality to objects without modifying their original code, by wrapping the object in a decorator that layers on new behavior while preserving the original interface."}
{"aliases": ["open for extension, closed for modification"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_67b28025ad", "name": "Decorator realizes the Open/Closed Principle", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Decorator Pattern.md", "message": "## Core idea", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Decorator Pattern is a direct application of the Open/Closed Principle: the extended entity is open for extension but closed for modification."}
{"aliases": ["The decorator implements the same interface as the component"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_b8c7b792fc", "name": "Decorator wraps and delegates", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Decorator Pattern.md", "message": "## How it works", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A decorator holds a reference to the wrapped component, implements the same interface as that component, and adds its own behavior before or after delegating to the component, so decorators can be stacked around one object."}
{"aliases": ["When subclassing would create an explosion of combinations"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_71f79f95d3", "name": "When to use the decorator pattern", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Decorator Pattern.md", "message": "## When to use", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Decorator Pattern is appropriate when behavior must be added at runtime rather than compile time, when subclassing would produce a combinatorial explosion of variants, or when the original class cannot or should not be modified."}
{"aliases": ["I don't know how to do this, but I know who does."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_c8dc62c009", "name": "Delegation pattern definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Delegation Pattern.md", "message": "opening definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Delegation Pattern is a structural design pattern in which one method hands off its work to another helper method instead of performing the work itself."}
{"aliases": ["Method overloading is the language feature that enables this pattern."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_b598e2fde3", "name": "Overloading enables delegation, intent defines it", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Delegation Pattern.md", "message": "## Relationship to method overloading", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Method overloading is the language feature that enables the Delegation Pattern, while the pattern itself is the architectural intent to separate concerns and hand work off to a delegate."}
{"aliases": ["Telescoping Methods", "each filling defaults and calling the next longer version"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_e7eeaddd22", "name": "Telescoping methods", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Delegation Pattern.md", "message": "## Also known as", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Telescoping methods are a stack of overloaded methods that collapse like a telescope, each filling in defaults and calling the next longer version until the method that does the real work is reached."}
{"aliases": ["delegation is how SRP is often implemented; each method keeps one job"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_82f372880c", "name": "Delegation implements single responsibility", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Delegation Pattern.md", "message": "## Related", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Delegation is the usual way the Single Responsibility Principle is implemented at method level, because each method keeps one job and hands the rest to another method."}
{"aliases": ["how a function changes at a point when you move in a chosen direction"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_f43698f165", "name": "Directional derivative definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Directional derivative.md", "message": "opening paragraph", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The directional derivative measures how a function changes at a point when movement occurs in a chosen direction, rather than only along a coordinate axis."}
{"aliases": ["J_f(x) r"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_6561fb0fb6", "name": "Directional derivative equals Jacobian times direction", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Directional derivative.md", "message": "definition formula", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For f mapping R^n to R^m, the directional derivative of f at x in direction r is the Jacobian-vector product J_f(x) r."}
{"aliases": ["A partial derivative is just a directional derivative along a coordinate axis."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_afd6bf5fc9", "name": "Partial derivative is a directional derivative", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Directional derivative.md", "message": "## Why it matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A partial derivative is the special case of a directional derivative taken along a coordinate axis."}
{"aliases": ["seeding the tangent with r computes this quantity directly"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_6284beaff7", "name": "Forward-mode AD computes directional derivatives", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Directional derivative.md", "message": "## Why it matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In forward-mode automatic differentiation, seeding the tangent with the direction r computes the directional derivative of the function directly."}
{"aliases": ["each character is mapped to a small dense vector"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_d99ae7dbdb", "name": "Dense learned character embeddings", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Distributed Representations (Character Embeddings).md", "message": "opening paragraph", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In distributed character representations each character is mapped to a small dense vector of learned floats, for example 10 numbers, instead of a sparse 27-dimensional one-hot vector."}
{"aliases": ["Similar characters end up with similar vectors.", "This isn't hardcoded — it emerges from the data."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_4d19a36051", "name": "Similar characters get similar vectors", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Distributed Representations (Character Embeddings).md", "message": "**Key property:**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "With learned character embeddings, similar characters end up with similar vectors, such as vowels clustering together or consonants appearing before 'h' clustering together, and this structure emerges from the training data rather than being hardcoded."}
{"aliases": ["A dense vector of 10 floats can encode rich information about a character's role"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_7ec0031861", "name": "Dense vectors encode graded properties", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Distributed Representations (Character Embeddings).md", "message": "A dense vector of 10 floats can encode rich information", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A sparse one-hot character code marks a single index and so cannot express degree, whereas a 10-float dense vector can encode graded properties of a character such as whether it is a vowel, commonly starts words, or pairs with 'h'."}
{"aliases": ["Discrete: jumping between rigid, separate buckets.", "Continuous: placing things on a smooth geometric map where distance = similarity."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_6fd08d510e", "name": "Continuous codes enable gradients and distance", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Distributed Representations (Character Embeddings).md", "message": "## Why \"Continuous\" Matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Continuous embeddings allow distances to be measured, interpolation between items, and gradient flow, whereas a discrete one-hot input has no in-between values and therefore nothing to differentiate, which is why continuous representations are what make gradient descent work."}
{"aliases": ["same as one-hot @ W but more efficient"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_883092d9b5", "name": "nn.Embedding is an efficient one-hot lookup", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Distributed Representations (Character Embeddings).md", "message": "**In PyTorch:** `nn.Embedding(27, 10)`", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In PyTorch, nn.Embedding(27, 10) is a learnable lookup table mapping each integer index to its 10-dimensional vector, equivalent to multiplying a one-hot vector by a weight matrix but more efficient."}
{"aliases": ["P_X(S) = P(X ∈ S) = P(X^{-1}(S))", "P_X = P ∘ X^{-1}"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_0800f3b39f", "name": "Distribution (law) of a random variable", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Distribution of a random variable.md", "message": "opening definition, equation (6.8) from MML", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Distribution of a random variable.md", "message": "## Mathematical perspective", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The distribution, or law, of a random variable X is the map P_X(S) = P(X ∈ S) = P(X^{-1}(S)) that assigns a probability to every set S of possible values, i.e. P_X = P composed with X^{-1}."}
{"aliases": ["probability about values of X = probability about underlying sample points whose X-value falls in that set"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_6890a27a4b", "name": "Distribution probability as pre-image probability", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Distribution of a random variable.md", "message": "## Intuition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Computing P_X(S) means finding all outcomes ω in the sample space for which X(ω) lies in S — the pre-image X^{-1}(S) — and adding up the probabilities of those outcomes."}
{"aliases": ["Models estimate P(X = label | features) on T, not probabilities on the vast raw sample space"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_06720459be", "name": "ML estimates distributions on the target space", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Distribution of a random variable.md", "message": "## ML significance", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Machine learning models estimate P(X = label | features) on a simple numeric target space rather than probabilities on the raw, vast sample space Ω, which is why the distribution of a random variable rather than the original probability measure is the working object."}
{"aliases": ["CUDA 12.4 code doesn't run on CUDA 11.8", "Docker isolates the CUDA toolkit inside the container while sharing the host GPU driver."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_20306c88fd", "name": "Docker isolates CUDA but shares the GPU driver", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Docker for AI Development.md", "message": "## Why AI Needs Docker", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Docker isolates the CUDA toolkit inside a container while sharing the host GPU driver, so a project pinned to one CUDA version can run even when the host ships a different one, such as CUDA 12.4 code on a CUDA 11.8 machine."}
{"aliases": ["A 7B model is 14 GB in fp16.", "without them, your 14 GB models vanish when the container stops"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_93326777ac", "name": "Volumes persist large model weights", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Docker for AI Development.md", "message": "## Volume Mounts for AI", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Docker volumes mount models and datasets from the host into the container so that large weights, such as a 7B model stored as 14 GB in fp16, survive container restarts instead of being re-downloaded."}
{"aliases": ["Image: A read-only template (your recipe).", "Container: A running instance of an image (your kitchen).", "Volume: Persistent storage that survives container restarts."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_5df74280fc", "name": "Image, container, and volume", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Docker for AI Development.md", "message": "## Key Vocabulary", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Docker image is a read-only template built layer by layer from a Dockerfile, a container is a running instance of an image, and a volume is persistent storage that survives container restarts."}
{"aliases": ["Exposes host GPUs to Docker containers."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_c05e3d685e", "name": "NVIDIA Container Toolkit exposes host GPUs", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Docker for AI Development.md", "message": "## NVIDIA Container Toolkit", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The NVIDIA Container Toolkit exposes host GPUs to Docker containers, and this can be verified by running a CUDA image with --gpus all and executing nvidia-smi inside it."}
{"aliases": ["Services reach each other by name (`http://qdrant:6333`)."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_a644b424d7", "name": "Docker Compose links services by name", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Docker for AI Development.md", "message": "## Docker Compose Pattern", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Docker Compose defines a multi-container application in YAML, and services within that application reach each other using the service name as a hostname, such as http://qdrant:6333."}
{"aliases": ["Don't Repeat Yourself", "The question is: if one changes, must the other change too?"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_ef1bd3457d", "name": "DRY is about knowledge duplication", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/DRY Principle.md", "message": "## What DRY actually means", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Don't Repeat Yourself concerns duplication of knowledge and intent rather than duplication of code text, and the diagnostic question is whether a change in one place forces a matching change in the other."}
{"aliases": ["coincidental duplication, not a problem"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_b67aecb2b5", "name": "Identical code is not necessarily a DRY violation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/DRY Principle.md", "message": "## What DRY actually means", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Two identical code fragments that represent different knowledge, meaning different reasons to change, are coincidental duplication rather than a DRY violation."}
{"aliases": ["Comments that repeat code"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_c2d5e86dd2", "name": "Comments that restate code violate DRY", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/DRY Principle.md", "message": "## Examples of DRY violations", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A comment that describes exactly what the code does is a DRY violation, because the intent is expressed twice."}
{"aliases": ["Caching for performance", "The violation must not be exposed to the outside world."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_4b01657ca4", "name": "Caching duplicates data but may be localized", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/DRY Principle.md", "message": "## When to violate DRY", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Caching the result of an expensive computation technically duplicates data, so the DRY violation is acceptable only when the duplication is kept internal to a class or module and never exposed to outside code."}
{"aliases": ["Language Server Protocol: standard for editors to get type info, completions, and diagnostics", "Microsoft's Python LSP using Pyright for type checking and IntelliSense"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_62fb9bd3d3", "name": "Language Server Protocol", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Editor and Remote Dev Setup.md", "message": "## Key Terms", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Language Server Protocol is a standard that lets editors obtain type information, completions, and diagnostics from a language-specific server; Pylance is Microsoft's Python language server built on Pyright."}
{"aliases": ["VS Code extension that runs a server on a remote machine and streams UI to your local editor"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:27Z", "id": "n_d73ceea19a", "name": "Remote SSH runs the server on the remote machine", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Editor and Remote Dev Setup.md", "message": "## Key Terms / ## Remote SSH Setup", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The VS Code Remote SSH extension runs a server on a remote machine and streams its UI to the local editor, so a remote GPU box can be edited and debugged as if its files were local."}
{"aliases": ["A smooth multivariable function looks linear if you zoom in close enough around a point.", "the curved map is replaced, near one point, by a constant linear map"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_c51690e0e3", "name": "Local linearity of smooth functions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Local linearity.md", "message": "The core idea", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A smooth multivariable function is locally linear: for a small input change Δx, f(x + Δx) ≈ f(x) + J_f(x)·Δx, so near one point the curved map is replaced by a constant linear map."}
{"aliases": ["one best-fit linear transformation", "It is the multivariable version of a tangent line"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_2c110c57cf", "name": "Jacobian as local linear map", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Local linearity.md", "message": "The matrix that captures that local linear behavior is the Jacobian matrix.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Local linearity.md", "message": "Why this matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Jacobian matrix J_f(x) is the matrix that captures a smooth multivariable function's local linear behavior at a point, making it the multivariable analogue of a tangent line: one best-fit linear transformation rather than one best-fit line."}
{"aliases": ["p_c = e^{z_c} / Σ_j e^{z_j}", "Logits are unnormalized scores; softmax turns them into probabilities."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_8d27e2aab9", "name": "Softmax normalizes logits", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Logits & Log-odds.md", "message": "Logits", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Logits & Log-odds.md", "message": "Logits are *scores before normalization* (any real value, no constraint)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Softmax converts raw logit scores z into probabilities by p_c = e^{z_c} / Σ_j e^{z_j}, so a logit is an unnormalized score and is not itself a probability of anything."}
{"aliases": ["z + c·1 leaves p unchanged", "Only differences matter — shift invariance"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_d1c7c7cc8b", "name": "Logit shift invariance", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Logits & Log-odds.md", "message": "Only differences matter — shift invariance: z + c·1 leaves p unchanged", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Adding a constant to every logit, z + c·1, leaves softmax probabilities unchanged, so only differences between logits carry information and absolute logit values are meaningless."}
{"aliases": ["z = log(p/(1−p)) exactly, and p = σ(z)", "This is the only place where \"logit\" and \"log-odds\" are the same object."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_c8b858fb68", "name": "Two-class logit equals log-odds", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Logits & Log-odds.md", "message": "Log-odds", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "With two classes the logit equals the log-odds exactly, z = log(p/(1−p)) and p = σ(z); this binary case is the only setting in which 'logit' and 'log-odds' name the same object."}
{"aliases": ["z_c − z_j = log(p_c/p_j)", "in multi-class the log-odds live in logit *differences*"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_24773443e2", "name": "Multi-class invariant is logit difference", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Logits & Log-odds.md", "message": "K classes: the invariant object is the pairwise log-odds", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "With K classes the invariant object is the pairwise log-odds z_c − z_j = log(p_c/p_j); a multi-class logit is a log-odds only relative to a designated reference class."}
{"aliases": ["certainty sits at infinity", "Probability 1 ⇔ log-odds → +∞"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_0653db2c0b", "name": "Certainty sits at infinite log-odds", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Logits & Log-odds.md", "message": "Probability 1 ⇔ log-odds → +∞: certainty sits at infinity, which is why Label Smoothing exists.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A probability of 1 corresponds to log-odds tending to +∞, so exact certainty sits at infinity and is unreachable by any finite logit; this is the reason label smoothing exists."}
{"aliases": ["measures how far the model's predictions are from ground truth"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_67a5bed419", "name": "Loss function measures prediction error", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Loss Functions (PyTorch).md", "message": "A **loss function** measures how far the model's predictions are from ground truth. Training minimizes this number.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A loss function measures how far a model's predictions are from ground truth, and training minimizes that number."}
{"aliases": ["expects raw logits (no softmax) — it applies softmax internally"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_8630c47d01", "name": "CrossEntropyLoss takes raw logits", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Loss Functions (PyTorch).md", "message": "Common Pitfall", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "PyTorch's nn.CrossEntropyLoss expects raw logits with no softmax applied beforehand, because it applies softmax internally."}
{"aliases": ["you're applying softmax twice and the model won't learn properly"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_1aec427e9c", "name": "Double softmax prevents learning", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Loss Functions (PyTorch).md", "message": "If you apply softmax in `forward()` and also use `CrossEntropyLoss`, you're applying softmax twice", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Applying softmax in the model's forward() and also using nn.CrossEntropyLoss applies softmax twice, and the model then does not learn properly."}
{"aliases": ["Binary classification | Binary Cross-Entropy | `nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss()`"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_2357853117", "name": "PyTorch loss per task type", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Loss Functions (PyTorch).md", "message": "Common Losses", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "PyTorch pairs binary classification with nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss, C-class classification with nn.CrossEntropyLoss, and regression with nn.MSELoss."}
{"aliases": ["solves the vanishing gradient problem of basic RNNs with **gates**", "learned mechanisms that decide what to *remember* and what to *forget*"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_3ed9fe2187", "name": "LSTM gates counter vanishing gradients", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory).md", "message": "LSTM networks solve the vanishing gradient problem of basic RNNs", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "LSTM networks address the vanishing gradient problem of basic RNNs using gates — learned mechanisms that decide what to remember and what to forget."}
{"aliases": ["3 (input, forget, output)", "2 (reset, update) — simpler"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_19c657afc9", "name": "LSTM versus GRU gate count", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory).md", "message": "LSTM vs GRU", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An LSTM has three gates (input, forget, output) while a GRU has two (reset, update) and roughly 25% fewer parameters, making the GRU preferable for smaller datasets and faster training."}
{"aliases": ["without it, LSTM expects `[seq_len, batch, features]`", "**batch_first=True** is critical"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_86280dc21a", "name": "nn.LSTM batch_first and shape", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory).md", "message": "batch_first=True is critical", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "By default nn.LSTM expects input shaped [seq_len, batch, features]; setting batch_first=True makes it accept [batch, seq_len, features], which matches DataLoader output."}
{"aliases": ["There is no clean split between \"development\" and \"maintenance.\"", "Every change is maintenance."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_35240e51a7", "name": "No split between development and maintenance", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Maintenance Mindset.md", "message": "Core idea", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "There is no clean split between development and maintenance: from the first line written, software is being maintained, because requirements change, understanding deepens, edge cases emerge, and dependencies update."}
{"aliases": ["\"Done\" is temporary", "Refactoring is maintenance, not a separate activity"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_ea3c353857", "name": "Done is temporary", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Maintenance Mindset.md", "message": "Implications", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In the maintenance mindset, 'done' is temporary and refactoring counts as continuous maintenance rather than a separate activity, since software that works today will need changes tomorrow."}
{"aliases": ["(m×n) @ (n×p) = (m×p)", "the inner dimensions MUST match"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_c4d5726705", "name": "Matrix multiplication shape rule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Matrix Operations from Scratch.md", "message": "Shape Rules (The Golden Rule of Matrix Multiplication)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Matrix multiplication requires matching inner dimensions: (m×n) @ (n×p) = (m×p), so a 128×784 weight matrix times a 784×1 input vector yields a 128×1 output."}
{"aliases": ["Different operations, different rules, different results."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_d0a50bcf10", "name": "Element-wise versus matrix multiplication", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Matrix Operations from Scratch.md", "message": "Element-wise vs Matrix Multiplication", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Element-wise multiplication (*) multiplies matching positions of two same-shaped matrices, whereas matrix multiplication (@) takes dot products of rows with columns; they obey different rules and give different results."}
{"aliases": ["broadcasting stretches the smaller array to fit by repeating along missing dimensions"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_207fbfe540", "name": "Broadcasting stretches smaller arrays", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Matrix Operations from Scratch.md", "message": "Broadcasting", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When shapes do not match for an element-wise operation, broadcasting stretches the smaller array to fit by repeating it along the missing dimensions, and NumPy, PyTorch, and TensorFlow all do this automatically."}
{"aliases": ["output = relu(W @ x + b)", "Every dense layer in every neural network does exactly this."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_1d6c66f49e", "name": "Dense layer is relu(Wx + b)", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Matrix Operations from Scratch.md", "message": "Dense Layer Forward Pass", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A dense layer computes output = relu(W @ x + b): a linear transformation by the weight matrix, a bias added by broadcasting, then a ReLU activation that clamps negatives to zero."}
{"aliases": ["Zero → singular matrix (crushes dimension, no inverse)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_894dbc8175", "name": "2x2 determinant and singularity", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Matrix Operations from Scratch.md", "message": "Key Implementation Details", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The determinant of a 2×2 matrix [[a, b], [c, d]] is ad − bc; a determinant of zero marks a singular matrix that crushes dimension and has no inverse."}
{"aliases": ["The matrix tells you exactly where the basis vectors [1, 0] and [0, 1] end up. Everything else follows."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_5f71c3e8c3", "name": "Matrix columns are transformed basis vectors", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Matrix Transformations.md", "message": "Transformations as matrices", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Every linear transformation in 2D can be written as a 2×2 matrix whose columns are exactly where the basis vectors [1, 0] and [0, 1] end up, and the image of every other point follows from that."}
{"aliases": ["Order matters: B @ A means apply A first, then B.", "Matrix multiplication is not commutative."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_cf7cc638ea", "name": "Composition is ordered matrix multiplication", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Matrix Transformations.md", "message": "Composition: chaining transformations", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Applying transformation A and then transformation B is the same as multiplying result = B @ A @ point, so composition order matters and matrix multiplication is not commutative."}
{"aliases": ["the matrix only scales them, never rotates them", "v is the eigenvector (direction that survives)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_096c42c723", "name": "Eigenvectors are only scaled", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Matrix Transformations.md", "message": "Eigenvalues and eigenvectors", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An eigenvector of a matrix is a direction the matrix only scales and never rotates, satisfying A @ v = λ·v, where the eigenvalue λ is the scalar factor by which that direction is stretched."}
{"aliases": ["Magnitude < 1 causes them to vanish.", "This is the vanishing/exploding gradient problem stated in one sentence."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_6ea4682110", "name": "Eigenvalue magnitude governs recurrent stability", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Matrix Transformations.md", "message": "Why eigenvalues matter", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In recurrent networks and dynamical systems, eigenvalues with magnitude greater than 1 cause outputs to explode while magnitude less than 1 causes them to vanish; this is the vanishing/exploding gradient problem."}
{"aliases": ["The determinant of a transformation matrix tells you how much it scales area (2D) or volume (3D)."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_57311d869d", "name": "Determinant is area or volume scaling", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Matrix Transformations.md", "message": "Determinant as volume scaling factor", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The determinant of a transformation matrix is the factor by which it scales area in 2D or volume in 3D: 1 preserves area, 2 doubles it, 0 crushes space to a lower dimension, and −1 preserves area but flips orientation."}
{"aliases": ["distinguished by **who decides when memory is freed**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_2fcb2f6731", "name": "Three memory strategies by who frees", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Memory management.md", "message": "Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Memory management.md", "message": "There are three primary strategies", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Memory management strategies are distinguished by who decides when memory is freed: garbage collection leaves it to the runtime, manual management to the programmer, and compile-time ownership to the compiler."}
{"aliases": ["Circular references", "data is freed instantly when its counter hits zero"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_6bbb9b4017", "name": "Reference counting leaks cycles", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Memory management.md", "message": "Reference Counting", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Reference counting frees an object instantly when its reference counter hits zero, but it never frees circular references, whose counters never reach zero, so a cyclic collector is needed as backup."}
{"aliases": ["An object is \"reachable\" if there exists a chain of references from any root to that object", "\"Stop-the-world\" pauses"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_d6e4185aea", "name": "Tracing GC marks reachable then sweeps", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Memory management.md", "message": "Tracing GC (Mark-and-Sweep)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Tracing garbage collection marks every object reachable by a chain of references from a root and then sweeps away everything unmarked, which collects circular references but costs stop-the-world pauses."}
{"aliases": ["Zero — no reference counting, no GC scans"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_2b56f88189", "name": "Manual memory management tradeoff", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Memory management.md", "message": "Manual Memory Management", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Manual memory management, using malloc/free in C or new/delete in C++, has zero runtime overhead but is error-prone: forgetting to free leaks memory and freeing too early causes dangling pointers and crashes."}
{"aliases": ["The **type is encoded in the receiver** (the object before the dot).", "each type has its own internal namespace (method table) of function implementations"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_1fa97ec171", "name": "Methods live in per-type namespaces", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Method scoping and namespaces.md", "message": "The Solution: Method Scoping (OO-style)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Method scoping and namespaces.md", "message": "Core Idea", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In object-oriented languages each type has its own internal method table, so the type is encoded in the receiver rather than in the function name and method names need not be globally unique."}
{"aliases": ["Same method name → different implementation, depending on the receiver's type."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_16d4046a30", "name": "Method dispatch on receiver type", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Method scoping and namespaces.md", "message": "How Method Dispatch Works", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Calling obj.method() makes the runtime look at the type of obj, look the method name up in that type's method table, and execute the implementation stored there, so the same name can mean different code per receiver type."}
{"aliases": ["The **type is encoded in the function name**."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_b2d2fb6ff1", "name": "Global namespaces need type prefixes", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Method scoping and namespaces.md", "message": "The Problem: Global Namespaces (Racket-style)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In a language with only top-level functions, all names share one global namespace, so type-specific prefixes such as hash-copy and vector-copy must encode the type in the function name to avoid collisions."}
{"aliases": ["A five-level hierarchy that builds from a single scalar autograd Value to a full neural network training loop", "Each level is built entirely on the one below it."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_3a9fc40614", "name": "Micrograd five-level hierarchy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Micrograd Architecture.md", "message": "micrograd architecture overview", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Micrograd is a five-level hierarchy — Value, Neuron, Layer, MLP, training loop — in which each level is built entirely on the level below it, the same structural pattern PyTorch follows with tensors and modules."}
{"aliases": ["scalar storing data, grad, _prev (children = the graph), _backward (local derivative), and _op label", "The autograd engine."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_7ed0960943", "name": "Micrograd Value is the autograd engine", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Micrograd Architecture.md", "message": "Value — scalar storing data, grad, _prev", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Micrograd's Value node is a scalar storing data, grad, _prev (its children, which form the graph), _backward (the local derivative), and an _op label; this single class is the autograd engine."}
{"aliases": ["**Neuron** — w·x + b → tanh", "**Layer** — list of Neurons. Parallel computation."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:28Z", "id": "n_1d97d3422b", "name": "Neuron Layer MLP composition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Micrograd Architecture.md", "message": "Neuron / Layer / MLP levels", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A micrograd Neuron computes tanh(w·x + b), a Layer is a list of neurons computed in parallel, and an MLP is a list of layers giving sequential depth."}
{"aliases": ["H(P) = −Σ p(x) log p(x) = Σ p(x)·(−log p(x))", "H(P) = E[−log p(X)]"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_bda21eb9c2", "name": "Shannon entropy as weighted average of surprise", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Entropy (Average Surprise).md", "message": "Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The entropy of a distribution P is H(P) = -sum over x of p(x) log p(x), equivalently the sum of p(x) times the surprise -log p(x), so entropy is the probability-weighted average of surprise."}
{"aliases": ["the weights **are** the probabilities", "p(x) appears twice in H(P) = −Σ p(x)·log p(x)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_fe63e7ad41", "name": "Entropy supplies its own weights", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Entropy (Average Surprise).md", "message": "Why the weights are the probabilities (2026-09-24)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In entropy the distribution p(x) appears twice: inside the log as the generator of surprise and outside as the weight, so the same distribution feeds itself and there is no second distribution from which to borrow weights."}
{"aliases": ["Entropy is a property of one distribution; [[Cross-Entropy from NLL]] measures a distribution *against* another"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_600f2aa8e3", "name": "Entropy is one distribution, cross-entropy two", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Entropy (Average Surprise).md", "message": "Nearest neighbor", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Entropy is a property of a single distribution, whereas cross-entropy measures one distribution against another, taking its weights from the true distribution Q and its surprises from the model P."}
{"aliases": ["Rare events don't blow it up"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_a78e024b9c", "name": "Rare events do not inflate entropy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Entropy (Average Surprise).md", "message": "Rare events don't blow it up", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A rare event does not inflate entropy much because its contribution p(x) log p(x) shrinks with its probability: a 1-in-1000 event with about 9.97 bits of surprise contributes only about 0.001 x 9.97 = 0.01 bits, and p log p tends to 0 as p tends to 0."}
{"aliases": ["Max at uniform, 0 at deterministic", "entropy is non-negative"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_961bd7aee6", "name": "Entropy bounds: uniform maximum, deterministic zero", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Entropy (Average Surprise).md", "message": "Examples", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Entropy is non-negative, reaches its maximum for a given variable at the uniform distribution (1 bit for a fair coin, log2 6 for a fair die) and equals zero at a deterministic distribution (a 99%-heads coin has H about 0.08 bits)."}
{"aliases": ["Every process receives a bag of `KEY=value` **strings** from its parent", "they have no types — everything is a string"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_1b1adf1b43", "name": "Environment variables are inherited untyped strings", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Environment Variables (Shell).md", "message": "What they are", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Every process receives a set of KEY=value strings from its parent and passes that environment along to any program it calls, which is how settings flow down a process tree; like shell variables generally, environment variables have no types."}
{"aliases": ["Setting them — three scopes", "**Per-command:** `TZ=Asia/Tokyo date`", "**Shell-local:** `FOO=1`"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_e2769880bc", "name": "Three scopes for setting a shell variable", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Environment Variables (Shell).md", "message": "Setting them — three scopes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A per-command assignment such as TZ=Asia/Tokyo date is visible only to that one child and never enters the shell, a shell-local assignment such as FOO=1 belongs to the shell process and is not inherited by children, and export marks a variable as part of the environment so every child inherits it."}
{"aliases": ["A child program cannot modify its parent's environment"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_f7866ac850", "name": "A child cannot modify its parent's environment", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Environment Variables (Shell).md", "message": "Why export/unset must be built-ins", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A child program cannot modify its parent's environment, because OS process isolation forbids it."}
{"aliases": ["Why export/unset must be built-ins"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_3da194caea", "name": "export and unset must be shell built-ins", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Environment Variables (Shell).md", "message": "Why export/unset must be built-ins", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "export and unset must be shell built-ins rather than external programs because they mutate the shell's own memory, its environment."}
{"aliases": ["Forgetting `export` is the classic bug"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_cdfb52a28e", "name": "Forgetting export is the classic env-var bug", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Environment Variables (Shell).md", "message": "Common uses & gotchas", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Forgetting export is the classic environment-variable bug: the script or program being called sees an empty variable while echo $VAR in the calling shell looks correct."}
{"aliases": ["good design is design that is **easier to change**", "ETC is the fundamental metric of good design"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_8c0195da42", "name": "ETC: good design is easier to change", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/ETC (Easier To Change).md", "message": "Core principle", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "ETC (Easier To Change) holds that good design is design that is easier to change, and treats ease of future change as the fundamental metric of good design."}
{"aliases": ["It's not about predicting the future — it's about not painting yourself into a corner", "It's not about making things perfect — it's about making things adaptable"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_091b983fab", "name": "ETC is adaptability, not prediction", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/ETC (Easier To Change).md", "message": "Core principle; Practical application", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "ETC aims at adaptability rather than perfection: every design decision is judged by how easy it makes future changes, and the goal is not predicting the future but avoiding painting yourself into a corner."}
{"aliases": ["Prefer loose coupling, clear interfaces, and small focused modules"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_d4bded39fb", "name": "Loose coupling, clear interfaces, small modules ease change", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/ETC (Easier To Change).md", "message": "Practical application", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Loose coupling, clear interfaces, and small focused modules are the structural choices that make code easier to change under ETC."}
{"aliases": ["\\frac{d}{dt} e^t = e^t", "The base \\(e\\) is the unique choice"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_15155d3148", "name": "e is the base whose exponential is its own derivative", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Euler number e.md", "message": "Why it is special", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Euler's number e is the unique base for which the exponential function equals its own derivative, so that d/dt e^t = e^t."}
{"aliases": ["\\frac{d}{dt} a^t = \\ln(a)\\,a^t", "differentiating an exponential does not change its shape; it only multiplies the function by a constant"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_1f888f3065", "name": "Derivative of an exponential is proportional to itself", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Exponential derivatives.md", "message": "Core rule", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Euler number e.md", "message": "Why it is special", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For any positive base a other than 1, the derivative of a^t is ln(a) times a^t, so differentiating an exponential does not change its shape and only multiplies it by the constant ln(a)."}
{"aliases": ["the derivative constant is \\(\\ln(a)\\)", "The base \\(e\\) is the unique choice where \\(\\ln(e) = 1\\)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_8b4be9e9e9", "name": "ln(e) = 1 makes e's derivative constant one", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Euler number e.md", "message": "Why it is special", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The derivative constant for base a is ln(a), and e is the unique base with ln(e) = 1, which is why writing exponentials in base e gives the exponent constant a clean derivative."}
{"aliases": ["a^t = e^{\\ln(a)t}", "This is why [[Natural logarithm]] appears whenever exponentials are differentiated"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_5da44ee3ce", "name": "Any exponential base converts to base e", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Euler number e.md", "message": "Connection to logs", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Any exponential base can be rewritten in base e as a^t = e^(ln(a) t), which is why the natural logarithm appears whenever exponentials are differentiated."}
{"aliases": ["Training loss going down doesn't prove your model works"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_b9b7bbe286", "name": "Falling training loss does not prove a model works", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Evaluation Protocol.md", "message": "Intro", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Training loss going down does not prove a model works; performance must be measured on data the model has never seen, a validation or test set."}
{"aliases": ["**Always call `model.eval()`** before evaluation", "dropout randomly disables neurons during inference"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_81cd94e1b2", "name": "model.eval() disables dropout and batch-norm training behaviour", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Evaluation Protocol.md", "message": "Critical Rules, rule 1", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Calling model.eval() before evaluation disables dropout and batch normalization's training behaviour, and forgetting it gives misleadingly bad results because dropout randomly disables neurons during inference."}
{"aliases": ["**Wrap in `torch.no_grad()`**", "autograd builds a graph and tracks gradients you'll never use"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_10ab03c7d7", "name": "torch.no_grad() disables gradient tracking", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Evaluation Protocol.md", "message": "Critical Rules, rule 2", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Wrapping evaluation in torch.no_grad() disables gradient tracking, saving memory and computation that autograd would otherwise spend building a graph for gradients that are never used."}
{"aliases": ["they work together but do different things"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_e2bc82df57", "name": "eval() and no_grad() do different jobs", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Evaluation Protocol.md", "message": "Critical Rules, rule 3", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "model.eval() switches layers into inference mode while torch.no_grad() disables gradient tracking; they are used together but do different things."}
{"aliases": ["**0 = success**, **any non-zero = failure**", "Read the last command's code with `$?`"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_abfe08c896", "name": "Exit code convention: zero is success", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Exit Codes & Short-Circuit Control Flow.md", "message": "Every command returns a number", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "By convention a command returns exit code 0 for success and any non-zero value for failure, and the shell variable $? holds the exit code of the most recently completed command."}
{"aliases": ["`echo $?` itself succeeds, so asking twice yields `0` the second time"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_cdf40043fa", "name": "$? is overwritten by the next command", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Exit Codes & Short-Circuit Control Flow.md", "message": "Every command returns a number", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because echo $? itself succeeds, querying $? a second time yields 0 immediately after a failure, so the status must be captured or branched on at once."}
{"aliases": ["`exit NUM` terminates the script (or shell) with code NUM", "Fatal paths in scripts should `exit 1`"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_7ecf307820", "name": "exit NUM terminates with a chosen status", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Exit Codes & Short-Circuit Control Flow.md", "message": "Choosing your own code: the `exit` built-in", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The exit built-in terminates the script or shell with code NUM, and fatal paths in scripts should exit 1 so callers such as cron, CI, or other scripts can detect the failure."}
{"aliases": ["Boolean operators act on return codes, not values", "Both are **short-circuiting**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_2310085024", "name": "Shell && and || dispatch on exit status", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Exit Codes & Short-Circuit Control Flow.md", "message": "Boolean operators act on return codes, not values", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The shell's && and || operators dispatch on the previous command's exit status rather than on true/false expression values, and both short-circuit so the right side runs only when the outcome demands it."}
{"aliases": ["the condition *is* a command judged by its exit status"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_4500f8eb6d", "name": "if and while conditions are commands judged by exit status", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Exit Codes & Short-Circuit Control Flow.md", "message": "Boolean operators act on return codes, not values", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In shell control flow the condition in if cmd; then ... and while cmd; do ... is itself a command judged by its exit status, which is the same mechanism as && and ||."}
{"aliases": ["the rate of change is proportional to the current distance from equilibrium"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_2aa9509a03", "name": "Newton's cooling rate tracks distance from equilibrium", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Exponential derivatives.md", "message": "Why this matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In Newton's law of cooling the rate of change is proportional to the current distance from equilibrium, making it an applied model of exponential decay."}
{"aliases": ["**Expression**: Its main job is to **produce a value**", "**Statement**: Its main job is to **produce an effect**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_f2f3403bdd", "name": "Expressions produce values, statements produce effects", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Expressions and statements.md", "message": "Intro", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An expression's main job is to produce a value, while a statement's main job is to produce an effect such as a side effect or control flow."}
{"aliases": ["An expression followed by a semicolon (`;`) **promotes** the expression to statement-hood"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_89f114a97d", "name": "A trailing semicolon promotes an expression to a statement", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Expressions and statements.md", "message": "Expression Statements", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An expression followed by a semicolon becomes an expression statement, taking something that computes a value and turning it into a standalone action."}
{"aliases": ["the distinction is about **intent**: computing a value vs executing an action"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_8a653be4cf", "name": "Expression/statement line blurs but intent does not", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Expressions and statements.md", "message": "Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Many languages blur the expression/statement line by allowing expressions where statements are expected, as in expression-oriented languages like Rust, but conceptually the distinction is about intent: computing a value versus executing an action."}
{"aliases": ["**Probe capability, not version.**", "Backports make versions lie"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_941a71e97f", "name": "Probe capabilities at runtime, not kernel versions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Feature Probing vs Kernel Version Checking.md", "message": "Why Not Version Checking; Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Production tools probe for a kernel feature's presence at runtime instead of checking the kernel version, because backports make version numbers lie and shipping a separate binary per kernel generation is unmaintainable."}
{"aliases": ["Estimated Available ≈ MemFree + Buffers + Cached", "only added in **Linux 3.14**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_e54622d1ea", "name": "Fallback estimate when MemAvailable is absent", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Feature Probing vs Kernel Version Checking.md", "message": "Graceful Fallback Math", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "MemAvailable was only added to /proc/meminfo in Linux 3.14, and on kernels without it available memory can be estimated as MemFree + Buffers + Cached using fields present since Linux 2.6, which is less precise than the kernel's own estimate but close on older systems."}
{"aliases": ["The sentinel value doubling as a feature probe", "`ULONG_MAX` is outside the legitimate domain"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_7548ce467f", "name": "A sentinel outside the domain doubles as a feature probe", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Feature Probing vs Kernel Version Checking.md", "message": "The Pattern: Runtime Feature Probing", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Initializing a field to a sentinel value outside its legitimate domain, such as ULONG_MAX for available memory, lets 'still sentinel' reliably mean the feature is absent, so the sentinel serves as both a correctness check and a runtime feature probe."}
{"aliases": ["**`MemAvailable`** is a kernel **estimate**", "This is what actually predicts \"can I start an app without paging?\""], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_900b45015a", "name": "MemAvailable estimates memory usable without swapping", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Feature Probing vs Kernel Version Checking.md", "message": "Which \"Available\" Field to Use (MemFree vs MemAvailable vs SwapFree)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "MemAvailable is a kernel estimate that combines MemFree with reclaimable memory such as file caches and buffers the kernel can discard or flush without swapping, so it is the field that predicts whether a new application can start without paging."}
{"aliases": ["**`SwapFree`** is only unused space on the secondary disk/swap partition, **not** main memory (RAM) at all"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_f32cbbd173", "name": "SwapFree is swap space, not main memory", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Feature Probing vs Kernel Version Checking.md", "message": "Which \"Available\" Field to Use (MemFree vs MemAvailable vs SwapFree)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "SwapFree measures only unused space on the disk swap partition and not main memory at all, so using it as a measure of RAM headroom puts the question on the wrong axis."}
{"aliases": ["no awareness of what already exists"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_b46d080982", "name": "scp copies over SSH without checking the destination", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/File Transfers over SSH — scp, rsync & ssh_config.md", "message": "scp — the simple copy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "scp src dest copies files or directories over SSH when either side may be written user@host:path, and it has no awareness of what already exists at the destination."}
{"aliases": ["**Skips identical files**", "Ideal for repeated syncs/backups"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_942c36e23d", "name": "rsync skips identical files via delta transfer", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/File Transfers over SSH — scp, rsync & ssh_config.md", "message": "rsync — delta-aware, resumable copying", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "rsync detects which files already match between source and destination and avoids re-copying them using delta transfer, which makes it suited to repeated syncs and backups."}
{"aliases": ["`-P`, which equals `--partial --progress`"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_992cbc6aa5", "name": "rsync -P makes interrupted copies resumable", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/File Transfers over SSH — scp, rsync & ssh_config.md", "message": "rsync — delta-aware, resumable copying", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The rsync flag -P equals --partial --progress, keeping partially transferred files and showing progress, so an interrupted copy continues instead of restarting."}
{"aliases": ["this file is **not only read by `ssh`**", "Now `ssh myserver` — or `rsync file.txt myserver:` — expands to all those flags"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_8d5e669b0c", "name": "~/.ssh/config host blocks are read by more than ssh", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/File Transfers over SSH — scp, rsync & ssh_config.md", "message": "~/.ssh/config — declare hosts once", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Host block in ~/.ssh/config declares a hostname, user, identity file, and port once, and unlike a shell alias that configuration is read not only by ssh but also by scp, rsync, mosh, and similar tools, which translate it into their own flags."}
{"aliases": ["a set of production rules that define which strings of symbols from an alphabet are valid in a given language"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_7897492cfd", "name": "Formal grammar definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Formal Grammar.md", "message": "A formal grammar is a set of production rules that define which strings of symbols from an alphabet are valid in a given language", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A formal grammar is a set of production rules that define which strings of symbols from an alphabet are valid in a given language, providing the mathematical foundation for both lexical analysis and parsing."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_752174489c", "name": "Lexical versus syntactic grammar levels", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Formal Grammar.md", "message": "## The Levels — \"A regular grammar (lower complexity) handles the lexical level. A context-free grammar (higher complexity) handles the syntactic level.\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In a compiler or interpreter a regular grammar handles the lexical level, mapping individual characters to a single token and implemented by the scanner, while a context-free grammar handles the syntactic level, mapping tokens to a full expression and implemented by the parser."}
{"aliases": ["G is defined as a tuple (N, Σ, P, S)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_3f2e659b25", "name": "Grammar as (N, Sigma, P, S)", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Formal Grammar.md", "message": "## Structure — \"A formal grammar G is defined as a tuple (N, Σ, P, S)\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A formal grammar G is defined as a tuple (N, Σ, P, S) of non-terminal symbols, terminal symbols, production rules, and a start symbol."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_07f9b4b19e", "name": "Chomsky hierarchy correspondence", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Formal Grammar.md", "message": "## Classification (Chomsky Hierarchy)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In the Chomsky hierarchy, Type 3 regular grammars correspond to finite automata and regex, Type 2 context-free grammars to pushdown automata, Type 1 context-sensitive grammars to linear-bounded automata, and Type 0 unrestricted grammars to Turing machines."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_1031fbffd8", "name": "Dual numbers in forward-mode autodiff", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Forward-Mode Autodiff.md", "message": "Forward-mode autodiff computes derivatives **alongside** values during the forward pass using **dual numbers**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Forward-mode autodiff computes derivatives alongside values during the forward pass using dual numbers, in which each value carries a primal (its actual value) and a tangent (its derivative with respect to the input of interest)."}
{"aliases": ["O(n) passes for n input variables"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_9eb290ed01", "name": "Forward-mode cost of O(n) passes", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Forward-Mode Autodiff.md", "message": "**Cost:** O(n) passes for n input variables — seed one input to 1, all others to 0, run a full forward pass.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Forward-mode autodiff requires O(n) forward passes for n input variables, because each pass seeds one input to 1 and all others to 0, so two inputs need two passes to yield both derivatives."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_7005b92467", "name": "Forward-mode memory is O(1)", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Forward-Mode Autodiff.md", "message": "**Memory:** O(1) — no graph needs to be stored.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Forward-mode autodiff uses O(1) memory because no computational graph needs to be stored."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_0f93a34554", "name": "Networks have millions of weight inputs", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Forward-Mode Autodiff.md", "message": "A network has millions of weight inputs.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A neural network has millions of weight inputs, one per learnable parameter."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_86c8aab20a", "name": "Forward mode impractical for neural networks", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Forward-Mode Autodiff.md", "message": "**Why not for neural nets:** ... would require millions of forward passes — computationally impossible.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Computing all gradients of a neural network by forward-mode autodiff would require millions of forward passes, one per weight input, making it computationally impossible in practice."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_46df78e952", "name": "Functional programming is immutable evaluation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Functional programming.md", "message": "## Core Idea — \"Treat computation as the evaluation of mathematical functions. Data is **read-only** (immutable)\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Functional programming treats computation as the evaluation of mathematical functions over read-only, immutable data: instead of changing an existing variable, a function receives old data and returns brand-new data."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_1e8935784e", "name": "Pure function determinism", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Functional programming.md", "message": "**Pure functions**: same input → same output, always. No side effects.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A pure function returns the same output for the same input every time and produces no side effects."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_6bdca16fc9", "name": "Immutability enables concurrency safety", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Functional programming.md", "message": "**No shared state**: avoids entire categories of concurrency bugs. / \"Concurrency-safe (immutable data can be read by multiple threads simultaneously)\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because functional data is immutable, it can be read by multiple threads simultaneously without synchronization, and the absence of shared mutable state avoids entire categories of concurrency bugs."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_2d2768a3ff", "name": "Functional programming costs", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Functional programming.md", "message": "## Pros & Cons — Cons", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Functional programming costs higher memory usage because data structures are copied instead of mutated in place, and has a steeper learning curve because recursion and composition replace loops and assignment."}
{"aliases": ["also called the normal distribution"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_cae3c9d257", "name": "Gaussian belongs to exponential family", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gaussian distribution.md", "message": "## Why it is useful — \"It belongs to the exponential family.\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Gaussian distribution, also called the normal distribution, belongs to the exponential family of probability distributions."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_f5dadfbc43", "name": "Gaussian closure under sums and linear maps", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gaussian distribution.md", "message": "Sums of Gaussian variables are still Gaussian. / Linear transformations of Gaussian variables are still Gaussian.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Gaussian variables are closed under addition and linear transformation: both a sum of Gaussian variables and a linear transformation of a Gaussian variable are still Gaussian, which makes Gaussians easy to work with."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_9218903511", "name": "Gaussian conjugacy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gaussian distribution.md", "message": "## Conjugacy — \"Gaussian priors and Gaussian likelihoods form a conjugate pair. That means the posterior is also Gaussian\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Gaussian prior and a Gaussian likelihood form a conjugate pair, so the resulting posterior is also Gaussian."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_e7886a266e", "name": "Gaussian posteriors allow closed-form updates", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gaussian distribution.md", "message": "\"the posterior is also Gaussian, so Bayesian updates can often be done in closed form\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because a Gaussian posterior stays Gaussian, Bayesian updates with Gaussian priors and likelihoods can often be computed in closed form."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_7a29ce2513", "name": "Gaussian as default noisy-quantity model", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gaussian distribution.md", "message": "## Intuition — \"The Gaussian is a good default model when many small effects combine to produce a noisy quantity.\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Gaussian is a good default model for a noisy quantity produced by many small effects combining."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_1430bae557", "name": "GCC four compilation stages", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/GCC Compilation Stages.md", "message": "## Overview — \"GCC compiles C programs through up to four distinct stages. Only the stages specified are carried out\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "GCC compiles C programs in up to four stages — preprocessing, compilation proper, assembly, and linking — performs only the stages specified on the command line, and by default runs all four when given a .c file."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_2eca6c12e5", "name": "Preprocessing expands directives", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/GCC Compilation Stages.md", "message": "### 1. Preprocessing", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The GCC preprocessing stage expands #include directives, #define macros, and other preprocessor directives, producing a preprocessed C file typically with a .i extension."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_c4eeabb7db", "name": "Compilation proper emits assembly", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/GCC Compilation Stages.md", "message": "### 2. Compilation Proper — \"Translates preprocessed C code into assembly language\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The GCC compilation-proper stage translates preprocessed C code into assembly language, typically in a .s file."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_ce319fa7ae", "name": "Assembly stage emits object file", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/GCC Compilation Stages.md", "message": "### 3. Assembly — \"The assembler converts assembly language into machine code object files\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The GCC assembly stage converts assembly language into a machine-code object file with a .o extension."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_c6df34f4da", "name": "Linking produces executable", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/GCC Compilation Stages.md", "message": "### 4. Linking — \"The linker combines all object files and libraries into an executable\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The GCC linking stage combines all object files and libraries into an executable binary."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_66f3446cc3", "name": "Git commit is a full snapshot", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Git for AI Workflows.md", "message": "**Commit** — A snapshot of the entire project at a point in time, not just changed files.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Git commit is a snapshot of the entire project at a point in time, not only the files that changed."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_2ba3b646d6", "name": "Git branch is a movable pointer", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Git for AI Workflows.md", "message": "## Branching for Experiments — \"Branch = a pointer to a commit that moves forward as you work.\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Git branch is a movable pointer to a commit that advances as work is committed, and branches are used for isolated experiments without breaking main."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_2ba302980b", "name": "Git merge applies branch changes", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Git for AI Workflows.md", "message": "**Merge** — Taking changes from one branch and applying them to another.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Git merge takes changes from one branch and applies them to another."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_049eb68e4a", "name": "Gitignore model checkpoints and binaries", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Git for AI Workflows.md", "message": "## ML Artifact .gitignore — \"Always exclude model checkpoints and large binary files\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Machine-learning repositories should exclude model checkpoints and large binary files via .gitignore, including *.pt, *.pth, *.safetensors, *.bin, *.onnx, and the data/ and models/ directories."}
{"aliases": ["Every time your code references global data, it ties itself into the other components that share that data."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_53183e0f09", "name": "Global data avoidance definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Global Data Avoidance.md", "message": "## Definition — \"explicitly passing required context into modules rather than reaching into shared global state\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Global data avoidance is the practice of explicitly passing required context into modules rather than reaching into shared global state, because every reference to global data ties code to every other component that shares that data."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_2f5cce28af", "name": "Read-only globals still cause coupling", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Global Data Avoidance.md", "message": "Even **read-only globals** cause trouble", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Even read-only global data causes problems: multithreading requires synchronization for it, a module cannot be tested in isolation because it depends on hidden global state, and a function cannot be understood without knowing the global scope."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_502eede31d", "name": "Explicit context passing removes hidden dependencies", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Global Data Avoidance.md", "message": "## The solution: explicit context / ## Benefits of avoidance", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Passing context explicitly, as constructor parameters in OOP or as structures containing the context, makes a function's dependencies visible in its signature, which improves testability, thread safety, understandability, and reuse."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_880051663f", "name": "Singletons are disguised globals", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Global Data Avoidance.md", "message": "## Singletons as global variables in disguise", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Singleton pattern ensures only one instance of a class exists and is often used as a way to have global variables in languages such as Java, so singletons carry the same coupling risks as global variables."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_f5a93f6136", "name": "Good enough over perfect", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Good Enough Software.md", "message": "The principle that software doesn't need to be perfect — it needs to be **good enough**. Knowing when to stop is a critical skill.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Software does not need to be perfect, only good enough, and knowing when to stop is a critical skill."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_e4d028a9f9", "name": "Perfection is infinite, scope creep harms shipping", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Good Enough Software.md", "message": "**It will never be perfect** / **Know when to stop**: More features ≠ better software. Scope creep is the enemy of shipping.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Chasing perfection is an infinite pursuit, so value should be delivered at the right threshold, and adding more features does not make better software because scope creep is the enemy of shipping."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_4571349fba", "name": "Users define good enough", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Good Enough Software.md", "message": "**Involve users in defining \"good enough\"** ... (Exception: life-critical systems like pacemakers, where you don't get to negotiate.)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The people using the software should help decide what counts as acceptable, except in life-critical systems such as pacemakers, where the threshold is not negotiable."}
{"aliases": ["Under-promise and over-deliver"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_f0b2fdffea", "name": "Under-promise and over-deliver", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Good Enough Software.md", "message": "**Under-promise and over-deliver**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A team should work out what it can reasonably deliver, deliver that excellently, and let users be pleasantly surprised, rather than promising more than it can achieve."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_4f51cbfa0b", "name": "CUDA is NVIDIA's GPU platform", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/GPU Computing.md", "message": "**CUDA** — NVIDIA's parallel computing platform; lets you run code on the GPU.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "CUDA is NVIDIA's parallel computing platform that lets code run on the GPU."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_4412e3bffc", "name": "VRAM limits model size", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/GPU Computing.md", "message": "**VRAM** — Video RAM on the GPU, separate from system RAM. Limits model size.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "VRAM is video RAM on the GPU, separate from system RAM, and it limits the size of the model that fits."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_528a4f104f", "name": "fp16 halves memory per parameter", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/GPU Computing.md", "message": "**fp16 (half precision)** — 16-bit floating point, uses half the memory of fp32 with minimal accuracy loss. Rule of thumb: 2 bytes per parameter.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "fp16 (half precision) is 16-bit floating point that uses half the memory of fp32 with minimal accuracy loss, giving a rule of thumb of 2 bytes per parameter."}
