{"aliases": ["The total gradient is the **sum** of all path contributions — this is the multivariable chain rule."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_a85a9ab7ed", "name": "Gradient is sum over all paths", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient Accumulation (+=).md", "message": "When a variable is used in multiple operations, it receives gradient contributions from **each** path through the computational graph.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When a variable is used in multiple operations it receives a gradient contribution from each path through the computational graph, and the total gradient is the sum of all path contributions, which is the multivariable chain rule."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_b8f72f72e1", "name": "Backward functions accumulate with +=", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient Accumulation (+=).md", "message": "This is why all backward functions use `+=` instead of `=` / \"If you use `=`, the last operation to run overwrites all previous contributions.\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Backward functions in an autograd engine must accumulate gradients with += rather than =, because = lets the last operation to run overwrite all previous contributions."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_93acc0a4b0", "name": "Worked example of accumulating gradients", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient Accumulation (+=).md", "message": "**Example:** `x = Value(3.0); y = x + x; z = y * x` ... Total: 6 + 6 = 12", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For x = 3, y = x + x, and z = y * x, x reaches z through two paths contributing 6 (directly, as y) and 6 (through y, as x times dy/dx = 3*2), so the total gradient dz/dx is 12."}
{"aliases": ["In the neural approach, the equivalent is L2 regularization"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_83f6cb85d7", "name": "L2 regularization as neural smoothing", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/L2 Regularization as Smoothing.md", "message": "In the counting approach, add-1 smoothing prevents zero probabilities. In the neural approach, the equivalent is L2 regularization", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "L2 regularization of a softmax classifier plays the role that add-1 smoothing plays in the counting approach: both pull the predicted distribution toward uniform."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_fcb46ed9ec", "name": "Zero weights give a uniform softmax", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/L2 Regularization as Smoothing.md", "message": "**The mechanism:** When `W = 0`, logits = 0, exp(0) = 1 for every class", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When all weights W are zero, every logit is 0, exp(0) = 1 for every class, and softmax returns a uniform distribution — the smoothest distribution possible."}
{"aliases": ["reg_loss = 0.01 * (W**2).mean()"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_228f630ee9", "name": "L2 penalty pulls weights toward zero", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/L2 Regularization as Smoothing.md", "message": "loss = negative_log_likelihood + reg_loss", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/L2 Regularization as Smoothing.md", "message": "The regularization strength controls how hard weights are pulled toward zero, just like the number of fake counts controls smoothness.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "L2 regularization adds a penalty such as 0.01 * (W**2).mean() to the negative log-likelihood, penalizing large weights; the regularization strength controls how hard the weights are pulled toward zero, just as the number of fake counts controls smoothness."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_2d86e5a086", "name": "Smoothing reserves mass for unseen events", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/L2 Regularization as Smoothing.md", "message": "**Why it matters:** Smoothing/regularization prevents overconfidence", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Smoothing and regularization prevent overconfidence by forcing the model to reserve probability mass for unseen events; without smoothing, a single unseen bigram makes the loss infinite."}
{"aliases": ["soft = (1−ε)·one-hot + ε/K", "the ε/K smear over *every* class"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_c428cb4366", "name": "Label smoothing soft-target mixture", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Label Smoothing.md", "message": "## The mixture", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For K classes and true class c, label smoothing replaces the one-hot target with a soft target of (1 - epsilon) + epsilon/K at c and epsilon/K at every class j != c."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_d41b6ded34", "name": "Smoothed loss splits into CE plus uniform entropy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Label Smoothing.md", "message": "## The loss, regrouped", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Cross-entropy against the label-smoothed target expands to L = (1 - epsilon)*CE(hard, p) + epsilon*H_uniform(p) with H_uniform(p) = -sum_c (1/K)*log p_c, so the per-class coefficient epsilon/K lives in the second, anti-overconfidence term."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_c307d636d9", "name": "One-hot targets demand certainty", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Label Smoothing.md", "message": "A one-hot target wants p_true = 1, i.e. −log p_true = 0, which only a runaway logit gap reaches in the limit", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A one-hot target demands p_true = 1, i.e. -log p_true = 0, which only a runaway logit gap reaches in the limit, so a model trained on one-hot targets is pushed into chasing certainty with ever-larger logits."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_89b934da1d", "name": "Label smoothing makes a finite logit gap optimal", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Label Smoothing.md", "message": "Smoothing lowers the demand to (1−ε) and puts a floor of ε/K under every other class, so a *finite* logit gap is optimal and the push stops.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Label smoothing lowers the demand on the true class to (1 - epsilon) and puts a floor of epsilon/K under every other class, so a finite logit gap is optimal and the push toward larger logits stops."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_f767282ad3", "name": "Count smoothing vs target smoothing differ in object", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Label Smoothing.md", "message": "## Nearest neighbour", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Laplace/add-1 smoothing smooths counts and estimates to avoid zero probabilities, whereas label smoothing smooths the training target distribution of a classifier or language-model head; the motive of removing an extreme demand is shared but the object differs."}
{"aliases": ["$$P(\\text{word} | \\text{class}) = \\frac{\\text{count}(\\text{word}, \\text{class}) + 1}{\\text{total words in class} + \\text{vocabulary size}}$$"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_82194746c9", "name": "Laplace smoothing formula", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Laplace Smoothing.md", "message": "## The Fix", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Laplace (add-1) smoothing estimates P(word|class) = (count(word, class) + 1) / (total words in class + vocabulary size)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_9af1987f46", "name": "Laplace denominator grows by vocabulary size", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Laplace Smoothing.md", "message": "## Why the Denominator Grows by Vocabulary Size", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Laplace smoothing adds the vocabulary size V to the denominator because the +1 is added to every word in the vocabulary; without that adjustment the word probabilities would sum to more than 1 and break the probability axioms."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_b0cabf1d3d", "name": "Laplace smoothing removes zero probabilities", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Laplace Smoothing.md", "message": "An unseen word gets `1/250 = 0.004` — small, but not zero. That nonzero floor is the whole point.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Laplace Smoothing.md", "message": "If a word never appeared during training → zero frequency → kills the entire probability product (log(0) is undefined).", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Under Laplace smoothing an unseen word receives a small nonzero probability (1/250 in the worked example) instead of zero, whereas an unsmoothed zero frequency makes log(0) undefined and kills the entire probability product."}
{"aliases": ["It is the single most important hyperparameter in optimization."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_1e873a9808", "name": "Learning rate in the gradient-descent update", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning Rate.md", "message": "The scalar that controls how far each gradient-descent step moves", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The learning rate eta is the scalar controlling how far each gradient-descent step moves, w <- w - eta*grad L(w), and is the single most important hyperparameter in optimization."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_9a12c463c0", "name": "Learning-rate too large vs too small", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning Rate.md", "message": "## The tradeoff", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A learning rate that is too large overshoots the valley, bouncing between walls or diverging entirely, while a learning rate that is too small crawls toward the answer over thousands of unnecessary steps."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_008f1e91b0", "name": "Optimizer-specific starting learning rates", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning Rate.md", "message": "## Starting points (Rohit rule of thumb)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning Rate.md", "message": "**No formula exists** — the right value is found by experiment.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "No formula gives the correct learning rate — the value is found by experiment — so optimizers start from rules of thumb: Adam at 0.001 and SGD with momentum (0.9) at 0.01."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_f253b0d70f", "name": "Learning rate and optimizer are coupled", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning Rate.md", "message": "## lr and optimizer choice (CP3, 2026-09-08)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The right learning rate depends on the optimizer, so optimizer choice and learning-rate choice are coupled rather than independent; in the Rosenbrock comparison Adam at lr = 0.001 was effective without tuning while vanilla gradient descent at lr = 0.0005 never converged, failing by zigzag rather than overshoot."}
{"aliases": ["\\(0.999^{1000} \\approx 0.368\\)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_8891ba212d", "name": "Decay factor sets a characteristic timescale", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning Rate.md", "message": "**Timescale insight (2026-09-10):**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning-rate Schedules (four types).md", "message": "## The timescale insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A decay factor of 0.999 looks gentle but 0.999^1000 is about 0.368, so that factor halves the learning rate every ~700 steps; choosing the decay factor is choosing this characteristic timescale rather than a freebie."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_b29559b875", "name": "Step decay schedule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning-rate Schedules (four types).md", "message": "Step decay | \\(\\eta \\leftarrow \\eta \\cdot \\text{factor}\\) every \\(N\\) epochs", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Step decay multiplies the learning rate by a fixed factor every N epochs, so the rate drops in jumps and its curve is a staircase."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_ab5709800a", "name": "Exponential decay schedule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning-rate Schedules (four types).md", "message": "Keeps shrinking toward **0 — no floor**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning-rate Schedules (four types).md", "message": "Exponential never settles at any nonzero value.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Exponential decay sets eta = eta_0 * decay^t, which keeps shrinking toward 0 with no floor, so the rate never settles at any nonzero value."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_d9153d6306", "name": "Cosine annealing lands on a floor", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning-rate Schedules (four types).md", "message": "**Only cosine has a floor parameter**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Cosine annealing sets eta = eta_min + (1/2)(eta_max - eta_min)(1 + cos(pi*t/T)); at t = T the cosine term is -1, leaving exactly eta = eta_min, so the rate lands on the chosen nonzero floor and stays there."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_460fe2563d", "name": "Warmup is the only schedule that ramps up", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning-rate Schedules (four types).md", "message": "**Only warmup ramps up** — it's the answer whenever the stem mentions early instability (large models blow up if started large).", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Warmup-then-decay is the only common schedule that ramps the learning rate up, linearly, before decaying, and it is used for large models because they blow up if started at a large rate."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_a641d98230", "name": "Predefined schedules cannot adapt per parameter", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Learning-rate Schedules (four types).md", "message": "## Why schedules aren't enough (Ruder's critique)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Pre-defined learning-rate schedules are a blunt instrument: the curve is fixed in advance before seeing how training unfolds, so it cannot adapt to a dataset's characteristics, and every weight is scaled the same regardless of feature frequency or gradient size — the gap that per-parameter adaptivity such as Adam fills."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_02d6162a53", "name": "less is a terminal pager", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/less (Pager).md", "message": "**`less`** is a terminal **pager**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "less is a terminal pager that displays a text file or command output one screenful at a time so long content waits for the reader; it quits with q, navigates with arrows/PageUp/PageDown, and searches within the text with /pattern."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_ccecfa8e2c", "name": "less reads input lazily", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/less (Pager).md", "message": "## Why `less` beats `more` and text editors", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "less does not load the entire file into memory before opening it, reading lazily only the portion being viewed, so less opens instantly at any file size on massive log files, unlike the older more utility and unlike text editors that must slurp and parse the whole document first."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_60c1e7ff66", "name": "Lexer definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Lexer.md", "message": "The **lexical analyzer** (lexer/scanner) is the first stage", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A lexer (lexical analyzer or scanner) is the first stage of a compiler or interpreter pipeline: it walks raw source code character by character and groups the characters into meaningful chunks called tokens."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_b5f25792c0", "name": "Lexer applies pattern-matching rules left to right", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Lexer.md", "message": "## How It Works", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The lexer reads the source string left to right and applies pattern-matching rules, typically regular expressions, to recognize lexical units such as keywords, identifiers, numbers, operators, and punctuation."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_c119886589", "name": "Lexer keeps both lexeme and literal value", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Lexer.md", "message": "it does two things: 1. Keeps the raw text string (`\"42\"` as the lexeme) 2. Converts it into a live binary integer (`42`) stored as the literal value", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For a numeric literal such as 42 the lexer keeps the raw text string \"42\" as the lexeme and also converts it into a live binary integer 42 stored as the literal value."}
{"aliases": ["How probable the evidence is if the hypothesis is true — restricted to the hypothesis class."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_8bf6be3af3", "name": "Likelihood is P(E|H)", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Likelihood.md", "message": "## Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The likelihood is P(E|H): how probable the evidence is if the hypothesis is true, restricted to the hypothesis class."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_d9027099f0", "name": "Likelihood is not the posterior", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Likelihood.md", "message": "## Key Distinction", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Likelihood is P(E|H), not P(H|E): it is the probability of the data given the hypothesis, not the probability of the hypothesis given the data."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_d18b1b13be", "name": "Disease-test likelihoods", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Likelihood.md", "message": "## In the Disease Example", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In the disease example, P(positive|disease) = 0.99 is the likelihood (the test catches the disease) and P(positive|healthy) = 0.01 is the evidence under the negated hypothesis, i.e. the false-positive rate."}
{"aliases": ["Every AI model is just matrix math wearing a fancy hat."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_c201e79e01", "name": "Matrices are transformations", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Linear Algebra Intuition.md", "message": "### Matrices Are Transformations", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A matrix transforms one vector into another — rotating, scaling, stretching, or projecting it — and in AI the neural-network weights, attention scores, and embeddings are such matrices, so matrices are the model."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_b711cb9831", "name": "Dot product measures similarity", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Linear Algebra Intuition.md", "message": "### Dot Product (Similarity Measure)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The dot product a . b = sum of a_i * b_i measures similarity: positive when the vectors point in the same direction, zero when perpendicular (unrelated), and negative when opposite (dissimilar), which is how search engines, recommendation systems, and RAG retrieve vectors with high dot products."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_612b6768ae", "name": "Rank counts independent columns and rows", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Linear Algebra Intuition.md", "message": "**Rank** = number of linearly independent columns = number of linearly independent rows.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The rank of a matrix equals its number of linearly independent columns, which equals its number of linearly independent rows; full rank gives a unique least-squares solution in a well-conditioned model, while rank deficiency means redundant features and infinitely many weight solutions."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_ec6782042a", "name": "Projection formula and orthogonal residual", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Linear Algebra Intuition.md", "message": "### Projection", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Linear Algebra Intuition.md", "message": "This orthogonal decomposition is the foundation of least-squares fitting.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Projecting vector a onto vector b gives (a . b)/(b . b) times b, and the residual a minus that projection is perpendicular to b; this orthogonal decomposition is the foundation of least-squares fitting and underlies linear regression, PCA, and attention."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_ff2544ac91", "name": "LoRA uses a low-rank weight update", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Linear Algebra Intuition.md", "message": "**LoRA:** Fine-tunes LLMs by decomposing weight updates into low-rank matrices.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "LoRA fine-tunes a large language model by decomposing its weight update into low-rank matrices: two matrices of size 4096x16 and 16x4096 (131K parameters) instead of a 4096x4096 update (16M parameters), the rank-16 constraint assuming the update lives in a 16-dimensional subspace."}
{"aliases": ["Each operation in an autograd engine only needs to know its own local derivative rule."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_1a9179669c", "name": "Autograd operations need only local derivatives", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Local Autograd Derivative Rules.md", "message": "Each operation in an autograd engine only needs to know its own local derivative rule. The graph handles composing them via the chain rule.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Each operation in an autograd engine only needs to know its own local derivative rule; the computational graph composes those local rules via the chain rule."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_f330849513", "name": "Local derivatives of addition and multiplication", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Local Autograd Derivative Rules.md", "message": "| a + b | a + b | 1 | 1 |", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Local Autograd Derivative Rules.md", "message": "| a · b | a × b | b | a |", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For the primitive operations, addition has local derivatives out/a = 1 and out/b = 1, multiplication has out/a = b and out/b = a, and x^n has derivative n*x^(n-1)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_01093ce80a", "name": "Local derivatives of elementwise functions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Local Autograd Derivative Rules.md", "message": "| tanh(x) | tanh(x) | 1 − tanh²(x) | — |", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The local derivatives of the standard elementwise functions are: tanh(x) gives 1 - tanh^2(x), ReLU(x) gives 1 if x > 0 else 0, exp(x) gives exp(x), and ln(x) gives 1/x."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:54Z", "id": "n_baa37f43a5", "name": "Subtraction and division compose from primitives", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Local Autograd Derivative Rules.md", "message": "**Composition pattern:** Subtraction and division don't need custom backward rules", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Subtraction and division need no custom backward rules because they compose from primitives, a - b = a + (-1 * b) and a / b = a * b^(-1); each backward pass is self-contained, needing only its own input values and the upstream gradient out.grad, and this decentralized design is why autograd engines scale."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_afe58fbc49", "name": "Jacobian determinant definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian determinant.md", "message": "The Jacobian determinant is the determinant of a Jacobian matrix.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Jacobian determinant is the determinant of a Jacobian matrix."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_c3a64f4ce8", "name": "Area scaling by the Jacobian determinant", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian determinant.md", "message": "Meaning", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For a 2D transformation, the magnitude of the Jacobian determinant measures how area scales near a point: areas expand where |det J| > 1 and shrink where |det J| < 1."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_2ddd8abe49", "name": "Negative Jacobian determinant flips orientation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian determinant.md", "message": "If \\(\\det J < 0\\), the map also flips orientation.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "If the Jacobian determinant is negative, the map also flips orientation."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_9973f9c498", "name": "Jacobian determinant as change-of-variables factor", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian determinant.md", "message": "Why it matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Jacobian determinant is the correction factor that appears when changing variables in multiple integrals."}
{"aliases": ["matrix of all first-order partial derivatives"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_a59d342c92", "name": "Jacobian matrix of first-order partial derivatives", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian matrix.md", "message": "The Jacobian matrix is the matrix of all first-order partial derivatives of a multivariable function.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Jacobian matrix of a multivariable function is the matrix of all its first-order partial derivatives."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_9aff412bb9", "name": "Shape and entries of the Jacobian", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian matrix.md", "message": "If \\(f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m\\), then the Jacobian is an \\(m \\times n\\) matrix.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For a function f from R^n to R^m, the Jacobian is an m-by-n matrix whose entry (i, j) is the partial derivative of the i-th output component with respect to the j-th input coordinate."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_6c8d250422", "name": "Rows and columns of the Jacobian", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian matrix.md", "message": "What the entries mean", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Each column of the Jacobian tells how the output changes when one input coordinate changes a little, and each row tells how one output component responds to the input variables."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_469a409123", "name": "Jacobian as best local linear approximation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian matrix.md", "message": "Local interpretation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "At a specific point, the Jacobian is the best linear approximation to the function near that point, so a tiny input step is mapped to an output step by that matrix."}
{"aliases": ["JVP"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_87986567e9", "name": "Jacobian-vector product definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian-vector product.md", "message": "A Jacobian-vector product, or JVP, is the product \\(J_f(x) r\\)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Jacobian-vector product (JVP) is the product J_f(x) r, the Jacobian of f at x applied to a direction vector r."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_230e078387", "name": "JVP avoids forming the full Jacobian", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian-vector product.md", "message": "Why it matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A JVP gives the effect of one chosen input direction without forming the full Jacobian, which makes it practical when the input dimension is large but only a few directions are needed."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_80a899665a", "name": "JVP is computed by forward-mode autodiff", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian-vector product.md", "message": "It is the quantity computed by forward-mode automatic differentiation.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Jacobian-vector product is the quantity computed by forward-mode automatic differentiation."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_15cceef7d7", "name": "JVP is a directional derivative", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian-vector product.md", "message": "Connection to directional derivatives", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Jacobian-vector product is the same thing as a directional derivative written in matrix form."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_f6f3ea639a", "name": "JVP with a basis vector is a partial derivative", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jacobian-vector product.md", "message": "If \\(r\\) is a coordinate basis vector, the JVP reduces to a partial derivative.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "If the direction vector r is a coordinate basis vector, the Jacobian-vector product reduces to a partial derivative."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_5a52d00ac7", "name": "Java class body allows only declarations", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Java Class Body Rules.md", "message": "Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Inside the main body of a Java class only declarations of variables and methods are allowed; action statements such as loops, prints, method calls, and assignments outside declarations are not."}
{"aliases": ["Declarations are allowed; actions must be inside a \"box\" (method, constructor, or static block)."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_80e62f4f2a", "name": "Java actions must sit in a box", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Java Class Body Rules.md", "message": "The Golden Rule", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In Java, action statements must sit inside a box: a method, a constructor, or a static initializer block."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_9db13a904e", "name": "Class body rule explains Java static blocks", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Java Class Body Rules.md", "message": "Why This Matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The declaration-only rule for the Java class body is the root reason Java needs static {} blocks for complex static initialization."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_f8a4250c6b", "name": "Static initializer block runs at class load", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Java Static Initializer Block.md", "message": "Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Java static {} block runs automatically when the JVM loads the class, before any main code executes, and provides a place for multi-step initialization actions."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_d990aee664", "name": "Filling a static field requires action statements", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Java Static Initializer Block.md", "message": "The Problem It Solves", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Declaring a static Java variable is a declaration that the class body allows, but filling it with data via .put() or .add() is an action statement, which the open class body forbids."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_5ac4c58822", "name": "When a static initializer block is overkill", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Java Static Initializer Block.md", "message": "It's overkill when", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Java static initializer block is overkill when the initialization fits on a single line, when Java 9+ collection factories such as Map.of(), List.of(), and Set.of() can be used, or when the field is not static and a constructor is the right place instead."}
{"aliases": ["Just-In-Time compilation", "compiled to native machine code *at runtime*"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_43b2f3f7a6", "name": "JIT compilation definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/JIT Compilation.md", "message": "**JIT (Just-In-Time) compilation** is a technique where [[Bytecode]] is compiled to native machine code *at runtime*", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "JIT (just-in-time) compilation compiles bytecode to native machine code at runtime, while the program is running, rather than ahead of time."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_68361afe7f", "name": "JIT compiles hot paths on the fly", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/JIT Compilation.md", "message": "How it works", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A JIT compiler identifies frequently executed (hot) code paths and compiles those paths to native machine code on the fly, so subsequent calls use the optimized native code instead of interpretation."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_d6b8e969bd", "name": "JIT optimizes from observed runtime behaviour", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/JIT Compilation.md", "message": "Runtime optimization", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A JIT compiler can optimize using the actual runtime behaviour of the program rather than static analysis alone."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_41333836f2", "name": "Costs of JIT compilation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/JIT Compilation.md", "message": "Downsides", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "JIT compilation pays a warm-up period before peak performance, holds the original bytecode, the compilation framework, and the generated machine code in memory at once, and spends CPU cycles in compiler threads, which makes it wasteful for short-running scripts."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_c3b68021af", "name": "Some platforms forbid dynamic code generation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/JIT Compilation.md", "message": "Platform constraints", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Some systems forbid dynamic code generation: Apple's iOS restricts executable memory pages, which makes JIT compilers impossible for third-party apps."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_74b9588749", "name": "Joint probability from a contingency table", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities.md", "message": "Joint probability", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The joint probability P(X = x_i, Y = y_j) equals n_ij / N, where n_ij is the count in cell (i, j) and N is the grand total."}
{"aliases": ["marginalizing it out"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_96391381b5", "name": "Marginal probability by summing out", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities.md", "message": "Marginal probability", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The marginal probability of one variable is obtained by summing (or integrating) out the other variable; marginalization is the sum rule in action."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_a6b4219ddf", "name": "Conditional probability divides by the conditioning total", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities.md", "message": "Conditional probability", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The conditional probability P(Y = y_j | X = x_i) equals n_ij / c_i, dividing by the column total c_i rather than the grand total N because conditioning on X = x_i restricts the universe to the cases in that column."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_c12d857c4c", "name": "Conditional probabilities are direction-dependent", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities.md", "message": "Note that these two conditional probabilities are generally **not** equal", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "P(Y | X) and P(X | Y) are generally not equal; they coincide only in special cases, and when both equal their respective marginals the two variables are independent."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_b743657442", "name": "Product rule for joint probability", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities.md", "message": "Relationship to probability rules", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The product rule states P(X, Y) = P(Y | X) P(X), which rearranges to P(Y | X) = P(X, Y) / P(X)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_7f824fe6be", "name": "Jupyter cells share one kernel", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jupyter Notebook Workflow.md", "message": "All cells share the same kernel — variables persist between cells.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "All cells in a Jupyter notebook share the same background Python kernel process, so variables persist between cells."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_13239c91e3", "name": "Out-of-order execution breaks notebooks", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jupyter Notebook Workflow.md", "message": "Out-of-order execution", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Out-of-order cell execution and hidden state left by deleted cells make a notebook fail when another person runs it top-to-bottom, which is why the kernel should be restarted and all cells run in order before sharing."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_2e2c4e7d0b", "name": "Notebooks for exploration, scripts for shipping", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Jupyter Notebook Workflow.md", "message": "The rule: explore in notebooks, ship in scripts.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Jupyter notebooks suit exploring a dataset, prototyping a model, visualizing results, and course exercises, while scripts suit training pipelines, reusable utilities, production code, and code that runs on a schedule."}
{"aliases": ["KL(Q∥P) = H(P,Q) − H(Q) = CE − H(data)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_2298e2c48e", "name": "KL divergence equals cross-entropy minus entropy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/KL Divergence.md", "message": "Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "KL divergence KL(Q∥P) = sum over events x of q(x) log2(q(x)/p(x)) equals the cross-entropy H(P,Q) of the model P against the truth Q minus the truth's own entropy H(Q)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_8087d22e07", "name": "KL divergence is non-negative", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/KL Divergence.md", "message": "Two properties that define it", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "KL(Q∥P) is at least 0 by Gibbs' inequality, with equality if and only if P = Q on every event, and the non-negativity follows from Jensen's inequality because the logarithm is concave."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_404d5f3537", "name": "KL divergence is not symmetric", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/KL Divergence.md", "message": "Not symmetric", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "KL divergence is a directed gap and is not symmetric: KL(Q∥P) and KL(P∥Q) are generally different numbers, so the direction must be named before quoting a value."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_18971692a6", "name": "Zero model probability makes KL infinite", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/KL Divergence.md", "message": "Infinity, and why smoothing exists", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "If an event has q(x) > 0 while the model assigns p(x) = 0, that event's term is infinite, so cross-entropy and KL become infinite while the data's own entropy H(Q) stays finite."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:19:59Z", "id": "n_d5dd89b912", "name": "Mutual information is a KL divergence", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/KL Divergence.md", "message": "Mutual information is a KL divergence", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Mutual information equals KL(p(x,y) ∥ p(x)p(y)), the joint distribution measured against the product of its own marginals, which is symmetric and zero exactly when X and Y are independent."}
{"aliases": ["I(X;Y) = H(X) − H(X|Y)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_d977a39123", "name": "Mutual information as deleted uncertainty", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mutual Information.md", "message": "The defining idea: I(X;Y) = H(X) − H(X|Y)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Mutual information I(X;Y) is the entropy of X minus the conditional entropy of X given Y, i.e. the amount of uncertainty about X that knowing Y removes."}
{"aliases": ["I(X;Y) = KL( p(x,y) ‖ p(x)·p(y) )"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_262e852617", "name": "Mutual information as a KL divergence", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mutual Information.md", "message": "Four equivalent forms, item 4: \"KL form\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mutual Information.md", "message": "The KL connection, walked: \"mutual information is KL divergence with the independence claim plugged in as the model\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Mutual information equals the KL divergence KL(p(x,y) ‖ p(x)·p(y)) of the joint distribution against the product of its own marginals, so its two arguments are distributions over the same cells rather than the variables X and Y."}
{"aliases": ["I(X;Y) = 0 iff X and Y are independent"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_b24d5cbc6e", "name": "MI vanishes exactly at independence", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mutual Information.md", "message": "Properties: \"I(X;Y) = 0 iff X and Y are independent\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mutual Information.md", "message": "Contrast with independence: joint [[0.25, 0.25], [0.25, 0.25]]", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Mutual information is zero exactly when the two variables are independent, because then every cell equals p(x)·p(y) and every log-ratio in the surprise form is zero."}
{"aliases": ["I(X;Y) ≥ 0"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_8a8cc03448", "name": "Mutual information is non-negative", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mutual Information.md", "message": "Properties: \"I(X;Y) ≥ 0 — read off form 4 with KL ≥ 0 (Gibbs' inequality)\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mutual Information.md", "message": "Why MI can never be negative — and why there is no absolute value", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Mutual information is never negative for any joint distribution, inheriting I(X;Y) ≥ 0 from the non-negativity of KL divergence (Gibbs' inequality)."}
{"aliases": ["I(X;Y) ≤ min(H(X), H(Y))"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_e3dda498d6", "name": "MI bounded by the smaller entropy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mutual Information.md", "message": "Properties: \"I(X;Y) ≤ min(H(X), H(Y))\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mutual Information.md", "message": "Why MI can never be negative: the anti-correlated case Y = 1 − X gives 1 bit", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Mutual information is bounded above by the smaller of the two entropies, I(X;Y) ≤ min(H(X), H(Y)), and for two binary variables the bound of 1 bit is attained by a bijection such as Y = 1 − X."}
{"aliases": ["P(class | f1, ..., fn) = P(class) · P(f1 | class) · ... · P(fn | class) / P(features)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_9c22b3f21e", "name": "Naive Bayes scoring formula", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Naive Bayes.md", "message": "Formula: \"Multiply the likelihoods of all features together... compare numerators only (argmax over classes)\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Naive Bayes scores each class as the class prior multiplied by the product of the per-feature likelihoods given that class, and because the feature denominator is identical for every class it can be dropped when taking the argmax."}
{"aliases": ["Naive Bayes assumes every word/feature is conditionally independent given the class"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_af3b92dd1b", "name": "Naive Bayes conditional independence assumption", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Naive Bayes.md", "message": "The \"Naive\" Part", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Naive Bayes assumes that every feature is conditionally independent of the others given the class label."}
{"aliases": ["the classifier only needs to rank classes correctly, not produce calibrated probabilities"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_51fd842099", "name": "Naive Bayes works because ranking suffices", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Naive Bayes.md", "message": "Why It Still Works", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Naive Bayes remains useful despite its false independence assumption because classification only requires ranking classes correctly, not producing calibrated probabilities."}
{"aliases": ["The independence assumption makes Naive Bayes overconfident"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_01bd088722", "name": "Naive Bayes overconfidence", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Naive Bayes.md", "message": "Calibration vs Ranking", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Treating correlated features as independent evidence multiplies their effects and pushes Naive Bayes probabilities toward 0 or 1, so its outputs should not be trusted for calibrated thresholds in cost-sensitive decisions such as medical diagnosis, credit risk or insurance pricing."}
{"aliases": ["score(class) = log P(class) + Σ log P(word|class)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_0abd5dbb85", "name": "Log-space Naive Bayes", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Naive Bayes.md", "message": "Log-Space Computation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Summing log-probabilities, score(class) = log P(class) + Σ log P(word|class), is mathematically equivalent to multiplying probabilities and avoids the floating-point underflow of multiplying hundreds of small factors."}
{"aliases": ["a^t = e^{ln(a)t}"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_8b78684d93", "name": "Expressing a base in terms of e", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Natural logarithm.md", "message": "\"The natural logarithm ln(a) is the conversion factor... a^t = e^{ln(a)t}\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Any exponential base a can be expressed in terms of base e through the identity a^t = e^{ln(a)·t}."}
{"aliases": ["\\frac{d}{dt} a^t = \\ln(a)\\,a^t"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_7f8d348e75", "name": "Derivative of an exponential base a", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Natural logarithm.md", "message": "\"That identity immediately gives the derivative rule\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The derivative of a^t with respect to t is ln(a) times a^t."}
{"aliases": ["ln(a) tells you how fast the exponential grows relative to itself"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_f6083d4e26", "name": "Meaning of ln(a)", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Natural logarithm.md", "message": "Interpretation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For an exponential a^t, ln(a) measures how fast the exponential grows relative to its own current value."}
{"aliases": ["loss = -ln(P(x))", "-ln(P(correct next token))"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_bd48bd638c", "name": "Negative log-likelihood of one prediction", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL).md", "message": "Overview and Formula", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Negative log-likelihood is the standard loss for language models and for a single prediction is loss = −ln P(x), where P(x) is the model's probability of the correct next token; the loss is zero for P(x) = 1 and grows without bound as P(x) approaches 0."}
{"aliases": ["Total Loss = (1/N) × Σ -ln(P(x_i))"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_0e24f5d69e", "name": "Dataset NLL is the mean loss", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL).md", "message": "Formula: \"Averaged across all N examples in the dataset\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Across a dataset the total loss is the mean of the per-example negative log-likelihoods, Total Loss = (1/N) × Σ −ln(P(x_i)), and models with lower NLL assign higher probability to the actual data."}
{"aliases": ["Maximizing the product of probabilities (likelihood) = minimizing the average negative log probability"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_6745bb71e1", "name": "Log converts likelihood maximization into NLL minimization", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL).md", "message": "Why Negative Log?", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because the logarithm turns a product of probabilities into a sum, maximizing the likelihood product is the same as minimizing the average negative log probability."}
{"aliases": ["If any example gets P(x) = 0, its loss is +∞"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_d4252808a3", "name": "Zero-probability example makes NLL infinite", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL).md", "message": "The Zero Problem", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "If any single example is assigned probability zero its negative log-likelihood is +infinity, which makes the entire dataset average infinite regardless of all other predictions, so model smoothing such as add-1 is essential."}
{"aliases": ["Forward pass — compute predictions and loss; Backward pass; Parameter update; Zero gradients"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_a4d88bb8c0", "name": "Four-step neural network training loop", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Neural Network Training Loop.md", "message": "\"The complete cycle that every deep learning framework implements\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Every deep learning framework implements the same training loop: a forward pass computing predictions and loss, a backward pass computing gradients for all parameters by autodiff, a parameter update step, and zeroing all gradients before the next iteration."}
{"aliases": ["θ ← θ - η·∇θL(θ)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_d4bc8b43d7", "name": "Gradient-descent update in the loop", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Neural Network Training Loop.md", "message": "\"In equation form: θ ← θ - η·∇θL(θ)\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The parameter update step of the training loop is θ ← θ − η·∇θL(θ), repeated thousands of times over batches of data."}
{"aliases": ["this loop IS training"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_929a9e744e", "name": "The loop is training", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Neural Network Training Loop.md", "message": "Key insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The forward/backward/update/zero cycle is itself what training a neural network consists of; architectures, loss functions and optimizers are variations built on that foundation."}
{"aliases": ["\\frac{d\\Delta T}{dt} = -k\\Delta T"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_7b3cf6bb31", "name": "Newton's law of cooling", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Newtons law of cooling.md", "message": "\"Newtons law of cooling says...\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate of temperature change is proportional to the current temperature difference from the surroundings, dΔT/dt = −kΔT."}
{"aliases": ["ΔT(t) = e^{-kt}"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_4d0b3a8ed9", "name": "Cooling follows exponential decay", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Newtons law of cooling.md", "message": "\"Its solution is exponential decay\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The solution of Newton's law of cooling is exponential decay of the temperature difference, ΔT(t) = e^{−kt}."}
{"aliases": ["Writing the solution with base e makes the proportionality constant appear directly in the exponent"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_1e5bf2e046", "name": "Base e puts the constant in the exponent", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Newtons law of cooling.md", "message": "Why it connects here", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Writing the solution of a differential equation with base e makes the proportionality constant appear directly in the exponent."}
{"aliases": ["F.one_hot(xs, num_classes=27).float()"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_3dc0bc1046", "name": "One-hot encoding definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/One-Hot Encoding.md", "message": "\"One-hot encoding converts each integer index into a binary vector of length num_classes\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "One-hot encoding converts a categorical integer index into a binary vector of length num_classes that is all zeros except for a single 1 at that index."}
{"aliases": ["Multiplying an integer by a weight implies ordinal relationships"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_4cf72b10a2", "name": "Integers imply false ordinal relations", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/One-Hot Encoding.md", "message": "Why not use the integer directly?", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Multiplying an integer index by a weight matrix implies ordinal relationships between categories, such as 'a' being closer to 'b' than to 'y', which character indices do not have."}
{"aliases": ["One-hot vectors are 27-dimensional but carry only 1 bit of information"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_b285703ece", "name": "One-hot vectors are sparse and low-information", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/One-Hot Encoding.md", "message": "Key limitation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A one-hot vector is high-dimensional but carries only one bit of information, which is why dense embeddings learned from data replace one-hots in larger models."}
{"aliases": ["Start with Adam — η = 0.001"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_a499a1b098", "name": "Adam as the default optimizer", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizer Selection (Rohit heuristic).md", "message": "Item 1: \"Start with Adam\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizers (SGD, Adam, AdamW).md", "message": "Practical Rule: \"Start with Adam (lr=0.001)\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Adam with learning rate 0.001 is the default optimizer choice because it works for most problems without tuning."}
{"aliases": ["Switch to SGD with momentum — η = 0.01, momentum 0.9"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_db4cd4832d", "name": "SGD with momentum for best final accuracy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizer Selection (Rohit heuristic).md", "message": "\"Why \\\"switch to SGD for best accuracy\\\" isn't a contradiction\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizers (SGD, Adam, AdamW).md", "message": "Practical Rule: \"its noise lands in flat minima that generalize better\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "SGD with momentum (learning rate 0.01, momentum 0.9) is the choice when the best final accuracy matters: Adam's aggressive per-parameter adaptivity can settle into sharp minima that generalize poorly, while SGD's noise keeps it out of those minima and lands in flat ones."}
{"aliases": ["Adam with decoupled weight decay"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_eaf540d636", "name": "AdamW for transformers", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizer Selection (Rohit heuristic).md", "message": "Item 3: \"Use AdamW ... for transformers\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizers (SGD, Adam, AdamW).md", "message": "Table row AdamW: \"Transformers, modern architectures\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "AdamW, which is Adam with decoupled weight decay, is the optimizer choice for transformers and modern architectures."}
{"aliases": ["always use a learning-rate schedule for runs longer than a few epochs"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_9d2a07a385", "name": "Always schedule the learning rate", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizer Selection (Rohit heuristic).md", "message": "Standing rule", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizers (SGD, Adam, AdamW).md", "message": "Practical Rule: \"Always schedule runs longer than a few epochs\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Any training run longer than a few epochs should always use a learning-rate schedule."}
{"aliases": ["a toy-race ranking is hyperparameter-dependent, not a general result"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_c9a8446747", "name": "Toy optimizer races do not generalize", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizer Selection (Rohit heuristic).md", "message": "The race was an artifact", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Optimizer rankings from a toy race such as the Rosenbrock function are hyperparameter-dependent and should not be generalized to real training, because the toy is deterministic, two-dimensional, ill-conditioned and free of noise, sparsity and sharp minima."}
{"aliases": ["weight -= lr × gradient"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_dd46bcbf2f", "name": "Plain SGD update rule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizers (SGD, Adam, AdamW).md", "message": "Table row SGD", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Plain SGD updates each weight as weight -= lr × gradient, the simplest optimizer, giving maximum control but requiring well-tuned schedules."}
{"aliases": ["Builds velocity in consistent directions (like a ball rolling downhill)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_2af3d89091", "name": "SGD with momentum builds velocity", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizers (SGD, Adam, AdamW).md", "message": "Table row SGD + Momentum", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "SGD with momentum accumulates velocity in consistently directed gradients, behaving like a ball rolling downhill."}
{"aliases": ["Adaptive learning rate per parameter. Combines momentum + RMSprop."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_c0aa370e16", "name": "Adam adapts per-parameter learning rates", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizers (SGD, Adam, AdamW).md", "message": "Table row Adam", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Adam adapts the learning rate for each parameter individually by combining momentum with RMSprop."}
{"aliases": ["Adam with decoupled weight decay"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_84a35ffb16", "name": "AdamW decouples weight decay", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Optimizers (SGD, Adam, AdamW).md", "message": "Table row AdamW", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "AdamW is Adam with decoupled weight decay, which gives better regularization and makes it preferred over Adam for modern architectures."}
{"aliases": ["components should be independent, loosely coupled, and self-contained"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_c8bccd80ca", "name": "Orthogonality design principle", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Orthogonality.md", "message": "Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Orthogonality is a design principle requiring components to be independent, loosely coupled and self-contained, so that modifying one component does not ripple into others."}
{"aliases": ["Unit testing itself is a test of orthogonality"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_37f6c7b110", "name": "Unit testing tests orthogonality", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Orthogonality.md", "message": "Testing orthogonality", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Unit testing is itself a test of orthogonality: if getting a unit test to build and run requires importing a large percentage of the rest of the system, that module is not well decoupled."}
{"aliases": ["Unit (module-level) testing is easier to specify and perform than integration testing"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:07Z", "id": "n_3bc315da4e", "name": "Unit tests are easier than integration tests", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Orthogonality.md", "message": "\"These tests should run automatically as part of the regular build process\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Unit or module-level tests are easier to specify and perform than integration tests and should run automatically as part of the regular build process."}
{"aliases": ["Comparing autodiff gradients against numerical finite-difference approximations to verify the backward pass is correct"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_727861a657", "name": "Gradient checking by finite differences", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient Checking.md", "message": "Comparing autodiff gradients against numerical finite-difference approximations to verify the backward pass is correct.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Gradient checking verifies an autodiff backward pass by comparing its gradients against central finite differences f'(x) ≈ (f(x+h) - f(x-h))/(2h) with h ≈ 10⁻⁶, which has O(h²) accuracy."}
{"aliases": ["if the difference exceeds a tolerance (~10⁻⁵), there's a bug in a backward rule"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_152bae0e45", "name": "Mismatch signals broken backward rule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient Checking.md", "message": "if the difference exceeds a tolerance (~10⁻⁵), there's a bug in a backward rule", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In gradient checking, a difference between the autodiff gradient and the finite-difference estimate that exceeds a tolerance of about 10⁻⁵ indicates a bug in a backward rule."}
{"aliases": ["gradient checking catches silent bugs"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_6dcb10538e", "name": "Gradient checking catches silent bugs", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient Checking.md", "message": "Key insight: gradient checking catches silent bugs", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient Checking.md", "message": "When to use: every time you implement a new operation's backward pass", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Gradient checking catches silent bugs — wrong autodiff gradients that corrupt training without any visible error — and is therefore run whenever a new operation's backward pass is implemented."}
{"aliases": ["the negative gradient points downhill fastest in the local linear approximation"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_df74925776", "name": "Gradient descent as first-order method", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient descent.md", "message": "Gradient descent is a first-order optimization method that updates parameters by moving in the direction of steepest decrease predicted by the local linear model.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Gradient descent is a first-order optimization method that updates parameters by moving in the direction of steepest decrease predicted by the local linear model."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_b735af58c6", "name": "First-order Taylor approximation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient descent.md", "message": "the first-order Taylor approximation around x", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For a smooth scalar function f: Rⁿ → R, the first-order Taylor approximation around x is f(x + δ) ≈ f(x) + ∇f(x)ᵀδ."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_0da37125f7", "name": "Steepest decrease is opposite the gradient", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient descent.md", "message": "the answer points opposite the gradient", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Among small steps of fixed size, the direction giving the largest decrease under the first-order Taylor model points opposite the gradient, δ ∝ −∇f(x)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_7ad24e7ec3", "name": "Gradient descent update rule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient descent.md", "message": "The update rule", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Gradient descent updates every weight by w ← w − η∇L(w), computing the gradient over all weights and moving opposite it, scaled by the learning rate η."}
{"aliases": ["it repeatedly step-oscillates side to side across the valley walls"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_4cc6445697", "name": "Zigzag failure in narrow valleys", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient descent.md", "message": "zigzag (narrow curved valley, gradient points across the walls)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Gradient descent.md", "message": "Vanilla GD's characteristic failure in a narrow curved valley", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Vanilla gradient descent in a narrow curved valley repeatedly oscillates side to side across the valley walls, so consecutive steps mostly cancel and it makes almost no net progress along the trough toward the minimum."}
{"aliases": ["the matrix of second-order partial derivatives of a scalar-valued function"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_a85df5eb62", "name": "Hessian as matrix of second partials", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Hessian matrix.md", "message": "The Hessian matrix is the matrix of second-order partial derivatives of a scalar-valued function.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Hessian matrix of a scalar-valued function f: Rⁿ → R is the n × n matrix of second-order partial derivatives whose (i,j) entry is ∂²f/∂xᵢ∂xⱼ."}
{"aliases": ["the Hessian is symmetric by Clairaut's theorem"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_8a2a60a7c4", "name": "Hessian symmetry from Clairaut's theorem", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Hessian matrix.md", "message": "Symmetry", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "If f is twice continuously differentiable, its Hessian is symmetric by Clairaut's theorem, and the condition that matters is continuity of the mixed second partials, not merely existence of second derivatives."}
{"aliases": ["the Hessian is what turns the linear approximation into a local quadratic bowl"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_d7a8eb8fd7", "name": "Hessian as second-order Taylor term", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Hessian matrix.md", "message": "Taylor-series view", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Hessian matrix.md", "message": "The Hessian tells you how the gradient itself changes locally, which is the curvature information.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In the multivariate Taylor series the Hessian supplies the second-order term ½(x−x₀)ᵀH(x₀)(x−x₀), which turns the linear approximation into a local quadratic bowl and carries the local curvature of the gradient."}
{"aliases": ["This makes the step size adaptive to curvature"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_9f6cfe149d", "name": "Newton's method scales steps by curvature", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Hessian matrix.md", "message": "Why it matters for optimization", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Newton's method updates x ← x − H⁻¹∇f(x), using the inverse Hessian to make the step size adaptive to curvature, with larger steps in flatter directions and smaller steps in sharper directions."}
{"aliases": ["That becomes infeasible for large models"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_e4a832595f", "name": "Full Hessian cost is quadratic in n", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Hessian matrix.md", "message": "Why full Hessians are hard", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For n parameters the Hessian has n² entries, requiring O(n²) memory and O(n³) time to invert, which becomes infeasible for large models."}
{"aliases": ["Counting tables explode exponentially with context length."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_ce8832aefe", "name": "Counting tables grow exponentially with context", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Hidden Layers Generalize via Shared Weights.md", "message": "The problem: A trigram needs 27³ entries.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A counting (n-gram) table needs one entry per possible context, so it grows exponentially with context length: 27³ entries for a trigram over 27 symbols and 27¹⁰ ≈ 200 trillion for a 10-character context."}
{"aliases": ["Hidden State = tanh(Inputs · W₁ + b₁)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_c5b1ebc567", "name": "Hidden layer replaces the counting table", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Hidden Layers Generalize via Shared Weights.md", "message": "The neural fix: Hidden layers with shared weights", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A neural hidden layer replaces the per-sequence counting table with a fixed number of neurons computing Hidden State = tanh(Inputs · W₁ + b₁), so capacity grows linearly by adding neurons rather than exponentially with context length."}
{"aliases": ["Every neuron in the hidden layer fires on every input."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_4ff16dde71", "name": "Weight sharing yields reusable pattern detectors", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Hidden Layers Generalize via Shared Weights.md", "message": "Weight sharing", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Weight sharing makes each hidden neuron a reusable pattern detector: a rule such as \"if the last 4 characters are 'scop', the next character is probably a vowel\" is learned once and fires on every input, whereas a counting table keeps \"th → e\" and \"sh → e\" as separate entries with zero shared information."}
{"aliases": ["the hidden layer learns general pattern detectors"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_aa710a9883", "name": "Hidden detectors are frozen at test time", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Hidden Layers Generalize via Shared Weights.md", "message": "Why it works at test time", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Hidden-layer pattern detectors are learned during training and then frozen at test time, where they still fire on any input."}
{"aliases": ["A model that's never seen \"microscop\" can predict it correctly because it has seen the sub-pattern \"scop\" in \"telescop\""], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_cda9785c79", "name": "Shared weights generalize to unseen words", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Hidden Layers Generalize via Shared Weights.md", "message": "Why it works at test time", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because shared weights recognize sub-patterns regardless of surrounding context, a model that has never seen \"microscop\" can predict it correctly after seeing the sub-pattern \"scop\" in \"telescop\"."}
{"aliases": ["Explicitly details the steps to achieve a result", "States the desired result; the system figures out how."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_e25848d3f4", "name": "Imperative versus declarative paradigms", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Imperative and declarative programming.md", "message": "Two contrasting paradigms for expressing computation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Imperative programming explicitly details the steps that achieve a result, whereas declarative programming states the desired result and leaves the system to figure out how to produce it."}
{"aliases": ["CPUs execute instructions imperatively (fetch-decode-execute cycle)."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_f0c492f06d", "name": "Hardware mimicry favors imperative style", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Imperative and declarative programming.md", "message": "1. Hardware Mimicry", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Imperative programming remains the mainstream default partly because CPUs execute instructions imperatively in a fetch-decode-execute cycle and early programming languages were thin wrappers over machine code, making the imperative model the foundation of modern computing."}
{"aliases": ["Switching costs are enormous — even when declarative approaches would be better suited."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_251225efe4", "name": "Legacy ecosystem locks in imperative style", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Imperative and declarative programming.md", "message": "3. Legacy and Ecosystem", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Imperative programming also persists because decades of libraries, tools, frameworks, and education are built around it, so switching costs are enormous even where declarative approaches would be better suited."}
{"aliases": ["Declare UI as a function of state"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_b51836b574", "name": "Declarative tools specify desired state", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Imperative and declarative programming.md", "message": "Declarative Languages/Frameworks", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "SQL, React, SwiftUI, and Terraform/Kubernetes are declarative tools: SQL states what data is wanted rather than how to retrieve it, React declares a UI as a function of state, and Terraform/Kubernetes declare desired infrastructure state."}
{"aliases": ["declarative systems require sophisticated runtimes to resolve \"how\""], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_b2f5220964", "name": "Declarative systems need sophisticated runtimes", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Imperative and declarative programming.md", "message": "[[Runtime]] — declarative systems require sophisticated runtimes to resolve \"how\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Declarative systems require sophisticated runtimes to resolve the \"how\" that the program leaves unspecified."}
{"aliases": ["differentiating an equation that defines y implicitly rather than solving for y first"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_665b4cfacc", "name": "Implicit differentiation defined", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Implicit differentiation.md", "message": "Implicit differentiation is the process of differentiating an equation that defines y implicitly", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Implicit differentiation differentiates an equation that defines y implicitly, such as F(x,y) = 0 with y depending on x, instead of first solving for y."}
{"aliases": ["differentiating both sides gives"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_28dd2ba172", "name": "Differentiating F(x,y)=0 in x", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Implicit differentiation.md", "message": "and y depends on x, then differentiating both sides gives", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Differentiating F(x,y) = 0 with respect to x gives F_x + F_y·(dy/dx) = 0."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_9501108924", "name": "Implicit derivative equals minus F_x over F_y", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Implicit differentiation.md", "message": "when F_y ≠ 0", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Rearranging F_x + F_y·(dy/dx) = 0 gives dy/dx = −F_x/F_y, provided F_y ≠ 0."}
{"aliases": ["you set dF = 0 and solve for the slope"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_daf3808288", "name": "Implicit differentiation via total differential", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Implicit differentiation.md", "message": "How it connects to differentials", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The implicit-differentiation slope is the same as the total-differential view: from dF = F_x dx + F_y dy, one sets dF = 0 along a level curve F(x,y) = c and solves for the slope."}
{"aliases": ["the derivative of ln(x) appears naturally from implicit differentiation"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_38f9bb65d1", "name": "Derivative of the logarithm by implicit differentiation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Implicit differentiation.md", "message": "Example: the logarithm", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Differentiating the inverse relation e^y = x implicitly gives e^y·(dy/dx) = 1, and since e^y = x this yields d/dx ln(x) = 1/x."}
{"aliases": ["knowing the value of one gives you no information about the other"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_00153be013", "name": "Independence means no information", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Independence of random variables.md", "message": "Independence means the variables don't influence each other, not that they're mutually exclusive.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Two random variables X and Y are independent when knowing the value of one gives no information about the other, which is not the same as being unable to occur together."}
{"aliases": ["Conditional equals marginal"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_274b248dfa", "name": "Conditional equals marginal form", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Independence of random variables.md", "message": "Equivalent definitions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Independence is equivalent to the conditional-equals-marginal condition P(Y = yⱼ | X = xᵢ) = P(Y = yⱼ), and symmetrically P(X = xᵢ | Y = yⱼ) = P(X = xᵢ)."}
{"aliases": ["Joint factorizes", "The joint probability is the product of the marginals."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_a66bf3c964", "name": "Joint probability factorizes under independence", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Independence of random variables.md", "message": "Joint factorizes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Under independence the joint distribution factorizes as P(X = xᵢ, Y = yⱼ) = P(X = xᵢ)·P(Y = yⱼ), the product of the marginals."}
{"aliases": ["independence is binary and exact"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_d141e5e6fe", "name": "Theoretical independence is binary and exact", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Independence of random variables.md", "message": "Theoretical vs statistical independence", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Theoretical independence is binary and exact — P(X,Y) = P(X)·P(Y) holds for every combination or fails — whereas in finite samples even truly independent variables almost never satisfy the factorization exactly."}
{"aliases": ["Absence of evidence ≠ evidence of absence."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_60799d4869", "name": "Chi-square test and the meaning of p-values", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Independence of random variables.md", "message": "Statistical independence testing", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The chi-square test of independence compares observed frequencies to expected frequencies E_ij = r_j·c_i/N; a p-value below 0.05 rejects independence, but a large p-value does not prove independence, only that the data lack sufficient evidence of dependence."}
{"aliases": ["Log base 2 gives bits; natural log gives nats"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_1dc22fb239", "name": "Information content is negative log probability", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Information Content (Surprise).md", "message": "Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The information content (surprise) of an event is I(x) = −log p(x), where log base 2 gives bits and natural log gives nats, with 1 nat ≈ 1.4427 bits."}
{"aliases": ["The minus sign makes surprise non-negative."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_ced7158fed", "name": "Minus sign makes surprise non-negative", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Information Content (Surprise).md", "message": "Why the minus sign", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because probabilities lie in (0, 1] and log p ≤ 0, the minus sign in I(x) = −log p(x) makes surprise non-negative, large for unlikely events and exactly 0 at p = 1."}
{"aliases": ["−log p is the optimal code length for an event of probability p"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_78544667db", "name": "Surprise equals optimal code length", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Information Content (Surprise).md", "message": "Code-length view (Olah)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "−log p is the optimal code length for an event of probability p, so rare events need long codes and common events short ones, with p = 1/8 taking about 3 bits."}
{"aliases": ["Surprise is per-event"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_e946f54ba9", "name": "Surprise is per-event, entropy is averaged", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Information Content (Surprise).md", "message": "Nearest neighbor", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Surprise is a per-event quantity, whereas entropy averages surprise over a distribution and cross-entropy measures surprise under the model's distribution."}
{"aliases": ["A form of DRY violation where different programmers on the same team unknowingly write the same code."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_ff69b82839", "name": "Interdeveloper duplication defined", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Interdeveloper Duplication.md", "message": "A form of DRY violation where different programmers on the same team unknowingly write the same code.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Interdeveloper duplication is a DRY violation in which different programmers on the same team unknowingly write the same code, an internal redundancy across team members rather than duplication of external interfaces."}
{"aliases": ["Interdeveloper duplication happens because of communication gaps, not technical failures."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_474a72e512", "name": "Interdeveloper duplication comes from communication gaps", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Interdeveloper Duplication.md", "message": "Root cause", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Interdeveloper duplication arises from communication gaps rather than technical failures, so its remedy is organizational and cultural rather than tool-based, though tools help."}
{"aliases": ["Assign a librarian"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_6e4685d87c", "name": "Countermeasures to interdeveloper duplication", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Interdeveloper Duplication.md", "message": "Solutions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Interdeveloper duplication is countered by constant communication such as stand-ups and team chat, a designated librarian who curates shared utilities, a centralized discoverable folder for common helpers, and code review for awareness of teammates' work."}
{"aliases": ["translates and executes source code line by line, without producing a standalone executable"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_94c4b0b1fc", "name": "Interpreter translates and executes lines", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Interpreter.md", "message": "An interpreter translates and executes source code line by line, without producing a standalone executable.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An interpreter translates and executes source code line by line without producing a standalone executable, reading the code as data and acting it out using its own pre-written logic."}
{"aliases": ["The EVAL loop"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_efa3b37f40", "name": "The EVAL loop of an interpreter", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Interpreter.md", "message": "The EVAL loop", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An interpreter's core is an EVAL loop: given an expression such as (+ 2 3) it reads the text, recognizes the symbol +, calls its own internal pre-compiled addition function, and returns 5."}
{"aliases": ["it cannot look ahead to see the big picture"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_a625345078", "name": "Interpreters cannot look ahead", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Interpreter.md", "message": "Because the interpreter reads code on the fly, it cannot look ahead to see the big picture.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because an interpreter translates and executes code on the fly, it cannot look ahead to see the whole program."}
{"aliases": ["sacrificing deep optimization for instant feedback"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_6b3c09e68f", "name": "Interpreters forgo whole-program optimization", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Interpreter.md", "message": "Why interpreters can't optimize", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An interpreter forgoes whole-program optimizations such as math cleanup, dead code elimination, and register allocation, trading that optimization for instant feedback."}
{"aliases": ["builds an AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) from source code and walks it node by node"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:14Z", "id": "n_876597631c", "name": "Tree-walk versus bytecode interpreters", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Interpreter.md", "message": "Types", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A tree-walk interpreter builds an abstract syntax tree from source and walks it node by node, which is simple but slow, whereas a bytecode interpreter executes pre-compiled bytecode on a virtual machine and is faster because parsing is done upfront."}
{"aliases": ["where the gradient is exactly zero"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_72b9f3cc5c", "name": "Critical point is zero gradient", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Saddle Points (critical point triage).md", "message": "Saddle Points (Critical-Point Triage)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A critical point of a function is a point where the gradient is exactly zero."}
{"aliases": ["flat, but not a minimum"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_fd228c5f43", "name": "Saddle point definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Saddle Points (critical point triage).md", "message": "A saddle point is a critical point that is a minimum in some directions and a maximum in others", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A saddle point is a critical point that is a minimum in some directions and a maximum in others — flat, but not a minimum."}
{"aliases": ["All λ ≥ 0 (positive semi-definite)", "All λ ≤ 0 (negative semi-definite)", "Mixed signs"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_23fae3a858", "name": "Hessian eigenvalue triage", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Saddle Points (critical point triage).md", "message": "Triage via Hessian eigenvalues", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The signs of the Hessian eigenvalues triage a critical point: all eigenvalues ≥ 0 (positive semi-definite) means a local minimum, all eigenvalues ≤ 0 (negative semi-definite) means a local maximum, and mixed signs mean a saddle."}
{"aliases": ["a critical point is almost surely a saddle"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_6084926989", "name": "Saddles dominate in high dimensions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Saddle Points (critical point triage).md", "message": "Why saddles dominate in high dimensions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In a network with a million weights a critical point is almost surely a saddle rather than a local minimum, because a strict local minimum needs all d Hessian eigenvalue signs positive while a saddle needs only one sign to disagree; the (1/2)^d coin-flip argument is an intuition aid, not an exact law about real Hessian spectra."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_49cc35a41e", "name": "Gradient descent stalls at saddles", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Saddle Points (critical point triage).md", "message": "Why vanilla GD stalls there", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The vanilla gradient descent update w ← w − η∇L(w) is proportional to the gradient, so at an exact saddle point where the gradient is zero no step is taken."}
{"aliases": ["given context, give me P(next token)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_e1c6592521", "name": "Sampling generates tokens from a distribution", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Sampling from a Language Model.md", "message": "Overview", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Sampling from a language model generates text by repeatedly picking the next token according to the model's predicted probability distribution for the current context."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_df070e92a2", "name": "Autoregressive sampling loop", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Sampling from a Language Model.md", "message": "Autoregressive Loop", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In autoregressive sampling the loop starts from a sentinel token, looks up the distribution for the current token, samples the next token, feeds that token back as the next input, and repeats until an end sentinel is generated."}
{"aliases": ["The same sampling loop works at every scale"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_80caf7cb87", "name": "One sampling loop at every scale", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Sampling from a Language Model.md", "message": "Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The same sampling loop works at every scale — bigram, RNN, and GPT — differing only in how rich the predicted next-token distribution is."}
{"aliases": ["independent, and with a single, well-defined purpose"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_fdff2feb62", "name": "Self-contained component definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Self-Contained Components.md", "message": "Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A self-contained component is independent, has a single well-defined purpose, does not reach into other components for data or behavior, and does not expose its internals to the outside world."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_98132bf84f", "name": "Isolation testability diagnostic", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Self-Contained Components.md", "message": "Testing self-contained components", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A self-contained component is testable in isolation, so a module that needs half the system mocked to unit-test it is not self-contained but too coupled."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_1ef36a958a", "name": "Benefits of self-contained components", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Self-Contained Components.md", "message": "Practical benefits", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Self-contained components enable parallel development by separate teams, replacement of one component without cascading changes, and composition of larger systems from well-understood independent pieces."}
{"aliases": ["0 is simultaneously a valid quantity and an absence marker"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_c2c249f631", "name": "Sentinel value ambiguity", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Sentinel Values vs Presence Flags.md", "message": "Overview", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Sentinel Values vs Presence Flags.md", "message": "Reusing an ordinary value to represent *structural* status (present vs missing) is a classic systems-programming trap", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A sentinel value is a special value such as 0 or -1 chosen to mark absent or not found, and it is ambiguous whenever that value is also legitimate in the domain, because a real zero then cannot be told apart from a missing field."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_4ddf668c67", "name": "Zero-init default hides missing fields", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Sentinel Values vs Presence Flags.md", "message": "Sentinel in the Smoke Test", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A {0} struct default silently turns a missing field into zero, so a sanity bound such as available_kb <= total_kb passes and a smoke test reports success on fake data."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_a82986939a", "name": "Presence flags decouple presence from value", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Sentinel Values vs Presence Flags.md", "message": "The Fix: Separate Presence from Value", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Explicit presence flags decouple structural presence from numeric payload: a boolean such as has_available records whether the key was found while the value field stores only the quantity."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_ffb0f4d1fc", "name": "Sentinel safety rule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Sentinel Values vs Presence Flags.md", "message": "When sentinel vs flags", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A sentinel value is safe only when it lies outside the legitimate domain — ULONG_MAX for memory sizes is fine, 0 is not — while presence flags work universally and are the robust choice when no safe sentinel exists."}
{"aliases": ["Today's Posterior Becomes Tomorrow's Prior"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_f1c8353590", "name": "Conjugate posterior becomes next prior", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Sequential Bayesian Updating.md", "message": "Today's Posterior Becomes Tomorrow's Prior", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because a conjugate posterior stays in the same family as the prior, it can be used directly as the prior for the next update, so a system learns incrementally without reprocessing history."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_8213e2f5fe", "name": "Beta counts add per batch", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Sequential Bayesian Updating.md", "message": "Coin Example", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In sequential Beta updating each observation batch adds its head count to the first Beta parameter and its tail count to the second, so a Beta(1,1) prior after 7 heads and 3 tails becomes Beta(8,4)."}
{"aliases": ["Batch and sequential are mathematically equivalent"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_facd897c29", "name": "Batch equals sequential updating", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Sequential Bayesian Updating.md", "message": "Order Invariance", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Sequential and batch Bayesian updating are mathematically equivalent and order invariant: 7 heads and 3 tails followed by 5 heads and 5 tails gives Beta(13,9), identical to updating with all 12 heads and 8 tails at once."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_8286dc68c6", "name": "Shell arguments are plain strings", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shell Arguments & the Untyped-Variable Model.md", "message": "1. Arguments are plain strings", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Every argument passed on the command line arrives inside a script as a plain string with no type attached, so 42 and \"42\" are the same kind of thing to the shell until arithmetic expansion or a test is applied."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_b54ed72494", "name": "Shell variables are untyped strings", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shell Arguments & the Untyped-Variable Model.md", "message": "2. Shell variables have no types — they are all strings (by default)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Shell variables have no types by default: a plain variable holds a string and its type is implied by how it is used, with (( x + 1 )) treating the string as a number and [[ -n $x ]] treating it as text."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_a0eded3ba4", "name": "Untyped values force quoting", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shell Arguments & the Untyped-Variable Model.md", "message": "Word-splitting & quoting", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because shell values are untyped strings, unquoted expansion of $var and $@ is split on whitespace and has globs expanded, so expansions must be quoted to preserve a value as a single unit."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_051006f06d", "name": "declare -i integer tagging", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shell Arguments & the Untyped-Variable Model.md", "message": "Nuance: bash *can* optionally tag a variable as integer with `declare -i x`", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Bash can optionally tag a variable as integer with declare -i, after which assignments do arithmetic, but unadorned assignments are always strings and $(( … )) does the math inline."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_1e75c92f20", "name": "Built-ins modify internal shell state", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shell Built-ins & Process Isolation.md", "message": "What Counts as Internal Shell State", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Some commands must be built into the shell because they modify the shell's own internal state: the working directory, shell and environment variables, background jobs, shell options, and command history."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_1dcf2db449", "name": "Memory isolation blocks child-to-parent writes", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shell Built-ins & Process Isolation.md", "message": "Core Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The OS enforces memory isolation between parent and child processes, so a child process physically cannot write to its parent's memory and any command that must change the shell itself has to run inside the shell process."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_c12beac2c4", "name": "cd must be a built-in", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shell Built-ins & Process Isolation.md", "message": "Why `cd` Is a Built-in", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "If cd were an external program, the child process would change its own working directory and exit, leaving the parent shell's $PWD unchanged."}
{"aliases": ["if the command's **result persists on disk or stdout** (outside the shell process), it can be external"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_b2b09ae50d", "name": "Built-in versus external decision rule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shell Built-ins & Process Isolation.md", "message": "Built-ins vs External Programs", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A command can be an external program if its result persists on disk or stdout outside the shell process, and must be a built-in if it must change something in the shell's memory."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_24316c8d0c", "name": "Short-circuiting evaluates conditionally", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Short-circuit evaluation.md", "message": "How It Works", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Short-circuit evaluation means the second operand of a logical operator is evaluated only if the first operand does not already determine the result."}
{"aliases": ["returns A if A is falsy, otherwise evaluates and returns B"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_372be4886f", "name": "Semantics of and", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Short-circuit evaluation.md", "message": "**`A and B`** — returns A if A is falsy, otherwise evaluates and returns B", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In Python and JavaScript, A and B returns A if A is falsy and otherwise evaluates and returns B, which is equivalent to if A then B else A."}
{"aliases": ["returns A if A is truthy, otherwise evaluates and returns B"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_08ba208151", "name": "Semantics of or", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Short-circuit evaluation.md", "message": "**`A or B`** — returns A if A is truthy, otherwise evaluates and returns B", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In Python and JavaScript, A or B returns A if A is truthy and otherwise evaluates and returns B, which is equivalent to if A then A else B."}
{"aliases": ["these operators return the **actual operand value**, not just `True`/`False`"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_62ef509544", "name": "Logical operators return operand values", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Short-circuit evaluation.md", "message": "Crucially, these operators return the **actual operand value**, not just `True`/`False`", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Python and JavaScript logical operators and/or return the actual operand value rather than a boolean: None and \"Hello\" is None, \"Apple\" and \"Banana\" is \"Banana\", and \"Apple\" or \"Banana\" is \"Apple\"."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_ff977b8594", "name": "Short-circuit skips side effects", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Short-circuit evaluation.md", "message": "Side Effects Can Be Silently Skipped", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A short-circuited right operand with side effects is silently skipped: in if condition or perform_action(), perform_action() is never called when condition is truthy."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_c61a4db376", "name": "Shy code and the Law of Demeter", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shy Code.md", "message": "Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Shy code limits an object's interactions to a small set of immediate collaborators — close friends, not strangers or friends-of-friends — and is formalized as the Law of Demeter."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_30855988f7", "name": "Law of Demeter allowed calls", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shy Code.md", "message": "The Law of Demeter in practice", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Law of Demeter says a method m of object O should call methods only of O itself, m's parameters, objects created within m, and O's direct component objects."}
{"aliases": ["If an object needs changing, change it from within."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_c040849f46", "name": "Change objects from within", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shy Code.md", "message": "Core rule", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The core rule of shy code is that if an object needs changing it is changed from within: code asks the object to change itself instead of reaching into its internal state."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_ac74455cc8", "name": "Shy code lowers coupling", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Shy Code.md", "message": "Why shy code works", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Shy code lowers coupling to other objects' internal structure, which makes refactoring safe, keeps the public API self-documenting, and preserves encapsulation."}
{"aliases": ["A piece of code (class or method) should do one thing and do it well."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_b60b76dcf8", "name": "Single Responsibility Principle statement", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Single Responsibility Principle (SRP).md", "message": "Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Single Responsibility Principle holds that a piece of code — a class or method — should do one thing and do it well."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_8b944d2b77", "name": "Multiple responsibilities are costly", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Single Responsibility Principle (SRP).md", "message": "Core idea", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A method or class with multiple responsibilities becomes harder to understand, test, and modify, so responsibilities should be split until each piece has a single crisp job."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_fddca22fc3", "name": "Tokenizer addToken split", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Single Responsibility Principle (SRP).md", "message": "Example from tokenizer design", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In tokenizer design, splitting addToken into a short version whose only responsibility is supplying a clean default input and a long version that extracts the text, bundles a Token, and saves it removes the need for if (literal == null) checks throughout the long version."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:15Z", "id": "n_f0d7ee0d3b", "name": "SRP versus DRY", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Single Responsibility Principle (SRP).md", "message": "SRP is about one thing per unit, DRY is about one representation per piece of knowledge", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "SRP is about one thing per unit while DRY is about one representation per piece of knowledge, which makes the two principles distinct but complementary."}
{"aliases": ["${var%pattern}", "${var%%pattern}"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_e195226992", "name": "Suffix removal with % and %%", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Parameter Expansion.md", "message": "Key Patterns table: Remove shortest suffix / Remove longest suffix", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In Bash, ${var%pattern} removes the shortest suffix matching pattern from the variable's value, while ${var%%pattern} removes the longest matching suffix."}
{"aliases": ["${var#pattern}", "${var##pattern}", "${file##*/}"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_7cc8868f86", "name": "Prefix removal with # and ##", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Parameter Expansion.md", "message": "Extracting basename from path", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In Bash, ${var#pattern} removes the shortest prefix matching pattern from the variable's value, while ${var##pattern} removes the longest matching prefix, so ${file##*/} yields the basename of a path."}
{"aliases": ["${var:-default}", "${var:=default}", "${var:+replacement}", "${var:?error}"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_eaa3a8e046", "name": "Default-value parameter operators", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Parameter Expansion.md", "message": "Key Patterns table: default, assign, alternative, error rows", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Bash parameter expansion supplies defaults inline: :- substitutes a default when the variable is unset or empty, := also assigns that default to the variable, :+ substitutes a replacement only when the variable is set, and :? exits with an error message when the variable is unset or empty."}
{"aliases": ["No subprocess overhead", "Pattern matching uses glob-style (`*`, `?`, `[...]`), NOT regex"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_d0843d5a29", "name": "Parameter expansion avoids subprocesses", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Parameter Expansion.md", "message": "Why It Matters; Gotchas", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Bash parameter expansion runs inside the shell process, so it transforms variable values without forking a child process the way sed, awk, or cut do; pattern matching inside it uses glob syntax (*, ?, [...]) rather than regex."}
{"aliases": ["parses the token stream into a syntax tree"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_b3c2f92f0f", "name": "Parser builds a syntax tree", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Parser.md", "message": "intro paragraph; What It Does", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A parser is the compiler or interpreter stage that consumes the flat token stream produced by the lexer and builds a structured representation (a parse tree or abstract syntax tree) of the program according to a context-free grammar."}
{"aliases": ["Input: Characters / Tokens; Grammar: Regular language / Context-free grammar"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_eb5e60f070", "name": "Lexer vs parser division of labour", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Parser.md", "message": "Contrast with the Lexer table", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A lexer consumes characters and emits tokens under a regular-language grammar and cannot handle nesting, whereas a parser consumes tokens and emits a syntax tree under a context-free grammar and does handle nested brackets, parentheses, and control flow."}
{"aliases": ["Recursive descent", "LR / LALR", "Pratt parsing", "Packrat (PEG)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_4fd4817a7f", "name": "Parser families", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Parser.md", "message": "Types of Parsers", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Parser implementations fall into four families: recursive descent (top-down, hand-written), LR/LALR (bottom-up, table-driven, more powerful but harder to debug), Pratt parsing (precedence-climbing, suited to expressions), and packrat parsing (parsing expression grammars with backtracking)."}
{"aliases": ["center → covariance → eigenvectors → sort by λ → project", "The eigenvectors are the new axes"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_6fa883321d", "name": "PCA recipe and eigenvector meaning", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PCA (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "The recipe (steps 1-5)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "PCA builds a new basis by centering the data, computing the d-by-d covariance matrix, eigendecomposing it, sorting the eigenvalues in descending order, and projecting the centered points onto the top-k eigenvectors; each eigenvector is one of the new axes and its eigenvalue is the variance along that axis."}
{"aliases": ["Σλ = trace(C)", "Pie detector (total variance)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_b2132d32bf", "name": "Eigenvalues partition total variance", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PCA (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "Choosing k; Pie detector (total variance)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The eigenvalues of a covariance matrix partition the total variance because Σλ = trace(C), so axis i carries the fraction λ_i/Σλ of total spread and keeping the top k axes discards exactly Σ_{dropped} λ_j."}
{"aliases": ["error_i = ‖x_i − x̂_i‖² = Σ_{dropped axes} (coordinate)²"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_ffb3ef6255", "name": "Per-sample PCA reconstruction error", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PCA (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "PCA as an anomaly detector", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A point's PCA reconstruction error is the squared distance between the point and its projection onto the kept subspace, which equals the sum of squares of that point's coordinates on the dropped axes."}
{"aliases": ["along = normal, across = suspect", "Shape fit lives across; depth lives along"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_bdf548788c", "name": "Anomaly detection is across-subspace only", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PCA (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "Geometry — along = normal, across = suspect", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because PCA reconstruction error measures only the distance across the kept subspace, an anomaly detector built on it never flags a point lying far out along a kept direction; it flags points whose large coordinate lies on a dropped axis."}
{"aliases": ["Kernel PCA diagonalizes the n × n pairwise similarity Gram matrix K"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_36a095ef6c", "name": "Kernel PCA diagonalizes the Gram matrix", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PCA (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "Kernel PCA — Matrix Dimension Contrast ($d \\times d$ vs $n \\times n$)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Kernel PCA handles data on a nonlinear manifold by implicitly lifting points into a feature space through a kernel and diagonalizing the n-by-n pairwise similarity Gram matrix instead of the d-by-d covariance matrix, moving computational cost from feature dimension d to sample size n."}
{"aliases": ["C(-u) = λ(-u)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_639e8af0f6", "name": "Eigenvectors are defined up to sign", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PCA Sign Ambiguity (svd_flip).md", "message": "intro paragraph", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An eigenvector is defined only up to sign: if u is an eigenvector of C with eigenvalue λ then −u is also an eigenvector, because C(−u) = λ(−u), so PCA has no canonical sign for its components."}
{"aliases": ["Immune to sign", "Flip together"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_7a355a13f5", "name": "What flips with the sign and what does not", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PCA Sign Ambiguity (svd_flip).md", "message": "What flips and what does not", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In PCA a sign flip leaves eigenvalues, explained variances, reconstruction error, trace, and determinant unchanged, while each component direction and its signed projected coordinates flip together, keeping the same axis line with the two ends relabelled."}
{"aliases": ["my_i − sk_i = −2·sk_i", "label mirror"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_3157dbccf9", "name": "Signed gap is scale, not error", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PCA Sign Ambiguity (svd_flip).md", "message": "Why the signed gap is large and still harmless", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When two PCA implementations differ only by a sign convention, the maximum signed coordinate gap equals twice the largest coordinate in the cloud, so it measures the scale of the data rather than the size of an error."}
{"aliases": ["svd_flip"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_ffae59ac5a", "name": "svd_flip is a cosmetic convention", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PCA Sign Ambiguity (svd_flip).md", "message": "svd_flip section", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "scikit-learn's SVD-based PCA solvers post-process components through svd_flip, which flips each component so its largest-magnitude entry is positive; this is a cosmetic sign convention that changes neither the eigenvalues nor the projected variances."}
{"aliases": ["Restate such rules as `|score|` thresholds before shipping them"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_c852052d1a", "name": "Signed PCA thresholds are convention-dependent", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PCA Sign Ambiguity (svd_flip).md", "message": "The practical caveat: sign-sensitive thresholds", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Any rule that compares a signed PCA score to a threshold (such as flagging score > +2) is convention-dependent and breaks when the sign convention changes, so such rules should be restated as |score| thresholds, and implementations should be compared on magnitudes and variances rather than signed coordinates."}
{"aliases": ["PPL = 2^{H(P,Q)}", "PPL = 1 / (the geometric mean of the probabilities the model assigns to the true tokens)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_db66688b4d", "name": "Perplexity as exponentiated cross-entropy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Perplexity.md", "message": "Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Perplexity is defined as PPL = 2^{H(P,Q)} when the cross-entropy is measured in bits and as e^{H(P,Q)} when it is measured in nats, making it a monotone re-expression of cross-entropy rather than a different quantity."}
{"aliases": ["PPL = 2^{log₂ k} = k", "The uniform anchor"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_4d3a1f56a6", "name": "Uniform model has perplexity k", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Perplexity.md", "message": "The uniform anchor", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A uniform model over k outcomes has entropy log₂ k and therefore perplexity exactly k, which is why perplexity reads as the effective number of equally likely choices a model is hesitating between."}
{"aliases": ["bits → base 2, nats → base e"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_31cb080190", "name": "Perplexity unit and tokenizer discipline", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Perplexity.md", "message": "Unit discipline; The uniform anchor", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Perplexity comparisons require the entropy base to match the exponential (bits use base 2, nats use base e) and the tokenization to be identical, so an H of 5 bits gives perplexity 2^5 = 32, not e^5 ≈ 148.4, and a tokenizer change alters the number without changing the model."}
{"aliases": ["Per ≤ n−1", "perplexity must be smaller than the number of points"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_22ba00a1a0", "name": "t-SNE per-point perplexity and its ceiling", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Perplexity.md", "message": "A third use: t-SNE's per-point perplexity", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In t-SNE each point's neighbour distribution P_i gets its own bandwidth tuned by binary search until its perplexity 2^{H(P_i)} equals the user's perplexity knob, and because P_i lives on the other n−1 points the perplexity can never exceed n−1."}
{"aliases": ["P(X = k) gives a real probability at each point"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_b4a16474b3", "name": "PMF assigns point probabilities", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PMF vs PDF.md", "message": "PMF", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A probability mass function applies to discrete random variables, gives a genuine probability P(X = k) at each point, and its values sum to 1."}
{"aliases": ["f(x) gives density, NOT probability at a point", "Can exceed 1; total area = 1"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_cb57bf958d", "name": "PDF gives density not probability", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PMF vs PDF.md", "message": "PDF; Key Distinction", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A probability density function applies to continuous random variables and returns a density f(x) rather than a probability at a point; a density can exceed 1, while its total area equals 1."}
{"aliases": ["PDF at a point ≠ probability. PDF = density. Integrate to get probability."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_3ecd17b1b7", "name": "Probability of an exact value is zero", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PMF vs PDF.md", "message": "PDF; Key Distinction", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For a continuous random variable the probability of any exact value is zero, P(X = exact value) = 0, because probability is obtained by integrating the density over an interval."}
{"aliases": ["F(x) = P(X ≤ x)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_55eb68d08f", "name": "CDF is the integral of the PDF", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PMF vs PDF.md", "message": "CDF", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The cumulative distribution function is F(x) = P(X ≤ x), obtained as the integral of the probability density function."}
{"aliases": ["nn.MaxPool2d(kernel_size=2, stride=2)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_70c9397e74", "name": "Max pooling downsamples feature maps", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Pooling (MaxPool2d).md", "message": "intro; code block", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Max pooling with a 2×2 window and stride 2 keeps only the maximum value in each window, mapping a [batch, channels, H, W] feature map to [batch, channels, H/2, W/2] while leaving the channel count unchanged."}
{"aliases": ["Reduces parameters", "Increases receptive field"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_12a6e541f2", "name": "Pooling shrinks parameters and widens receptive field", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Pooling (MaxPool2d).md", "message": "Three Purposes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Pooling reduces the number of parameters needed by the final classifier layers and increases the receptive field, so each pixel in deeper layers sees more of the original image."}
{"aliases": ["Translation invariance — a lesion shifted slightly produces the same pooled output"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_001ff7cf16", "name": "Pooling gives translation invariance", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Pooling (MaxPool2d).md", "message": "Three Purposes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Pooling yields translation invariance: a lesion shifted slightly within the image produces the same pooled output."}
{"aliases": ["$0", "$#", "$@"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_5079656fc5", "name": "Positional parameters carry script arguments", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Positional & Special Shell Parameters.md", "message": "Positional parameters table", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Shell arguments arrive as plain strings and are reached through positional parameters: $0 names the program being run, $1 through $9 are the first nine arguments in order, $# is the total number of arguments, and $@ is all arguments as a list with one element per original argument."}
{"aliases": ["$10 ≠ the 10th argument", "${10}, ${11}, …"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_a0cac5346b", "name": "Braces are mandatory past the ninth argument", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Positional & Special Shell Parameters.md", "message": "Positional parameters table; Why this matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Beyond the ninth argument braces are mandatory: $10 is parsed as $1 followed by a literal 0, whereas ${10} is the tenth argument."}
{"aliases": ["The shell is stringly typed."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_0cb68dcf43", "name": "Shell is stringly typed and unquoted $@ word-splits", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Positional & Special Shell Parameters.md", "message": "Why this matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The shell is stringly typed — every argument is a string — and unquoted $@ word-splits each argument on whitespace, which breaks arguments containing spaces, so \"$@\" must be quoted."}
{"aliases": ["echo \"$0: cannot open $1\" >&2"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_0d412219c3", "name": "$0 identifies the running program", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Positional & Special Shell Parameters.md", "message": "Why this matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "$0 tells a script which program is executing, which supports error messages like \"$0: cannot open $1\" and scripts that behave differently based on the name they were invoked as."}
{"aliases": ["Your updated belief after incorporating evidence"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_87aeda91b0", "name": "Posterior is updated belief", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Posterior Probability.md", "message": "Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The posterior probability P(H|E) is the belief in hypothesis H after incorporating evidence E, and it is the output of Bayes' theorem."}
{"aliases": ["P(H|E) = P(E|H) · P(H) / P(E)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_f842496239", "name": "Bayes' theorem posterior formula", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Posterior Probability.md", "message": "Formula", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Bayes' theorem computes the posterior as P(H|E) = P(E|H)·P(H)/P(E), the likelihood of the evidence given the hypothesis times the prior, divided by the probability of the evidence."}
{"aliases": ["P(disease|positive) ≈ 9%"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_571b997650", "name": "Rare disease keeps posterior near 9 percent", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Posterior Probability.md", "message": "Disease Example Result", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "With a 99%-accurate test for a disease whose base rate is very low, the posterior probability of disease after a positive result is only about 9%."}
{"aliases": ["Your belief before seeing any evidence — the base rate"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_8bcba21875", "name": "Prior is the base rate", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prior Probability.md", "message": "Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The prior probability P(H) is the belief in a hypothesis before any evidence is seen, that is, the base rate."}
{"aliases": ["The prior dominates when a condition is rare."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_de2efd1cb0", "name": "Prior dominates for rare conditions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prior Probability.md", "message": "Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The prior dominates when a condition is rare: even accurate tests produce mostly false positives when the base rate is low."}
{"aliases": ["P(S) = 1 — something always happens"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_32599d8c47", "name": "Three axioms of probability", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability & Distributions for ML.md", "message": "The Three Axioms of Probability", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A probability function on a sample space S satisfies P(A) >= 0 for every event A, P(S) = 1, and P(A union B) = P(A) + P(B) whenever A and B are mutually exclusive."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_32d548d1cb", "name": "Conditional probability definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability & Distributions for ML.md", "message": "Conditional Probability & Bayes' Theorem", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The conditional probability of event A given event B is P(A | B) = P(A intersect B) / P(B)."}
{"aliases": ["the posterior probability of a hypothesis given data is proportional to the likelihood times the prior"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_4467d0fdf4", "name": "Bayes' theorem", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability & Distributions for ML.md", "message": "Bayes' theorem inverts the condition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability foundations.md", "message": "4. Bayes' rule", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Bayes' theorem inverts a conditional probability: P(A | B) = P(B | A) * P(A) / P(B), converting the likelihood P(B | A) into the posterior P(A | B)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_c4a9f0454f", "name": "Softmax normalizes logits into probabilities", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability & Distributions for ML.md", "message": "Softmax & Cross-Entropy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability Mass Function (PMF).md", "message": "the softmax at the end converts raw logits into a valid PMF", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Softmax converts a vector of raw logits z into probabilities via softmax(z_i) = exp(z_i) / sum_j exp(z_j)."}
{"aliases": ["Cross-entropy loss = negative log probability of the correct class"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_3815b7cc6c", "name": "Cross-entropy of one-hot labels is negative log-likelihood", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability & Distributions for ML.md", "message": "With one-hot labels, this collapses to Loss = -log(q_correct)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Cross-entropy H(p, q) = -sum_i p_i log(q_i) collapses, when labels are one-hot, to loss = -log(q_correct), the negative log-likelihood of the correct class."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_64116ec041", "name": "Sum rule marginalizes a joint distribution", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability foundations.md", "message": "3. Basic probability rules", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The sum rule obtains a marginal distribution from a joint distribution by summing (discrete) or integrating (continuous) over the other variables."}
{"aliases": ["The product rule lets you factor a joint distribution into a marginal and a conditional."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_4c1742a108", "name": "Product rule factors a joint distribution", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability foundations.md", "message": "3. Basic probability rules", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The product rule factors a joint probability into a marginal and a conditional, P(A intersect B) = P(B | A) * P(A)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_0244c587ba", "name": "Correlation is bounded scaled covariance", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability foundations.md", "message": "6. Covariance and correlation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Correlation is the scaled version of covariance, so it always lies between -1 and 1."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_bca172f52f", "name": "Gaussians are closed under sums and linear maps", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability foundations.md", "message": "8. Gaussian distributions and conjugacy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Sums of Gaussian random variables are still Gaussian, and linear transformations of Gaussian variables are still Gaussian."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_c2195f7a99", "name": "Gaussian conjugacy gives closed-form posteriors", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability foundations.md", "message": "8. Gaussian distributions and conjugacy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Pairing a Gaussian prior with a Gaussian likelihood yields a Gaussian posterior, a property called conjugacy that gives closed-form Bayesian updates."}
{"aliases": ["A PMF is just a lookup table."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_2fa8fc5801", "name": "Probability mass function", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability mass and density functions.md", "message": "PMF", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability Mass Function (PMF).md", "message": "Properties", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability foundations.md", "message": "A PMF assigns probability mass to discrete states, and all the masses add up to 1.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A probability mass function assigns each discrete state of a random variable a nonnegative probability, and the masses over all states sum to 1."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_52f7d52255", "name": "Probability density function", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability mass and density functions.md", "message": "PDF", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability foundations.md", "message": "A PDF is the continuous analogue", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A probability density function describes a continuous random variable: probabilities of intervals are areas under the density curve computed by integration, and the probability of one exact point is effectively zero."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_b32af5d412", "name": "PDF validity conditions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability mass and density functions.md", "message": "Why f(x) can exceed 1", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The only requirements for a probability density function are f(x) >= 0 for all x and integral of f(x) dx over the whole real line equal to 1."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_3bf07f579a", "name": "PDF values may exceed one", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability mass and density functions.md", "message": "A pdf value like f(5) = 2.0 is perfectly valid", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability & Distributions for ML.md", "message": "Can exceed 1? Yes (it's a density)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A PDF value can exceed 1 without meaning a probability above 1; f(5) = 2.0 is valid because a density measures concentration per unit, much like population density."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_6f5d28c9a1", "name": "CDF accumulates probability and differentiates to the PDF", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability mass and density functions.md", "message": "CDF: the bridge between PDF and probability", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The cumulative distribution function F_X(x) = P(X <= x) accumulates probability up to x for both discrete and continuous variables, and for continuous variables the PDF is its derivative, f(x) = d/dx F_X(x)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_af46d2e2f0", "name": "Classifier outputs are probability mass functions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Probability Mass Function (PMF).md", "message": "Every classifier output is a PMF.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A classifier's output is a probability mass function: the final softmax turns raw logits into class probabilities that sum to 1, which cross-entropy then compares against the one-hot true label."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_4379a8eac3", "name": "Process substitution yields a file path", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Process Substitution.md", "message": "Process substitution runs a command, sends its output to a temporary file", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Process substitution runs a command, sends its output to a temporary file or /dev/fd pipe, and substitutes that path in place of <(CMD), letting commands that require a file path argument consume live command output."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_2a6115a55a", "name": "Command substitution versus process substitution", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Process Substitution.md", "message": "Process substitution vs command substitution", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Command substitution $(cmd) captures a command's output text as a value, whereas process substitution <(cmd) yields a file path to that output."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_e8650f46a3", "name": "Trust is built through reliability", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Professional Responsibility.md", "message": "Why it matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Trust in a developer is built through reliability: admitting when one is wrong, fixing problems instead of explaining them away, following through on commitments, and being honest about what one does not know."}
{"aliases": ["I don't know, but I'll find out"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_fa76ab85c4", "name": "Commit to finding out when you do not know", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Professional Responsibility.md", "message": "Core principles", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Saying \"I don't know\" should never stand alone; it should always be followed by a commitment to discover the answer."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_5618a83335", "name": "Programming paradigm definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Programming paradigms comparison.md", "message": "A programming paradigm is a style or approach to structuring code.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A programming paradigm is a style or approach to structuring code; the three most popular paradigms are imperative, functional, and object-oriented programming."}
{"aliases": ["Tell the computer HOW to do it"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_afa80ed368", "name": "Imperative programming changes state step by step", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Programming paradigms comparison.md", "message": "1. Imperative Programming", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Imperative programming expresses computation as step-by-step instructions that change program state through variable updates and loops."}
{"aliases": ["Tell the computer WHAT to calculate"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_b8e1e210fa", "name": "Functional programming uses pure immutable functions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Programming paradigms comparison.md", "message": "2. Functional Programming (FP)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Functional programming builds computation from mathematical functions over immutable data with no side effects, flowing data through pipelines of pure functions."}
{"aliases": ["Group data and behavior into OBJECTS"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_aac42ec161", "name": "Object-oriented programming bundles data and behavior", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Programming paradigms comparison.md", "message": "3. Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Object-oriented programming models programs as objects that bundle data (properties) and behavior (methods) into classes, using inheritance and encapsulation."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_6b02bad442", "name": "Multi-paradigm rule of thumb", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Programming paradigms comparison.md", "message": "The Hybrid Reality", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A practical multi-paradigm rule of thumb is to use object-oriented code for structure and architecture, functional code for business logic and data transformations, and imperative code for performance bottlenecks and direct hardware access."}
{"aliases": ["Variables Are Labels, Not Boxes"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_dcf3ead040", "name": "Python variables are labels pointing to PyObjects", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Python performance tradeoffs.md", "message": "1. Variables Are Labels, Not Boxes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In Python a variable is a reference label pointing to a PyObject that carries type information, a reference count, and the value, so every access requires pointer dereferencing and type checking rather than a fixed-size memory box."}
{"aliases": ["Every Operator Is a Function Call"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_49ed36bd16", "name": "Python operators are method calls", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Python performance tradeoffs.md", "message": "3. Every Operator Is a Function Call", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In Python every operator is a function call: a + b is syntactic sugar for a.__add__(b), so even simple arithmetic goes through dynamic method dispatch."}
{"aliases": ["The very dynamism that makes Python productive defeats JIT optimization."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_e80deb44a7", "name": "PyPy's JIT only wins on stable loops", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Python performance tradeoffs.md", "message": "PyPy: JIT's Limits with Dynamic Code", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "PyPy's tracing JIT compiles stable, type-consistent loops into efficient machine code but falls back to slower interpretation, re-analysis, and abandoned optimizations when code fetches unpredictable data or follows complex framework paths."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_02d9b2d310", "name": "Garbage collection imposes a runtime tax", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Python performance tradeoffs.md", "message": "The Garbage Collection Tax", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Python pays high runtime overhead for reference counting plus cyclic garbage collection, while Rust's compile-time ownership eliminates runtime memory-management bookkeeping entirely."}
{"aliases": ["Python's flexibility and its performance limitations are two sides of the same coin."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_cb7d1c5788", "name": "Python flexibility and slowness are one tradeoff", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Python performance tradeoffs.md", "message": "Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Python's flexibility and its performance limitations are two sides of the same coin: dynamic typing, runtime introspection, and automatic memory management make Python fast to write and slow to run."}
{"aliases": ["Dependency hell is real in AI/ML. Virtual environments are the cure."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_46921a6636", "name": "Virtual environments isolate project dependencies", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Python Virtual Environments.md", "message": "The Problem", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A virtual environment gives each project its own isolated set of package versions, avoiding the impossible conflicts that arise when all packages are installed globally."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_28a6967731", "name": "Do not mix pip and conda in one environment", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Python Virtual Environments.md", "message": "Mixing pip and conda — Pick one environment manager and stick with it.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "If conda is used for an environment it should be used for everything in that environment, because mixing pip into conda causes painful dependency conflicts."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_ee19d2a5ee", "name": "pyproject.toml replaces legacy project config", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Python Virtual Environments.md", "message": "pyproject.toml", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "pyproject.toml is the modern Python project configuration file, replacing setup.py, setup.cfg, and requirements.txt."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_83dc508a92", "name": "Lockfiles pin all dependencies for reproducibility", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Python Virtual Environments.md", "message": "Lockfiles", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A lockfile pins every dependency, including transitive ones, to exact versions for reproducibility and should be committed to git."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_186835cebe", "name": "PyTorch networks subclass nn.Module", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PyTorch Model Building.md", "message": "Every neural network in PyTorch is a subclass of nn.Module.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Every PyTorch neural network is a subclass of nn.Module that declares its layers in __init__ and defines its data flow in forward."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_0dd58d776f", "name": "PyTorch layer computation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PyTorch Model Building.md", "message": "A single layer computes: output = activation(weights × input + bias)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A PyTorch layer computes output = activation(weights * input + bias) using learnable weights and biases."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_7ba0e74c8a", "name": "nn.CrossEntropyLoss applies softmax internally", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PyTorch Model Building.md", "message": "nn.CrossEntropyLoss() applies softmax internally", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "PyTorch's nn.CrossEntropyLoss applies softmax internally to the logits it receives."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_96213c3bb2", "name": "PyTorch output layers return raw logits", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PyTorch Model Building.md", "message": "raw logits — no activation on output", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A PyTorch classification model's output layer returns raw logits with no softmax activation applied."}
{"aliases": ["X : Ω → T"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_9c98597cdd", "name": "Random variable as a function", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Random variable.md", "message": "Formal definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A random variable X is a function X : Ω → T from the sample space Ω of all possible outcomes of an experiment to a target space T of possible values, whose elements are called states."}
{"aliases": ["A random variable is the mapping, not the value."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_38734a5ccf", "name": "Random variable is the mapping, not the value", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Random variable.md", "message": "Key insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A random variable is the mapping from outcomes to values, not the value itself; a value x = X(ω) is a realization produced when a particular outcome ω occurs."}
{"aliases": ["X^{-1}(S) = {ω ∈ Ω : X(ω) ∈ S}"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_6f6e540060", "name": "Pre-image under a random variable", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Random variable.md", "message": "Pre-image", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The pre-image of a subset S ⊆ T under a random variable X is X^{-1}(S) = {ω ∈ Ω : X(ω) ∈ S}, the set of all outcomes whose X-value falls in S, and it lives in Ω rather than in T."}
{"aliases": ["P_X(S) = P(X^{-1}(S))"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_a801c8a695", "name": "Distribution induced on the target space", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Random variable.md", "message": "Why formalize it this way", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Probability about values of a random variable equals probability of the underlying outcomes, P_X(S) = P(X^{-1}(S)), so distributions on the target space T are induced by the probability measure on Ω through X."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_010886d8cb", "name": "ML estimates probabilities on the target space", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Random variable.md", "message": "Why this matters for ML", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Machine-learning models estimate probabilities on a random variable's target space T, for example P(X = 1 | pixel values) for classification, rather than on the enormous sample space of all possible images, where working directly with probabilities is infeasible."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_198ea65a5c", "name": "RNNs process sequences with a hidden state", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs).md", "message": "All models so far process one fixed-size input → one output. RNNs handle sequences", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Recurrent neural networks process sequence data such as time series, text, audio, and video one element at a time, maintaining a hidden state that carries information from previous steps, unlike models that map one fixed-size input to one output."}
{"aliases": ["vanishing gradients through time", "information from 20+ steps ago is essentially lost"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_7a44bbd76e", "name": "Vanishing gradients through time in basic RNNs", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs).md", "message": "The Problem with Basic RNNs", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Basic recurrent neural networks suffer from vanishing gradients through time, so information from 20 or more steps earlier is essentially lost, which makes them useless for long sequences such as predicting rainfall from 365 days of data."}
{"aliases": ["write a failing test first (Red), write the minimal code to make it pass (Green), then clean up the design (Refactor)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_10760309b8", "name": "Red-Green-Refactor loop", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Red-Green-Refactor.md", "message": "The Three Steps", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The test-driven development loop consists of writing a test that fails for a meaningful reason (Red), writing the minimal code needed to make it pass (Green), then cleaning up code and improving design without breaking the now-passing test (Refactor)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_f626cb404e", "name": "Red must precede Green", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Red-Green-Refactor.md", "message": "Why Red Must Come First", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Seeing a test fail before the implementation is written proves both that the test actually runs the new code rather than an old version or a test never wired up, and that its assertion can catch a failure instead of passing unconditionally."}
{"aliases": ["A test that passes with broken code is worse than no test: it manufactures false confidence."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_9da4e53d0b", "name": "A green test never seen red is worse than none", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Red-Green-Refactor.md", "message": "Why Red Must Come First", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A test that passes with broken code is worse than no test, because it manufactures false confidence instead of catching the defect."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_c96d2bfa36", "name": "Red can be a compile-time failure", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Red-Green-Refactor.md", "message": "Red can be a compile-time failure.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A compile-time failure, such as calling a function symbol that does not exist yet, can serve as the Red state, because the test compiles only if the declared interface matches, so the test doubles as an interface specification before implementation details distract."}
{"aliases": ["model memorizes training data but fails on new data"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_af317bdd56", "name": "Overfitting definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Regularization Techniques.md", "message": "Overfitting = model memorizes training data but fails on new data.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Overfitting occurs when a model memorizes its training data but fails on new data, and it is the primary obstacle when medical or agricultural datasets are small."}
{"aliases": ["Randomly disables neurons during training.", "Forces redundancy"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_5cf9aa9c38", "name": "Dropout", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Regularization Techniques.md", "message": "Regularization Techniques table, Dropout row", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Dropout regularization randomly disables neurons during training, forcing redundancy so that no single neuron can dominate."}
{"aliases": ["Adds penalty for large weights. Encourages simpler models."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_af08ee8760", "name": "Weight decay (L2)", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Regularization Techniques.md", "message": "Regularization Techniques table, Weight Decay (L2) row", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Weight decay adds a penalty for large weights to the loss, encouraging simpler models."}
{"aliases": ["Stop training when validation loss stops improving."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_d60c57b07e", "name": "Early stopping", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Regularization Techniques.md", "message": "Regularization Techniques table, Early Stopping row", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Early stopping ends training when validation loss stops improving, which prevents the model from memorizing the training data."}
{"aliases": ["Artificially expands dataset — the best regularizer."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_8dab53165c", "name": "Data augmentation is the strongest regularizer", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Regularization Techniques.md", "message": "Regularization Techniques table, Data Augmentation row", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Data augmentation artificially expands the dataset and is described as the best regularizer."}
{"aliases": ["Total Loss = Data Loss (NLL) + Regularization Loss"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_71b5c66d01", "name": "Total loss is data loss plus regularization loss", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Regularization Tug-of-War.md", "message": "The total loss is a compromise between two competing forces", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The total loss minimized during training is the sum of a data loss term, the negative log-likelihood, and a regularization loss term."}
{"aliases": ["Pulls weights UP", "Pulls weights DOWN (toward zero)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_39f9b99fc6", "name": "Two opposing forces on the weights", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Regularization Tug-of-War.md", "message": "**Data Loss (NLL):** Pulls weights UP ... **Regularization Loss:** Pulls weights DOWN", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Data loss (NLL) pulls weights up, wanting sharp confident distributions that fit the training data exactly, while regularization loss pulls weights down toward zero, wanting flat uniform distributions."}
{"aliases": ["c = 0: Data loss dominates — overconfident, overfits"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_352734f00d", "name": "Zero regularization overfits", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Regularization Tug-of-War.md", "message": "The dial, c = 0 row", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "With regularization strength c = 0 the data loss dominates, weights grow large, the distribution becomes sharp and peaked, and the model overfits with training-data NLL approaching zero."}
{"aliases": ["NLL = -ln(1/27) ≈ 3.295", "W = 0 → uniform distribution"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_881b9f39a6", "name": "Infinite regularization gives the uniform distribution", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Regularization Tug-of-War.md", "message": "c = ∞: Regularization dominates — W = 0 → uniform distribution → NLL = 3.295", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "With regularization strength c = ∞ the regularization dominates, all weights go to zero, the next-character distribution becomes uniform at 1/27, and the negative log-likelihood is -ln(1/27) ≈ 3.295."}
{"aliases": ["c = optimal: Balanced — ~2.47 for the bigram names dataset"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_e8d0f3ec0c", "name": "Intermediate regularization strength is optimal", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Regularization Tug-of-War.md", "message": "Key numbers for the bigram case table, Optimal row", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An intermediate regularization strength balances the two forces, and on the bigram names dataset the optimal setting gives moderate weights, a smooth distribution, and NLL ≈ 2.47."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_d1028f936b", "name": "Representational duplication definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Representational Duplication.md", "message": "intro: A form of DRY violation that occurs when your code connects to external systems", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Representational duplication is a DRY violation in which code connecting to an external system such as an API, database, or third-party service manually recreates that external system's data model."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_b923261375", "name": "Hand-written external models break silently", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Representational Duplication.md", "message": "The problem", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When both sides of an integration must agree on how data looks and the code hand-writes matching structures, a change on the external side silently breaks the code."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_81ba003cc8", "name": "Schema-driven code generation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Representational Duplication.md", "message": "Option 1: Schema-driven code generation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Schema-driven code generation reads the external schema and auto-generates the matching code structures, using introspecting ORMs such as Prisma, Entity Framework or SQLAlchemy, OpenAPI/Swagger generators for REST APIs, or Protocol Buffer compilers for gRPC, so a schema change is handled by rerunning the tool."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_b928f35502", "name": "Flexible containers plus validation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Representational Duplication.md", "message": "Option 2: Flexible data structures + validation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Storing external data in generic containers such as dicts, maps, or JSON objects and validating it against a lightweight rules table, as with Pydantic, Zod, or JSON Schema, means a schema change requires updating only the validation rule rather than many code structures."}
{"aliases": ["∂output/∂this_node"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_1282040764", "name": "Reverse-mode autodiff walks the graph backward", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Reverse-Mode Autodiff & Backprop.md", "message": "Reverse-mode autodiff computes derivatives after the forward pass by walking backward", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Reverse-mode automatic differentiation computes derivatives after the forward pass by walking backward through the computational graph and propagating adjoints, each adjoint being ∂output/∂this_node."}
{"aliases": ["Forward mode needs n passes (one per input variable); Reverse mode needs m passes (one per output variable)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_3805f8fa19", "name": "Pass counts for forward and reverse mode", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Reverse-Mode Autodiff & Backprop.md", "message": "For a function with n inputs and m outputs", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For a function with n inputs and m outputs, forward-mode autodiff needs n passes, one per input variable, while reverse-mode autodiff needs m passes, one per output variable."}
{"aliases": ["millions of weight inputs → one scalar loss output"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_6946f75c10", "name": "Neural networks are n-large, m-equals-one", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Reverse-Mode Autodiff & Backprop.md", "message": "Neural network asymmetry: millions of weight inputs → one scalar loss output", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A neural network training problem has millions of weight inputs and a single scalar loss output, so n is in the millions while m = 1."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_e4ca963032", "name": "Reverse mode is the practical choice for neural networks", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Reverse-Mode Autodiff & Backprop.md", "message": "This makes reverse mode the only practical choice", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because a neural network has millions of weight inputs and one scalar output, reverse-mode autodiff yields all weight gradients in a single backward pass whereas forward mode would need millions of passes, making reverse mode the only practical choice."}
{"aliases": ["Backpropagation is reverse-mode autodiff applied to a neural network's computational graph. It's not a separate algorithm."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_4ea7958844", "name": "Backpropagation is reverse-mode autodiff", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Reverse-Mode Autodiff & Backprop.md", "message": "Backpropagation **is** reverse-mode autodiff applied to a neural network's computational graph.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Backpropagation is reverse-mode automatic differentiation applied to a neural network's computational graph, not a separate algorithm."}
{"aliases": ["xenc @ W  →  W[ix, :]", "plucks out the ix-th row"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_716e4c1828", "name": "Row-select property of one-hot times matrix", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Row-Select Property.md", "message": "When you multiply a one-hot vector by a matrix W, the result is just the corresponding row of W", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Multiplying a one-hot vector by a weight matrix W returns the row of W at the one-hot's index, so the product simply plucks out that row."}
{"aliases": ["logits for input \"m\" (index 13) = W[13]"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_13f72b9341", "name": "Logits then softmax give the next-character distribution", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Row-Select Property.md", "message": "So logits for input \"m\" (index 13) = W[13]. After softmax, this is the probability distribution for the next character.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The logits for input character \"m\" at index 13 are row W[13], and applying softmax to that row produces the probability distribution over the next character."}
{"aliases": ["They converge to the same values"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_4f3e94a88c", "name": "Neural bigram learns the counting table", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Row-Select Property.md", "message": "What this means", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A neural bigram model with a single linear layer, no bias, and no hidden layer learns the same 27×27 table as the counting approach, stored as log-probabilities instead of counts, and the two converge to the same values."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_e4e1e0ae9c", "name": "Hidden layers go beyond the counting table", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Row-Select Property.md", "message": "The power is that the same framework extends to hidden layers", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The neural framework extends to models with hidden layers, which the counting approach cannot do."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_0b5fd4d2f5", "name": "What a runtime does", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Runtime.md", "message": "What the runtime does", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A runtime is the background system that keeps a program alive while it executes, handling memory management, bounds/type/null checks, exception handling, thread and async scheduling, and I/O with files, networks, and devices."}
{"aliases": ["Engine — the executor that reads and runs code", "Environment — the surrounding context and capabilities provided to the program"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_cf73b53c62", "name": "Engine and environment components", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Runtime.md", "message": "The two components", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A runtime has two components: an engine, the executor that reads and runs code such as V8, the JVM, or CPython, and an environment, the surrounding context and capabilities such as browser APIs, Node.js APIs, or Python's standard library."}
{"aliases": ["Neither is complete without the other"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_cb012956ea", "name": "Engine and environment are mutually dependent", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Runtime.md", "message": "musicians-and-concert-hall metaphor", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A runtime's engine and environment are each incomplete without the other: the best musicians sound terrible in a poor hall, and the finest hall is silent without musicians."}
{"aliases": ["you \"travel with your own crew\""], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_3c51a1fc72", "name": "Compiled vs interpreted runtime delivery", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Runtime.md", "message": "Compiled vs interpreted runtimes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Compiled languages such as Go and Rust compile the runtime directly into the binary executable, whereas interpreted or JIT languages such as Java, Python, and JavaScript rely on a separate runtime piece installed on the user's computer."}
{"aliases": ["A learnable lookup table that maps each word ID to a dense vector (e.g., 100 numbers)."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_977f07b18d", "name": "nn.Embedding as learnable lookup table", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Word Embeddings (nn.Embedding).md", "message": "## What It Is", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "nn.Embedding is a learnable lookup table that maps each word ID to a dense vector of fixed size, such as 100 numbers."}
{"aliases": ["Integer IDs (1, 2, 3…) are meaningless to a neural network — ID 5 is not \"more than\" ID 3.", "Embeddings give words a meaningful representational space."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_6c160cb9be", "name": "Integer IDs are meaningless; embeddings give structure", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Word Embeddings (nn.Embedding).md", "message": "## The Problem It Solves", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Integer word IDs are meaningless to a neural network because ID 5 is not \"more than\" ID 3, so embeddings are used to give words a meaningful representational space."}
{"aliases": ["Initialized randomly. During training, backpropagation updates them"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_28cab3d2ca", "name": "Embedding vectors are learned by backpropagation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Word Embeddings (nn.Embedding).md", "message": "## How Embeddings Learn", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Embedding vectors are initialized randomly and are updated during training by backpropagation like any other parameter."}
{"aliases": ["omusujja", "omulwadde"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_0c50b19230", "name": "Context-similar words drift together", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Word Embeddings (nn.Embedding).md", "message": "\"omusujja\" (fever) and \"omulwadde\" (patient) drift close because they co-occur in medical texts.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Words that appear in similar contexts end up with nearby embedding vectors; for example \"omusujja\" (fever) and \"omulwadde\" (patient) drift close because they co-occur in medical texts."}
{"aliases": ["`num_embeddings` = vocabulary size (rows in lookup table)", "`embedding_dim` = vector size per word (typical: 100-300)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:55Z", "id": "n_3a3df166d8", "name": "num_embeddings and embedding_dim parameters", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Word Embeddings (nn.Embedding).md", "message": "## Key Facts", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In nn.Embedding, num_embeddings sets the vocabulary size (the rows of the lookup table) and embedding_dim sets the vector size per word, typically 100 to 300."}
{"aliases": ["the exact gradient"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_bebbf02c7b", "name": "Full-batch GD stalls at exact-zero saddles", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mini-batch Noise (two effects).md", "message": "Full-batch exact vs mini-batch noisy estimate", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "With full-batch gradient descent the step uses the exact gradient over the entire dataset, so at a saddle where the true gradient is exactly zero there is no direction to move and the iterate can sit on the flat spot."}
{"aliases": ["a noisy estimate of the true gradient"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_a6a9129207", "name": "Mini-batch gradient is a noisy estimate", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mini-batch Noise (two effects).md", "message": "With mini-batch GD the gradient uses only a small random slice", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A mini-batch gradient is computed from a small random slice of the data and is a noisy estimate of the true gradient that is almost never exactly zero, even where the true gradient is zero."}
{"aliases": ["The noisy mini-batch gradient is almost never exactly zero, so SGD doesn't stall at saddles / plateaus"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_ef6ae2dea4", "name": "Mini-batch noise escapes saddles and plateaus", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mini-batch Noise (two effects).md", "message": "Mini-batch Noise (Two Effects) — Optimization row of the effects table", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Mini-batch noise lets SGD keep moving off saddles and plateaus where full-batch gradient descent can stall, because the random push of a nonzero mini-batch gradient estimate moves the iterate away from the flat spot."}
{"aliases": ["Sharp minima are narrow pits overfit to training quirks — they generalize poorly"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_9aada102bd", "name": "Sharp versus flat minima generalization", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mini-batch Noise (two effects).md", "message": "Why it matters: SGD vs Adam on test accuracy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Sharp minima are narrow pits overfit to training quirks and generalize poorly, while flat minima are wide basins whose whole neighborhoods of good solutions are robust to the train-to-test shift and generalize well."}
{"aliases": ["SGD's noise keeps it out of the sharp pits"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_32286fa323", "name": "SGD noise beats Adam on test accuracy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Mini-batch Noise (two effects).md", "message": "This is one reason SGD with momentum often beats Adam on final test accuracy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "SGD with momentum often beats Adam on final test accuracy even where Adam's adaptivity makes faster early progress, one reason being that SGD's noise keeps the optimizer out of sharp minima."}
{"aliases": ["software interrupt"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_869e7023a7", "name": "Definition of a signal", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Command-line Environment.md", "message": "Signals (software interrupts)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A signal is a short asynchronous message that the kernel or another process delivers to a process, and the receiving process can catch it, ignore it, or take the default action, which is often to terminate."}
{"aliases": ["nuclear option — always works, skips cleanup"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_1c5b60d0e1", "name": "Graceful-to-hard kill ladder", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Command-line Environment.md", "message": "The graceful-to-hard kill ladder", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "SIGINT from Ctrl-C and SIGTERM from kill are catchable, so a process can ignore them and Ctrl-C sometimes fails, whereas SIGKILL from kill -9 cannot be caught, always works, and skips cleanup."}
{"aliases": ["SIGHUP trap"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_1a3a453b94", "name": "SIGHUP kills background jobs with the terminal", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Command-line Environment.md", "message": "SIGHUP trap", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A backgrounded process is still a child of the terminal, so closing the terminal sends SIGHUP and kills it unless nohup or disown protects it."}
{"aliases": ["Per-command prefix (no export needed)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_4d22323361", "name": "Export versus per-command variable prefix", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Command-line Environment.md", "message": "Environment Variables", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "export puts a variable in the environment block that every child process inherits, whereas an assignment placed before a command sets that variable only in that single command's environment."}
{"aliases": ["short-circuiting"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_1dcc69fc37", "name": "Exit codes drive short-circuit operators", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Command-line Environment.md", "message": "Return Codes & Boolean Operators", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Exit code 0 means success and a nonzero code means failure, and the && and || operators short-circuit on those return codes rather than on values."}
{"aliases": ["the same keystroke means different things in different modes"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_ca437fdf42", "name": "Vim is a modal editor", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Editors (Vim).md", "message": "Why a modal editor", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Vim is a modal editor in which the same keystroke means different things in different modes, because navigating and editing existing text is constant while typing long new streams is rare."}
{"aliases": ["Vim's interface is a programming language"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_0675b9d81f", "name": "Vim commands compose as verbs and nouns", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Editors (Vim).md", "message": "Normal-mode keystroke categories", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Vim's Normal-mode interface is a programming language in which editing commands are verbs, movements are nouns, counts multiply motions, and the modifiers i and a select inside or around a text object."}
{"aliases": ["No 1:1 buffer↔window mapping"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_7c0e490b62", "name": "Buffer, window and tab in Vim", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Editors (Vim).md", "message": "Buffers, Windows & Tabs", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In Vim a buffer is an open file loaded in memory, a window is a view onto a buffer, and a tab is a collection of windows, so one buffer can be shown in two windows at once and closing a window with :q does not close the buffer."}
{"aliases": ["Vim everywhere (readline)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_7df377b7c7", "name": "Readline supports Vim keybindings", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Editors (Vim).md", "message": "Vim everywhere (readline)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "readline supports Vim emulation, so Vim keystrokes also work at the bash prompt, the Python REPL and other readline-based tools, reachable in bash via set -o vi or globally via set editing-mode vi in ~/.inputrc."}
{"aliases": ["Shell settings you type at the prompt do not survive"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_e1e616345f", "name": "Shell settings persist only via config files", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Shell Configuration & Dotfiles.md", "message": "Why shell configs need to persist", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Shell settings typed at the prompt do not survive logout or closing the shell; a setting persists only when it is written into the shell's configuration file, which the shell reads on startup — ~/.bashrc or ~/.bash_profile for bash."}
{"aliases": ["evaluates the file line by line"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_ea2746d95e", "name": "source evaluates a config in the current shell", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Shell Configuration & Dotfiles.md", "message": "source: apply a config without re-logging-in", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "source evaluates a configuration file line by line in the current shell, so an edited config takes effect in the running shell without starting a new one."}
{"aliases": ["non-interactive shells skip user startup files"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_56f1411e9f", "name": "Non-interactive shells skip user startup files", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Shell Configuration & Dotfiles.md", "message": "Startup modes: which configs actually run", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Non-interactive shells skip user startup files entirely, so cron jobs and non-interactive scripts do not load ~/.bashrc and their aliases vanish and $PATH can differ from an interactive terminal."}
{"aliases": ["the leading dot hides them from the default ls listing"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_ec18fe8397", "name": "Dotfiles hide from ls by convention", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Shell Configuration & Dotfiles.md", "message": "Dotfiles: the convention", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Dotfiles are plain-text configuration files whose leading dot hides them from the default ls listing, and they live in standard per-program locations such as ~/.bashrc, ~/.gitconfig and ~/.ssh/config."}
{"aliases": ["The dotfiles repo + symlink script is the standard \"portable environment\" setup."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_ba5692b572", "name": "Symlink script restores an environment", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Shell Configuration & Dotfiles.md", "message": "Symlink them into place", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Collecting dotfiles in one version-controlled folder and using an install script that creates symbolic links from each program's standard config location into that folder restores a familiar environment on a new machine without retyping settings."}
{"aliases": ["Child Process Memory Isolation"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_8cf5066237", "name": "Child process memory isolation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Shell.md", "message": "Why built-ins exist: Child Process Memory Isolation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An external command runs as a child process with its own copy of the parent's memory, and the operating system prevents the child from writing to the parent's memory, so any changes the child makes vanish when it exits."}
{"aliases": ["If a command needs to change the shell's own memory (variables, working directory, options), it must be a built-in."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_74fc84174e", "name": "Shell state changes require built-ins", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Shell.md", "message": "Shell Built-ins vs External Programs", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A command that must change the shell's own memory — the current working directory, variables, the background job list, or shell options — must be a shell built-in, because a child process cannot alter the parent shell's state."}
{"aliases": ["Pipes run in parallel, not in sequence"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_18e5f83508", "name": "Pipelines run concurrently, not in sequence", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Shell.md", "message": "Pipes & Pipeline Composition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Every command in a pipeline is started at once and runs concurrently with the others, so output streams through the pipeline as it is produced rather than waiting for each stage to finish."}
{"aliases": ["Redirections are evaluated left to right"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_0891956ef1", "name": "Redirection order determines destinations", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Shell.md", "message": "Why redirection order matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Redirections are evaluated left to right, so > \"$LOGFILE\" 2>&1 puts both stdout and stderr in the file, while 2>&1 > \"$LOGFILE\" leaves stderr pointing at the terminal because stderr was bound to where stdout pointed at that moment."}
{"aliases": ["set Flags (Strict Mode)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_d9057fdfc3", "name": "set -euo pipefail strict mode", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MIT Missing Semester — Shell.md", "message": "set Flags (Strict Mode)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "set -euo pipefail makes a script exit immediately on any failing command (-e), abort on undefined variables (-u), and fail a whole pipeline when any stage fails (-o pipefail)."}
{"aliases": ["what parameters make the data I saw most likely?"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_6e920d9b31", "name": "MLE maximizes the likelihood", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MLE vs MAP Estimation.md", "message": "MLE — Maximum Likelihood Estimation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Maximum likelihood estimation chooses the parameters that maximize P(data|parameters); for discrete counts this reduces to relative frequency, and with no prior it overfits on small data."}
{"aliases": ["P(parameters|data) ∝ P(data|parameters) · P(parameters)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_512f0bf805", "name": "MAP maximizes the posterior", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MLE vs MAP Estimation.md", "message": "MAP — Maximum A Posteriori", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Maximum a posteriori estimation chooses the parameters that maximize P(parameters|data), which by Bayes' theorem is proportional to P(data|parameters)·P(parameters) and therefore adds a prior over plausible parameter values."}
{"aliases": ["Every regularization term is a Bayesian statement"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_793399ac6f", "name": "Priors correspond to regularization penalties", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MLE vs MAP Estimation.md", "message": "The Regularization Connection", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A Gaussian prior on weights corresponds to L2 (ridge) regularization and a Laplace prior to L1 (lasso), so weight_decay in Adam, C in a linear SVM and alpha in ridge regression are priors in disguise."}
{"aliases": ["With massive data the likelihood overwhelms any prior and MLE ≈ MAP"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_3ac0d02845", "name": "Prior dominates with scarce data", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MLE vs MAP Estimation.md", "message": "When the Prior Matters Most", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "With scarce data the prior carries the signal in a MAP estimate, while with massive data the likelihood overwhelms any prior so MLE and MAP agree, which is why large-scale training is effectively frequentist."}
{"aliases": ["the prior pulls the estimate back toward 0.5"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_1035bf5708", "name": "Coin-flip MLE versus Beta MAP", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/MLE vs MAP Estimation.md", "message": "Worked Example", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For 10 coin flips with 7 heads, MLE gives a bias of 7/10 = 0.7 while a Beta(2,2) prior gives MAP (7+2-1)/(10+2+2-2) = 8/12 ≈ 0.667, so the prior pulls the estimate back toward 0.5."}
{"aliases": ["single-line factory methods"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_b602ae681f", "name": "Java 9 collection factory methods", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Modern Java Collection Factory Methods.md", "message": "Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Java 9+ provides List.of(), Set.of(), Map.of() and Map.ofEntries() as single-line factory methods that create and populate immutable collections, making static initializer blocks unnecessary for simple initialization."}
{"aliases": ["10 key-value pairs", "Larger maps (unlimited entries)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_c430ba0727", "name": "Map.of caps at ten pairs", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Modern Java Collection Factory Methods.md", "message": "Available Methods", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Map.of accepts at most 10 key-value pairs, whereas Map.ofEntries with Map.entry accepts an unlimited number of entries."}
{"aliases": ["you cannot add, remove, or modify elements after creation"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_39418df4eb", "name": "Factory collections are immutable", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Modern Java Collection Factory Methods.md", "message": "Important: Immutability", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Collections returned by List.of, Set.of, Map.of and Map.ofEntries are immutable, so elements cannot be added, removed or modified after creation and a mutable collection still requires new HashMap<>() and possibly a static block."}
{"aliases": ["You still need a static block if"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_7fb32ec0f9", "name": "When static blocks remain necessary", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Modern Java Collection Factory Methods.md", "message": "When This Replaces Static Blocks", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A static initializer block is still required when initialization needs mutable collections, involves loops, conditionals or error handling, or configures complex objects over multiple setup steps."}
{"aliases": ["v ← β v + g", "w ← w − η v"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_80b81fcfde", "name": "Momentum update rule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Momentum (SGD with Momentum).md", "message": "Momentum (SGD with Momentum)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Momentum accumulates past gradients into a velocity term, updating v to beta*v + g and then w to w - eta*v, with beta typically 0.9 so that 90% of the last step's velocity is kept and the new gradient is added."}
{"aliases": ["Momentum is a selective accumulator"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_361c0b3371", "name": "Momentum as selective accumulator", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Momentum (SGD with Momentum).md", "message": "Why it works (beyond the ball analogy)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In a narrow valley the cross-valley gradient oscillates in sign so opposite-sign contributions cancel in the velocity sum, while the small but consistent along-valley gradient accumulates, making momentum a selective accumulator that keeps what is consistent and averages away what zigzags."}
{"aliases": ["it gives you a direction-dependent step size"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_6933a0d52b", "name": "Momentum gives direction-dependent step size", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Momentum (SGD with Momentum).md", "message": "Eigenvalue & step-size interpretation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Momentum rescales the effective step size per eigenvector direction of the Hessian, taking larger steps along low-curvature directions and keeping steps small across steep high-curvature walls, which balances convergence speeds across directions."}
{"aliases": ["The first step from zero velocity is identical to plain GD"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_1e96432fc6", "name": "Momentum's first step equals plain GD", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Momentum (SGD with Momentum).md", "message": "Notes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The first momentum step from zero velocity is identical to a plain gradient descent step, so momentum's advantage compounds over subsequent steps."}
{"aliases": ["momentum converged first (~2941 steps), beating Adam (~6156 steps)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_693bc6e24d", "name": "Momentum beat Adam on Rosenbrock", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Momentum (SGD with Momentum).md", "message": "Rosenbrock race result (CP3, 2026-09-08)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "On the Rosenbrock function from (-1,1) at learning rate 0.0005, SGD with momentum converged in about 2941 steps while Adam needed about 6156, and vanilla gradient descent never converged."}
{"aliases": ["multiple pathways that merge"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_dcac2ca63f", "name": "Multi-input models are merging pathways", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Multi-Input & Multi-Modal Models.md", "message": "Pattern", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A multi-input model consists of multiple pathways that each extract features from one data type, whose outputs are concatenated and then passed to a classifier."}
{"aliases": ["Combines image features + tabular patient data for diagnosis"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_81f7c9c3a1", "name": "Image plus tabular multimodal diagnosis model", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Multi-Input & Multi-Modal Models.md", "message": "a cervical cancer diagnosis might combine a Pap smear image with patient tabular data", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A multi-modal diagnosis model can pair a CNN pathway over a Pap smear image with an MLP pathway over tabular patient data such as age, HPV status and previous screenings, concatenating the image and tabular feature vectors before a final linear classifier."}
{"aliases": ["f(x) = Σ (1/k!) D_x^k f(x_0) δ^k"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_0adbd3afa9", "name": "Compact tensor form of multivariate Taylor series", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Multivariate Taylor series.md", "message": "the compact tensor form is", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For a smooth scalar function f from R^n to R expanded at x0 with displacement delta = x - x0, the multivariate Taylor series is the sum over k of (1/k!) times the k-th derivative tensor of f at x0 applied to the k-fold outer product of delta, contracted over all matching indices to a scalar."}
{"aliases": ["the local slope or tangent-plane approximation"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_eb70c00030", "name": "Gradient term is the tangent plane", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Multivariate Taylor series.md", "message": "Geometric meaning", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In the multivariate Taylor expansion the k=1 term is the gradient at the expansion point applied to the displacement, which is the local slope, i.e. the tangent-plane approximation to the function near that point."}
{"aliases": ["the local curvature along the direction δ"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_76e63e86ca", "name": "Hessian term is local curvature", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Multivariate Taylor series.md", "message": "Geometric meaning", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In the multivariate Taylor expansion the k=2 term is the displacement transposed times the Hessian at the expansion point times the displacement, which is the local curvature of the function along that direction."}
{"aliases": ["T1: a linear approximation, which keeps the value and gradient only"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_00daef568b", "name": "Taylor truncation: linear versus quadratic", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Multivariate Taylor series.md", "message": "Truncation", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The degree-n Taylor polynomial truncates the multivariate series at k=n, so T_1 keeps only the value and gradient for a linear approximation while T_2 additionally keeps curvature through the Hessian for a quadratic approximation."}
{"aliases": ["gradient descent behaves like it is following a local plane"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:20:58Z", "id": "n_58f87fe530", "name": "GD follows a plane, Newton a quadratic bowl", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Multivariate Taylor series.md", "message": "This is why gradient descent behaves like it is following a local plane, while Newton's method uses the Hessian to fit a local quadratic bowl.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because the first-order Taylor polynomial keeps only value and gradient while the second-order one adds Hessian curvature, gradient descent behaves as if it follows a local plane while Newton's method fits a local quadratic bowl."}
{"aliases": ["splitting text into tokens (words, subwords, or characters) and mapping each to an integer ID"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_bb9a0388a2", "name": "Text tokenization maps tokens to integer IDs", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Text Tokenization.md", "message": "What It Is", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Text tokenization is the first step in making text understandable to machines: it splits text into tokens (words, subwords, or characters) and maps each token to an integer ID."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_837f5daad1", "name": "Word-level tokenization inflates agglutinative vocabularies", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Text Tokenization.md", "message": "Three Levels", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Word-level tokenization via text.split() is simple but produces huge vocabularies for agglutinative languages."}
{"aliases": ["best balance for morphologically rich languages"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_2b0b342268", "name": "Subword tokenization balances vocabulary and meaning", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Text Tokenization.md", "message": "Three Levels", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Subword tokenization (BPE, SentencePiece) splits text into meaningful chunks such as \"omu\" + \"lwadde\" and is the best balance for morphologically rich languages."}
{"aliases": ["tiny vocabulary (~50-200 chars), can generate any word, but sequences are ~6× longer"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_ae01a849ac", "name": "Character-level tokenization trades sequence length for coverage", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Text Tokenization.md", "message": "Three Levels", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Character-level tokenization uses a tiny vocabulary of roughly 50-200 characters and can generate any word, but produces sequences about 6 times longer."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_fdc23d0e2c", "name": "Luganda is agglutinative", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Text Tokenization.md", "message": "Why It Matters for African Languages", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Luganda is an agglutinative language: the word \"omulwadde\" decomposes as \"omu\" (person) plus \"lwadde\" (sick), so word-level tokenization explodes its vocabulary and subword or character-level tokenization is usually better."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_8d7baed7e6", "name": "tmux has a session-window-pane hierarchy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/tmux — Sessions, Windows & Panes.md", "message": "The three-level hierarchy", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "tmux organizes terminals in three levels: a session is an independent workspace holding one or more windows, a window fills the terminal viewport like a tab, and a pane is a split screen within a single window."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_b0c3d4e760", "name": "tmux sessions survive disconnects", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/tmux — Sessions, Windows & Panes.md", "message": "Sessions survive disconnects.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Detaching a tmux session leaves everything running server-side, so the session survives SSH drops, laptop sleep, or accidental window closes and can be reattached later."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_0900d1c822", "name": "tmux panes allow concurrent terminal views", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/tmux — Sessions, Windows & Panes.md", "message": "Why it matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "tmux panes let several terminal views be visible at once within one window, so a build can be watched in one split while editing in another without alt-tabbing."}
{"aliases": ["the abstract unit of currency in compiler design"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_e98dad023b", "name": "Token is the compiler's unit of currency", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Token.md", "message": "A token is the abstract unit of currency in compiler design", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A token is the abstract unit of currency in compiler design: a package bundling a raw text string with its identity and location, produced by the lexer and consumed by the parser."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_652f17461d", "name": "Token bundles type, lexeme, and location", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Token.md", "message": "Components", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A token bundles three components: a token type or class (such as IDENTIFIER, KEYWORD, or INTEGER_LITERAL), the lexeme value (the raw source text), and metadata (line number, column number, and source file) used for error reporting."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_2c2214cb5b", "name": "Lexer converts value lexemes to literal objects", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Token.md", "message": "Literal Value", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For value-typed tokens such as numbers and strings, the lexer also converts the lexeme into a live runtime literal (for example lexeme \"42\" to integer 42), so the compiler or interpreter does not re-parse the text later."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_586e041e1f", "name": "Backward pass needs all gradient contributions first", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Topological Sort for Backprop.md", "message": "The backward pass must process nodes in an order where each node receives all incoming gradient contributions before it propagates to its own children.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The backward pass must process each node in an order where the node has received all incoming gradient contributions before it propagates gradient to its own children, and topological sort guarantees such an order."}
{"aliases": ["topo = [inputs_first, ..., outputs_last]"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_ab6b5054c6", "name": "Post-order DFS yields inputs-first topological order", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Topological Sort for Backprop.md", "message": "Algorithm", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A DFS post-order traversal from the output node that appends each node after its children produces a topological list ordered inputs first and outputs last."}
{"aliases": ["reversed(topo) = [outputs_first, ..., inputs_last]"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_965b68822f", "name": "Reversed topological order drives the backward pass", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Topological Sort for Backprop.md", "message": "Why reverse?", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Reversing the topological list gives outputs first and inputs last, which is exactly the order in which the backward pass steps through the nodes calling each node's _backward()."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_b0ee70a3bd", "name": "Out-of-order backprop loses gradient contributions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Topological Sort for Backprop.md", "message": "Why it matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For c = a*a + a, processing b before a would push b's gradient into a before a receives its direct contribution from c's +a, losing half the gradient; topological sort prevents this."}
{"aliases": ["dF = F_x\\,dx + F_y\\,dy"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_4eaafda1d3", "name": "Total differential of a scalar function", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Total differential.md", "message": "The total differential is the linearized change of a multivariable scalar function.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The total differential of a multivariable scalar function F(x,y) is dF = F_x dx + F_y dy, the first-order approximation to how the output changes when the inputs change a little."}
{"aliases": ["dF=0"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_b419fbd296", "name": "Total differential vanishes on a level set", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Total differential.md", "message": "Level sets", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "On a level set where F(x,y) = c, the value of F stays constant, so the total differential satisfies dF = 0."}
{"aliases": ["dS = 2x\\,dx + 2y\\,dy"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_07af6f6285", "name": "Total differential of x squared plus y squared", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Total differential.md", "message": "Example", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For S(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 the total differential is dS = 2x dx + 2y dy, and staying on the circle S = 25 forces dS = 0 so the input changes must satisfy that first-order balance."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_1c5f20dd87", "name": "Total differential is a Jacobian row", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Total differential.md", "message": "Connection to Jacobians", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For a scalar function, the total differential is the Jacobian row applied to the input displacement vector, and the Jacobian matrix generalizes the same idea to vector-valued functions."}
{"aliases": ["thin, end-to-end slices of functionality"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_88016a6a83", "name": "Tracer bullet is an end-to-end slice", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Tracer Bullets.md", "message": "A metaphor from The Pragmatic Programmer", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A tracer bullet is a thin, end-to-end slice of functionality that gives visibility into the final system, named after tracer rounds whose visible trail shows where shots are landing."}
{"aliases": ["People find it easier to get on board with an ongoing success."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_e14f08f0f8", "name": "Working slice beats a perfect plan", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Tracer Bullets.md", "message": "Core ideas", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A working end-to-end slice, even a minimal one, is more convincing than a perfect plan: it rallies support, clarifies requirements, and exposes problems early."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_8bf042c679", "name": "Tracer bullets are kept, prototypes are discarded", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Tracer Bullets.md", "message": "Tracer bullets vs Prototypes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Tracer bullets are meant to be kept and built upon as the skeleton of the real system, whereas prototypes are exploratory throwaway code meant to answer questions and then be discarded."}
{"aliases": ["Zero → Forward → Loss → Backward → Update"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_e331b7de72", "name": "Five-step training loop", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Training Loop Pattern.md", "message": "The 5-Step Mantra", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The training loop follows the five-step order zero gradients, forward pass, loss, backward pass, and weight update, and every Marconi Lab model runs on this same loop."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_8dc818a69d", "name": "Unzeroed gradients accumulate across batches", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Training Loop Pattern.md", "message": "ZERO gradients (or they accumulate)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "optimizer.zero_grad() resets gradients from the previous batch; skipping it lets gradients accumulate, effectively enlarging the batch size and possibly producing NaN."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_7bc875df0e", "name": "Backward computes gradients and step applies them", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Training Loop Pattern.md", "message": "Why Each Step Matters", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "loss.backward() computes the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight, and optimizer.step() updates the weights using those gradients."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_91d51396e5", "name": "No loss means no learning signal", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Training Loop Pattern.md", "message": "Loss | Measures prediction error | No signal to guide learning", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Skipping the loss computation leaves no signal to guide learning."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_9c1e854a8e", "name": "No backward or step means no weight update", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Training Loop Pattern.md", "message": "What Happens If You Skip It", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Skipping backward() leaves the weights without gradients, and skipping step() leaves computed gradients unused, so in either case the weights never update."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_7dcab97baf", "name": "Text transfer learning initializes embeddings from pretrained vectors", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Transfer Learning for Text.md", "message": "What It Is", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Transfer learning for text initializes the nn.Embedding layer with pre-trained word embeddings such as GloVe or FastText instead of random vectors, mirroring transfer learning for images."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_acde2c7aca", "name": "FastText uses subword information", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Transfer Learning for Text.md", "message": "Available Pre-trained Embeddings", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "FastText handles subword information, which makes it especially good for morphologically rich languages."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_998a81d6ee", "name": "Freezing pretrained embeddings after copying vectors", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Transfer Learning for Text.md", "message": "How to Load Them", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Loading pre-trained embeddings copies each vocabulary word's vector into the embedding weight at that word's index and then sets requires_grad = False to freeze the layer, as in image transfer learning."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_00d8b383ae", "name": "African language embedding gap", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Transfer Learning for Text.md", "message": "The African Language Gap", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Pre-trained embeddings exist for Swahili via FastText but are limited for Luganda, Runyankole, and Acholi, for which the alternatives are training embeddings from scratch on available corpora or using multilingual models such as mBERT, XLM-R, or AfroLM."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_2f853acb0f", "name": "Fine-tune ImageNet models instead of training from scratch", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Transfer Learning (PyTorch).md", "message": "take a model pre-trained on ImageNet (1.2M images) and fine-tune it on your small medical/agricultural dataset", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Instead of training a CNN from scratch, which needs millions of images, transfer learning takes a model pre-trained on ImageNet (1.2M images) and fine-tunes it on a small medical or agricultural dataset."}
{"aliases": ["Early CNN layers learn universal visual features"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_55a1923e22", "name": "Early CNN layers learn universal visual features", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Transfer Learning (PyTorch).md", "message": "Why It Works", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Early CNN layers learn universal visual features — edges, textures, shapes, and color patterns — that transfer across domains, so only the final layers need to adapt to a specific task."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_06e7f34302", "name": "PyTorch transfer-learning recipe freezes and replaces the head", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Transfer Learning (PyTorch).md", "message": "The Recipe", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The PyTorch transfer-learning recipe loads a pre-trained ResNet, sets requires_grad = False on all parameters to freeze the ImageNet knowledge, replaces the final classification layer, and trains only the new classifier."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_15293cf68d", "name": "Transfer learning leaves the training loop unchanged", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Transfer Learning (PyTorch).md", "message": "The training loop from Day 1 stays the same — only the model and data change.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In transfer learning the training loop stays the same as in ordinary training; only the model and the data change."}
{"aliases": ["source-to-source compiler"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_afe8ceb2c1", "name": "Transpiler translates between high-level languages", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Transpiler.md", "message": "A transpiler (source-to-source compiler) translates code from one high-level language to another high-level language", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A transpiler (source-to-source compiler) translates code from one high-level language to another high-level language rather than to machine code."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_27a23a1e8d", "name": "Transpiler examples", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Transpiler.md", "message": "Examples", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "TypeScript to JavaScript is the most common transpiler case, where the transpiler strips the types and outputs plain JavaScript; SCSS to CSS and Babel's ES6+ to ES5 are further examples."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:22Z", "id": "n_e009595951", "name": "Transpiler versus compiler output level", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Transpiler.md", "message": "Transpiler vs Compiler", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A compiler outputs machine code or bytecode and lowers the abstraction level, whereas a transpiler outputs another high-level language at roughly the same abstraction level."}
{"aliases": ["Converts raw logits into a probability distribution"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_b3c6d5aedc", "name": "Softmax: exponentiate then normalize", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Softmax Function.md", "message": "Converts raw logits into a probability distribution. Two steps", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The softmax function converts raw logits into a probability distribution in two steps: exponentiating every logit so all values become positive, then dividing each row by its own sum so each row sums to 1."}
{"aliases": ["it amplifies differences — large logits get much larger probabilities"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_d1ef256798", "name": "Exponentiation positivity and amplification", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Softmax Function.md", "message": "**Why exponentiate?**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Exponentiating logits guarantees positive values for any real input and amplifies differences, so large logits receive much larger probabilities."}
{"aliases": ["Without it, broadcasting normalizes columns instead of rows"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_402ed0e755", "name": "keepdim omission normalizes columns", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Softmax Function.md", "message": "**Why `keepdim=True`?**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Omitting keepdim=True when normalizing a batch of logits makes broadcasting divide by column sums instead of row sums, silently producing wrong probabilities."}
{"aliases": ["does both steps in one numerically stable call"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_f9d0f87668", "name": "F.softmax bundles both steps stably", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Softmax Function.md", "message": "In practice, `F.softmax(logits, dim=1)` does both steps in one numerically stable call.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "F.softmax(logits, dim=1) performs the exponentiate-and-normalize steps in one numerically stable call, whereas writing the steps out manually reveals the structure of softmax."}
{"aliases": ["exp(x) overflows when x > ~709 in float64"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_e9ba15c381", "name": "exp overflow threshold in float64", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Softmax Subtract-Max Trick.md", "message": "## The Problem", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In float64 arithmetic, exp(x) overflows once x exceeds roughly 709."}
{"aliases": ["Max cancels in numerator/denominator."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_fd24ce35dc", "name": "Subtract-max identity for softmax", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Softmax Subtract-Max Trick.md", "message": "## The Fix", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Subtracting max(z) from every component before exponentiating gives mathematically identical softmax values, because the max factor cancels between numerator and denominator: softmax(z_i) = exp(z_i − max(z)) / Σ exp(z_j − max(z))."}
{"aliases": ["Subtract max(z) before exp to prevent float overflow. Mathematically identical."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_0d75a31c7f", "name": "Subtract-max prevents overflow, same output", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Softmax Subtract-Max Trick.md", "message": "Subtract max(z) before exp to prevent float overflow. Mathematically identical.", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Subtracting the maximum value before exponentiating prevents floating-point overflow in softmax without changing the computed result."}
{"aliases": ["Does NOT force sum to 1. Purely numerical stability."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_c1712abd23", "name": "Subtract-max is not normalization", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Softmax Subtract-Max Trick.md", "message": "## What It Does NOT Do", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The subtract-max trick provides numerical stability only; it does not by itself force the softmax outputs to sum to 1."}
{"aliases": ["DRY is about duplication of *knowledge*, not duplication of code."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_8a11361cb1", "name": "DRY is about knowledge, not code", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Software Engineering Philosophy.md", "message": "## 8. DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) targets duplication of knowledge rather than duplication of code."}
{"aliases": ["localize the violation — hide the duplicated data inside one class that manages consistency"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_f3b8a5bbfa", "name": "Localize deliberate DRY violations", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Software Engineering Philosophy.md", "message": "**Breaking DRY intentionally:**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When duplication is introduced deliberately for performance, such as caching an expensive computation, the DRY violation should be localized inside a single class that manages consistency."}
{"aliases": ["Don't expose implementation details. Expose behavior."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_8fc9383153", "name": "Information hiding exposes behavior", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Software Engineering Philosophy.md", "message": "## 9. Information Hiding", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Information hiding means a module exposes behavior rather than implementation details, so its internals can change without forcing changes in callers."}
{"aliases": ["Users of a module shouldn't know whether data comes from memory, computation, cache, or database."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_189a9a1fae", "name": "Uniform Access Principle", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Software Engineering Philosophy.md", "message": "**Uniform Access Principle**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Under the Uniform Access Principle, users of a module cannot tell whether a value comes from memory, computation, a cache, or a database, so the interface stays the same while the implementation stays flexible."}
{"aliases": ["Good design is about adaptability, not beauty."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_2321a93253", "name": "ETC: design is adaptability", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Software Engineering Philosophy.md", "message": "### ETC — Easier To Change", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "ETC (Easier To Change) holds that good design is about adaptability rather than beauty, so every design decision should make future changes cheaper."}
{"aliases": ["%n reports characters consumed in the format match, not where the line ends"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_320f2c6cc1", "name": "%n reports match progress, not line end", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/sscanf %n & Line Advancement.md", "message": "## Trap 2: %n Reports Match Progress, Not Lines", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The %n conversion in sscanf reports how many characters the format match has consumed up to that point, not where the current line ends."}
{"aliases": ["Data is dropped without an error."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_d561153ce1", "name": "strchr is not line-aware", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/sscanf %n & Line Advancement.md", "message": "## Trap 1: strchr Is Not Line-Aware", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "strchr(line, '\\n') returns the first newline anywhere ahead in the remaining buffer rather than the newline ending the current line, so a newline missing mid-buffer makes the scan silently jump over unread lines and drop data without an error."}
{"aliases": ["on \"MemTotal: 1000 kB\", `consumed` stops right after `1000`, leaving \" kB\" behind"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_fcbe56b2a3", "name": "Trailing unit left behind by %lu", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/sscanf %n & Line Advancement.md", "message": "`%lu` stops at the first non-digit", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because %lu stops at the first non-digit, a format string that omits the trailing unit leaves text such as \" kB\" behind, and advancing the pointer by the %n count makes the next sscanf match a garbage key spanning the newline (since %[^:] accepts newlines), silently losing the value."}
{"aliases": ["the format string must consume the trailing unit"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_508a994b74", "name": "Advance by whole lines or full patterns", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/sscanf %n & Line Advancement.md", "message": "**Fix:** match the full line pattern so `%n` captures everything", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A line-based sscanf parser must either consume the trailing unit inside the format string (for example \"%31[^:]: %lu kB%n\") so %n captures the whole line, or advance whole lines with strchr instead of by %n-consumed bytes."}
{"aliases": ["a **challenge–response** proof that you hold the private key, without ever sending the key"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_87fe284b5e", "name": "SSH challenge-response authentication", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/SSH — Public-Key Auth & Remote Commands.md", "message": "## The Key-Based Authentication Handshake (step-by-step)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "SSH key-based authentication is a challenge-response exchange: the server sends a random value, the client signs it with its private key, and the server verifies that signature with the client's public key stored in ~/.ssh/authorized_keys."}
{"aliases": ["the real SSH handshake is **sign / verify**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_5e8dd37651", "name": "SSH handshake is sign/verify", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/SSH — Public-Key Auth & Remote Commands.md", "message": "**Mechanism note (corrected):**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The real SSH handshake proves key possession by signing and verifying a challenge, not by encrypting with the public key and decrypting with the private key."}
{"aliases": ["the private key never leaves the client"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_2618eb311f", "name": "SSH private key never crosses the wire", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/SSH — Public-Key Auth & Remote Commands.md", "message": "The private key is **never transmitted**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In SSH public-key authentication the private key never leaves the client; only a signature proving possession of the private key travels over the network."}
{"aliases": ["without one, your private key is just a **plain-text file**"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_c89d37a74d", "name": "Passphrase encrypts the private key file", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/SSH — Public-Key Auth & Remote Commands.md", "message": "**Add a passphrase to the private key.**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "An SSH key passphrase encrypts the private key file itself; without a passphrase the private key is a plain-text file that anyone or any malware with file access can copy and immediately use."}
{"aliases": ["Quoting decides where the pipe runs"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_831f70eeec", "name": "Quoting decides where an SSH pipe runs", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/SSH — Public-Key Auth & Remote Commands.md", "message": "**Quoting changes WHERE the pipe runs:**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "In ssh host cmd, an unquoted pipe is interpreted by the local shell so only the first command runs remotely, while a single-quoted pipeline is sent whole and executes entirely on the remote machine."}
{"aliases": ["controlled, predictable sample data used as test input"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_4ad5d410dd", "name": "Fixture as fixed test contract", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Static Fixtures & Boundary Cases.md", "message": "## Overview", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A fixture is controlled, predictable sample data used as test input, which turns a moving target such as live OS text into a fixed contract: given this exact text, the output must be that exact value."}
{"aliases": ["prove it fails cleanly instead of crashing or corrupting"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_847bbcc859", "name": "Boundary fixtures prove clean failure", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Static Fixtures & Boundary Cases.md", "message": "## Boundary Fixture Classes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Boundary and adversarial fixtures deliberately push a parser outside its happy path, covering format drift, malformed input, extreme values, empty input, and cross-platform variants, to prove it fails cleanly rather than crashing or corrupting memory."}
{"aliases": ["Fixture tests (deterministic)", "Smoke tests (live/system)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_15d3d952b3", "name": "Fixture tests vs smoke tests", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Static Fixtures & Boundary Cases.md", "message": "## Fixture Tests vs Smoke Tests (2026-08-15)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Fixture tests feed known static text to the pure parser and assert exact values, while smoke tests run through the I/O layer against the live system file and assert only range or sanity bounds, because live values fluctuate between reads."}
{"aliases": ["available_kb <= total_kb is the true invariant"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_7ae4797514", "name": "meminfo physical invariants", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Static Fixtures & Boundary Cases.md", "message": "Boundary lessons from this pattern:", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For /proc/meminfo the meaningful invariants are total_kb > 0, which guards a downstream divide-by-zero in percentage math, and available_kb <= total_kb, where strict < is brittle because equality is reachable on fresh or controlled systems."}
{"aliases": ["total_kb >= 0 is *always true* by definition"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_ea5705299c", "name": "Unsigned >= 0 guard is vacuous", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Static Fixtures & Boundary Cases.md", "message": "**Avoid the redundant check:**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because total_kb is an unsigned long, a total_kb >= 0 check is always true by definition and passes even when the value is accidentally 0, so it is a useless guard."}
{"aliases": ["defines a family of interchangeable algorithms"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_bba9efc85c", "name": "Strategy Pattern definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Strategy Pattern.md", "message": "## Definition", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Strategy Pattern is a Gang-of-Four behavioral design pattern that defines a family of interchangeable algorithms, encapsulates each one in its own class, and makes them interchangeable through a common interface."}
{"aliases": ["maybe they share common code at the start and end, but each has a different central algorithm"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_66faa6aa52", "name": "Shared scaffolding yields duplicate code", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Strategy Pattern.md", "message": "## The problem it solves", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "When functions share common code at the start and end but differ in their central algorithm, copy-pasting the shared scaffolding around each variant produces duplicate code and is brittle."}
{"aliases": ["Extract the varying algorithm into a common interface"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_98e6aeadf3", "name": "How Strategy removes duplication", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Strategy Pattern.md", "message": "## How Strategy Pattern fixes it", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Strategy removes duplicated scaffolding by extracting the varying algorithm into a common interface, giving each variant its own concrete class, and having the context hold a strategy reference and delegate to it, so strategies can be swapped at runtime."}
{"aliases": ["A class has a large conditional (if/switch) picking between behaviors"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_12b1ea8673", "name": "When to use Strategy", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Strategy Pattern.md", "message": "## When to use", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Strategy is appropriate when multiple algorithms differ only in behavior and not structure, when algorithms must switch at runtime, when a class has a large if/switch choosing among behaviors, or when algorithm-specific data structures should not be exposed."}
{"aliases": ["multi-dimensional array of numbers"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_d1fbd1854f", "name": "Tensor as universal data container", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Tensors (PyTorch).md", "message": "A tensor is a multi-dimensional array of numbers", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A tensor is a multi-dimensional array of numbers and serves as the universal data container of deep learning, since images, text, audio, and tabular data all become tensors before a model processes them."}
{"aliases": ["`[batch, time, features]`"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_93e933da0b", "name": "Common tensor shape patterns", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Tensors (PyTorch).md", "message": "## Common Shapes by Domain", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Standard tensor shapes by domain are [batch, features] for tabular data, [batch, 1, H, W] for grayscale images, [batch, 3, H, W] for color images, [batch, time, features] for sequences, and [batch, seq_len] for tokenized text."}
{"aliases": ["PyTorch uses **channels-first** (NCHW) format"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_36214fba4e", "name": "PyTorch is channels-first", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Tensors (PyTorch).md", "message": "PyTorch uses **channels-first** (NCHW) format", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "PyTorch represents image tensors in channels-first (NCHW) layout, unlike TensorFlow's channels-last (NHWC) layout."}
{"aliases": ["random numbers gradually adjusted into something meaningful"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_a98bb9bab5", "name": "torch.randn initializes weights", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Tensors (PyTorch).md", "message": "`torch.randn()` is how most neural network weights start", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "torch.randn produces random normal values with mean 0 and standard deviation 1, which is how most neural network weights begin before training gradually adjusts them into something meaningful."}
{"aliases": ["deliberate boundary in your code"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_964ecb9e4a", "name": "Testable seam definition", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Testable Seam.md", "message": "## Overview", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A testable seam is a deliberate boundary in code where a real, unpredictable dependency such as a live OS file can be replaced by a controlled input during tests."}
{"aliases": ["The test outcome depends on machine state, not code correctness."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_5d24b14315", "name": "Flaky test depends on environment", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Testable Seam.md", "message": "## Why It Exists", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A test is flaky when its outcome depends on machine state rather than code correctness, as with an assertion on a live free-RAM value that changes every millisecond."}
{"aliases": ["Split the job in two"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_f059d921bd", "name": "Split fetching from parsing", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Testable Seam.md", "message": "## The Pattern", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The testable-seam pattern splits a job into a fetch part that reads the changing value from the live source as a side effect, and a parse part that turns raw text into structured data as pure logic, with the seam at the boundary between them."}
{"aliases": ["Side effects belong in their own layer, separate from logic"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_12b8c2775a", "name": "Side effects get their own layer", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Testable Seam.md", "message": "## Key Insight", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Side effects belong in their own layer, separate from logic, so that the logic can be tested in isolation as an isolated unit."}
{"aliases": ["a test failure means the parser is wrong — nothing else"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:35Z", "id": "n_de1ab5941f", "name": "Fixture on the pure parser removes the moving target", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Testable Seam.md", "message": "Feed the parser a fixed, static piece of sample text", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Feeding a fixed static fixture to the pure parser removes the moving target, so that a test failure means the parser is wrong rather than the environment being different."}
{"aliases": ["maps high-dimensional data to 2D (or 3D) while preserving which points are near each other", "Points that were neighbors in 784 dimensions stay neighbors in 2D."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_1114385867", "name": "t-SNE matches pair-neighbour distributions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/t-SNE (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "The goal, in the source's words / \"in the original space, compute a probability distribution over pairs of points based on their distances\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "t-SNE embeds high-dimensional data into 2D or 3D by computing a probability distribution over pairs of points in the original space, where near points get high probability and far points low probability, and then searching for a low-dimensional arrangement in which that same pair distribution holds."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_e8079ff228", "name": "Linear projection invents false neighbours", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/t-SNE (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "Why a linear squash invents false neighbours (CP4 mini 1, 2026-10-08)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A linear projection can only shrink or preserve pairwise distances and can never enlarge them, so flattening a curved manifold never tears true neighbours apart but does collapse distinct regions onto each other, turning points that were far apart into false neighbours."}
{"aliases": ["a guess about the number of close neighbors each point has", "Controls the **effective** number of neighbors each point considers"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_dec34c759f", "name": "t-SNE perplexity is 2^H of one point's neighbour distribution", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/t-SNE (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "Perplexity = `2^H` on one point's neighbour distribution (CP4 mini 2, 2026-10-09)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Each point in t-SNE carries its own neighbour distribution P_i over the other n−1 points, with perplexity Per(P_i) = 2^{H(P_i)} and H(P_i) = −Σ_j p_{j|i} log₂ p_{j|i}; the algorithm binary-searches that point's bandwidth σ_i until its perplexity equals the single user-set knob, so perplexity is an effective, soft neighbour count rather than a hard cutoff at k neighbours."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_68827b06c5", "name": "t-SNE minimizes KL(P‖Q) with P frozen", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/t-SNE (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "The objective: minimize KL(P‖Q) (CP4 mini 3, 2026-10-09)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "t-SNE is trained by minimizing KL(P‖Q) = Σ_i Σ_j P_ij log(P_ij/Q_ij) over the low-dimensional coordinates, with the high-dimensional pair distribution P frozen as the data; because the weights in the sum come from P, 'data says near, map says far' is the expensive mismatch while 'data says far, map says near' is comparatively cheap."}
{"aliases": ["mismatched tails [to] compensate for mismatched dimensionalities"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_120cfa1d7a", "name": "Student-t low-dimensional kernel relieves crowding", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/t-SNE (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "Why the low-dimensional kernel is a heavy-tailed Student-t (CP4 mini 3b, 2026-10-09)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "t-SNE's low-dimensional kernel is a Student-t with one degree of freedom, q_ij ∝ 1/(1 + d_ij²), whose polynomial tail keeps Q_ij from collapsing toward zero at distance, so the P-weighted log-ratio cost of pushing crowded moderately related pairs apart stays affordable while pairs with genuinely large P_ij still pay to be separated."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_cd68da99bf", "name": "Forward pass computes values and records the graph", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Two-Pass Autodiff Algorithm.md", "message": "1. **Forward Pass**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The forward pass of autodiff computes every node's value in topological order from inputs to output and builds the computational graph implicitly by recording which values produced which outputs."}
{"aliases": ["Seed `output.grad = 1.0` (since ∂output/∂output = 1)"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_09981b6210", "name": "Backward pass multiplies local derivative by upstream gradient", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Two-Pass Autodiff Algorithm.md", "message": "2. **Backward Pass**", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The backward pass seeds output.grad = 1.0 and walks the graph in reverse order, and at each node computes grad_to_children = local_derivative × upstream_gradient."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_b0df72a131", "name": "Two-pass autodiff is the universal backprop algorithm", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Two-Pass Autodiff Algorithm.md", "message": "\"This is the universal algorithm behind backpropagation.\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The forward-then-reverse two-pass procedure, computing values forward and then propagating local derivative times upstream gradient backward, is the universal algorithm behind backpropagation."}
{"aliases": ["The data is uniformly distributed on Riemannian manifold;", "The embedding is found by searching for a low dimensional projection of the data that has the closest possible equivalent fuzzy topological structure."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_03f7e66960", "name": "UMAP's three manifold assumptions", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/UMAP (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "The three assumptions (the docs, verbatim)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "UMAP is founded on three assumptions about the data — that it is uniformly distributed on a Riemannian manifold, that the Riemannian metric is locally constant, and that the manifold is locally connected — and from these it models the manifold with a fuzzy topological structure, taking as the embedding the low-dimensional projection whose fuzzy topological structure is closest to it."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_0401cfd71d", "name": "Manifold is a local property, not a dimension count", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/UMAP (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "Manifold = a local property, not a dimension count", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A manifold is defined by its local structure — every sufficiently small patch looks like ordinary flat space — not by a dimension count or by being folded, so a straight line is a 1-D manifold and an unrolled flat sheet is also a manifold."}
{"aliases": ["controls how UMAP balances local versus global structure in the data", "the local/global tradeoff provided by `n_neighbors`"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_8518f2e498", "name": "n_neighbors is UMAP's input-side dial", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/UMAP (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "`n_neighbors` — the INPUT-side dial (a hard integer)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "UMAP's n_neighbors is an input-side dial, a hard integer k handed to a k-nearest-neighbour query that fixes each point's local chart size; because charts are glued where they overlap and there is no separate global module, k alone carries the local-versus-global trade-off, so small k merely glues together small chains and leaves many components and singleton points, while large k makes the map cohere at the loss of finer local structure."}
{"aliases": ["controls how tightly UMAP is allowed to pack points together", "It, quite literally, provides the minimum distance apart that points are allowed to be in the low dimensional representation."], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_f37a9d967b", "name": "min_dist is UMAP's output-side packing floor", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/UMAP (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "`min_dist` — the OUTPUT-side dial (the packing floor)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "UMAP's min_dist sets the minimum distance apart that points are allowed to be in the low-dimensional representation; it never touches the input graph, so a fragmented graph stays fragmented whatever min_dist is set to, and the floor is enforced as a strong gradient penalty from a fitted smooth curve rather than as a hard wall."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_fcfbe36137", "name": "UMAP global structure is a goal, not a guarantee", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/UMAP (Dimensionality Reduction).md", "message": "Global structure: goal, not guarantee", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "UMAP's preservation of global structure is a design goal rather than a guarantee: negative sampling repels an edge's vertices against randomly sampled vertices across the whole embedding, and the symmetric union lets connectedness transmit pull transitively, but the claim stays at 'arguably' because the spectral Laplacian initialization contributes to it and the documented random-k-NN outlier collapse can drag the whole embedding into one blob."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_9b6db9e4bb", "name": "Meyer's Uniform Access Principle", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Uniform Access Principle.md", "message": "\"all services offered by a module should be available through a **uniform notation**\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Bertrand Meyer's Uniform Access Principle states that all services offered by a module should be available through a uniform notation that does not reveal whether they are implemented through storage (a field) or computation (a method)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_3495a5aedf", "name": "Accessors prevent coupling", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Uniform Access Principle.md", "message": "Accessor functions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Reading and writing object attributes through getter and setter methods rather than exposing raw data structures prevents coupling, because changing the internal representation later does not break external code that used the accessors."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_fa69ef0587", "name": "DTOs may skip uniform access", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Uniform Access Principle.md", "message": "When to skip it", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Data transfer objects — simple data buckets with no behaviour, caching, or logic, used only to move data between layers — do not need accessors, which would add unnecessary ceremony there."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_6d6a0e6e2d", "name": "Value class carries five fields", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Value Class Architecture.md", "message": "\"Each instance has five fields\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A micrograd-style Value instance has five fields: data (the float value), grad (accumulated ∂loss/∂value, starting at 0), _prev (the set of children that created it, which is the computational graph), _backward (a closure that pushes gradient to children), and _op (a label for the operation that created it)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_4725c404f4", "name": "Set of children and accumulating grad", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Value Class Architecture.md", "message": "Key design decisions", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Storing children in a set ensures each child appears once even if it was used multiple times, and grad starts at 0 and is updated with += because gradients accumulate across the multiple paths that reach a node."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_bc06c84e01", "name": "Computational graph is built implicitly", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Value Class Architecture.md", "message": "\"The graph is built **implicitly**\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A micrograd-style autograd engine needs no explicit graph data structure, because every operation returns a new Value whose _prev points at its operands as children, so the graph is built implicitly as operations run."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_8307b0b620", "name": "Vanishing gradients from squashing activations", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Vanishing & Exploding Gradients.md", "message": "\"With sigmoid/tanh activations, each layer multiplies the gradient by a number ≤ 1\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "With sigmoid or tanh activations each layer multiplies the backward-flowing gradient by a factor no greater than one, so after ten or more layers the gradient reaching the input is essentially zero — the vanishing-gradient problem."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_a979636766", "name": "Exploding gradients from large weights", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Vanishing & Exploding Gradients.md", "message": "\"large weight values compound multiplicatively, gradients become huge, and loss goes to NaN\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Large weight values compound multiplicatively through the layers, so gradients become huge and the loss goes to NaN — the exploding-gradient problem."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_5ff19e9aa6", "name": "ReLU and batch norm fix vanishing gradients", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Vanishing & Exploding Gradients.md", "message": "Standard Fixes table — ReLU activation, Batch Normalization", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "ReLU activations, whose gradient is 0 or 1 with no squashing, and batch normalization, which normalizes layer outputs to mean 0 and standard deviation 1, are standard fixes that keep gradients in a healthy range, with ReLU the default for hidden layers."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_2c48c96ebc", "name": "Gradient clipping and lower learning rate fix exploding gradients", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Vanishing & Exploding Gradients.md", "message": "Standard Fixes table — Gradient Clipping, Lower learning rate", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Exploding gradients are standardly fixed by gradient clipping, which caps gradients at a maximum norm, and by lowering the learning rate so that smaller steps are less likely to overshoot into instability."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_28fe78da91", "name": "Variance is an average of squares, so non-negative", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variance is Non-Negative (PSD Covariance).md", "message": "The chain, step 1", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Variance is the average of squared deviations from the mean, Var(X) = E[(X − μ)²] ≥ 0, because every term is a square, and it equals 0 only for a constant variable."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_74020eac13", "name": "Projected variance is uᵀCu", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variance is Non-Negative (PSD Covariance).md", "message": "The chain, step 2", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "For a unit direction u the variance of the projected coordinates xᵀu equals uᵀCu, scaling by ‖u‖² for non-unit u, and this projected variance is exactly the quantity PCA maximizes."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_a84252bdcb", "name": "Covariance matrix is positive semidefinite", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variance is Non-Negative (PSD Covariance).md", "message": "The chain, steps 3–4", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Writing the covariance as C = 1/(n−1)·XcᵀXc with Xc the centered data gives uᵀCu = ‖Xc u‖²/(n−1) ≥ 0 for every vector u, which is the definition of a positive semidefinite matrix."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_af9c8ef5d1", "name": "Covariance eigenvalues are non-negative variances", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variance is Non-Negative (PSD Covariance).md", "message": "The chain, step 5 / Why it matters here", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A positive semidefinite matrix has non-negative eigenvalues, since for an eigenvector u, λ = uᵀCu ≥ 0; the eigenvalues of a covariance matrix are variances along directions, so a zero eigenvalue means zero spread in that direction and a negative eigenvalue out of a numerical computation is float noise rather than information."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_158d08db43", "name": "PSD is a covariance property, not a symmetric-matrix one", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variance is Non-Negative (PSD Covariance).md", "message": "Precise scope", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Positive semidefiniteness is a property of covariance matrices and not of symmetric matrices in general — the symmetric matrix [[0,1],[1,0]] has eigenvalues +1 and −1 — while the 1/(n−1) sample covariance is always PSD and, when n < d, additionally singular with rank at most n−1."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_b741eddea6", "name": "Variation of information is the sum of conditional entropies", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variation of Information.md", "message": "The defining idea / Range, and both extremes", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Variation of information is V(X,Y) = H(X|Y) + H(Y|X), the sum of the two conditional entropies, read as a dissimilarity that is 0 when each variable determines the other and equal to H(X) + H(Y) when the two are independent."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_941223e6d1", "name": "Variation of information in four equivalent forms", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variation of Information.md", "message": "\"The same picture, three other ways\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Since H(X,Y) = H(X|Y) + H(Y|X) + I(X;Y), variation of information also equals H(X,Y) − I(X;Y), H(X) + H(Y) − 2·I(X;Y), and 2·H(X,Y) − H(X) − H(Y), so the measures partition the same bookkeeping as V + I = H(X,Y) and V + 2I = H(X) + H(Y)."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_d5efa8587c", "name": "Variation of information is a true metric", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variation of Information.md", "message": "Why V is a metric — and MI is not", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Variation of information is a true metric on partitions of a finite set by Meilă's theorem — non-negative with V = 0 iff the variables determine each other, symmetric term by term, and satisfying the triangle inequality — whereas mutual information is not, because MI(X,X) = H(X) ≠ 0 and any pure function of I fails the identity axiom."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_31eac97346", "name": "VI's geometric flip survives where arithmetic flips fail", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variation of Information.md", "message": "\"Why the geometric flip survives\"", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Variation of information is not a function of I alone but the Venn complement of the overlap inside the union, built jointly from H(X), H(Y), and H(X,Y), so V(X,X) = H(X) + H(X) − 2·I(X,X) = 0 for every value of H(X); that identically cancelling offset is why the geometric rearrangement survives the identity axiom that kills arithmetic flips of mutual information."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_f6ccc93113", "name": "Metric property gives free bounds and clustering comparison", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Variation of Information.md", "message": "What the metric property buys (why anyone reaches for it)", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because variation of information is a metric, the triangle inequality supplies free bounds through a reference point (VI(A,B) = 0.8 and VI(B,C) = 0.6 imply 0.2 ≤ VI(A,C) ≤ 1.4 without measuring A against C), and VI = 0 exactly when two partitions agree up to relabelling, which makes bits a usable unit for comparing clusterings without label matching."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_a4256a4a28", "name": "Virtual machine emulates a hypothetical chip", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Virtual Machine.md", "message": "How it works", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "A virtual machine is a program that emulates a hypothetical chip by reading bytecode line by line, translating each bytecode instruction into native machine code for the real CPU, and executing the translated instructions."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_a898d17ed4", "name": "VMs exist for portability of bytecode", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Virtual Machine.md", "message": "Why VMs exist", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Without a virtual machine, compiled code runs only on the chip architecture it was compiled for; a virtual machine lets the same bytecode run on any device because the VM performs the device-specific translation, and the cost is that a VM implementation must be built separately for every major platform."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_e0499c321f", "name": "JVM, Python VM, and JS engines as virtual machines", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Virtual Machine.md", "message": "Examples", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The JVM runs Java bytecode, the Python VM runs Python bytecode such as .pyc files, and JavaScript engines such as V8 and SpiderMonkey run JavaScript, often with JIT compilation."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_119081ddc2", "name": "Waterbed theory: complexity is conserved", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Waterbed Theory.md", "message": "Core idea", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The Waterbed Theory, also called the Law of Conservation of Complexity, states that in complex systems such as programming languages, tools, and software designs, complexity cannot be eliminated: pushing it down in one place makes it bulge somewhere else, in the interface, the implementation, or the user's mental model."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_331546e5d1", "name": "Design places complexity where it is least harmful", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Waterbed Theory.md", "message": "Key insight for design", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Because complexity has an irreducible minimum, the design goal is not to eliminate it but to place it where it is most manageable and least harmful for the typical user, which is why explicit trade-offs such as Rust's borrow checker are preferable to hidden complexity that resurfaces when defaults fail."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:21:51Z", "id": "n_cbb0e69c51", "name": "Waterbed theory in language design examples", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Waterbed Theory.md", "message": "Examples in PL design", "session": "backfill-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "Garbage collection pushes memory complexity down but creates complexity around GC pauses, tuning, and allocation patterns; static typing pushes complexity into the type system and compile time while dynamic typing shifts it to runtime errors and testing burden; syntax sugar simplifies common patterns while adding surface area and cognitive load."}
{"aliases": ["P(X = x_i) = c_i / N"], "createdAt": "2026-10-25T23:24:19Z", "id": "n_2e9b5b3ff9", "name": "Marginal probability from a contingency table", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities.md", "message": "contingency-table marginals: P(X = x_i) = c_i / N", "session": "backfill-fix-2026-10-26"}], "statement": "The marginal probability P(X = x_i) equals c_i / N, where c_i is the column total for X = x_i and N is the grand total."}
