{"aliases": ["Device placement must be verified explicitly."], "createdAt": "2026-10-26T00:15:24Z", "id": "n_c0b8a4dfe4", "name": "GPU/CPU device mismatch raises RuntimeError", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/PyTorch Model Building.md", "message": "device placement", "session": "fact-check-graph-2026-10-26", "url": "https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/tensors.html"}], "statement": "If a tensor that participates in computation stays on CPU while the model is on GPU, PyTorch raises a device-mismatch RuntimeError; data must be moved to the model's device before the forward pass."}
{"aliases": ["Adam update is not generally scale-invariant sign descent."], "createdAt": "2026-10-26T00:15:24Z", "id": "n_a36f931558", "name": "Adam is only approximately sign-like", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Adam (adaptive moments).md", "message": "bias correction", "session": "fact-check-graph-2026-10-26", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6980"}], "statement": "At t=1, bias correction makes m-hat = g and v-hat = g squared, so the first step is eta times sign(g); in general m-hat and v-hat are EMAs, so the update is only approximately sign-like when gradients are consistent."}
{"aliases": ["ReLU default is architecture-dependent."], "createdAt": "2026-10-26T00:15:24Z", "id": "n_1b2e9b8daf", "name": "ReLU is a common default hidden-layer activation", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Activation Functions.md", "message": "ReLU", "session": "fact-check-graph-2026-10-26", "url": "https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/nn.html#non-linear-activations"}], "statement": "ReLU has output range [0, infinity) and is a common default for hidden layers in MLPs and CNNs, but many modern architectures such as Transformers use GELU or SwiGLU instead."}
{"aliases": ["ReLU default claim softened."], "createdAt": "2026-10-26T00:15:24Z", "id": "n_1821a504ee", "name": "ReLU and batch norm mitigate vanishing gradients", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Activation Functions.md", "message": "ReLU and batch norm", "session": "fact-check-graph-2026-10-26", "url": "https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/nn.html#non-linear-activations"}], "statement": "ReLU activations, whose gradient is 0 or 1 with no squashing, and batch normalization, which normalizes layer outputs to mean 0 and standard deviation 1, are standard fixes that keep gradients in a healthy range; ReLU is a common default for hidden layers in MLPs and CNNs, though many modern architectures use GELU or SwiGLU."}
{"aliases": ["Smoothing vs regularization analogy only."], "createdAt": "2026-10-26T00:15:24Z", "id": "n_86095ec1b6", "name": "Add-1 smoothing is analogous to L2 regularization", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Add-1 Smoothing.md", "message": "smoothing", "session": "fact-check-graph-2026-10-26", "url": "https://www.deeplearning.ai/"}], "statement": "Add-1 smoothing in count-based models is analogous to L2 regularization in a neural softmax classifier, both pulling predictions toward uniform, but they are not mathematically equivalent."}
{"aliases": ["Graph RAG not universally better."], "createdAt": "2026-10-26T00:15:24Z", "id": "n_9babfaffde", "name": "Graph RAG for aggregation queries", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Knowledge Portfolio.md", "message": "graph rag", "session": "fact-check-graph-2026-10-26", "url": "https://neo4j.com/developer/graph-data-science/"}], "statement": "For queries involving counts, aggregations, or multi-hop relationships, a knowledge graph queried with Cypher or SPARQL can compute exact answers that text or vector RAG cannot; for other queries, text or vector RAG may be preferable."}
{"aliases": ["Teardown not always part of Arrange."], "createdAt": "2026-10-26T00:15:24Z", "id": "n_34928f72d4", "name": "Setup in Arrange; teardown separate when needed", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA).md", "message": "AAA", "session": "fact-check-graph-2026-10-26", "url": "https://wiki.c2.com/?ArrangeActAssert"}], "statement": "In Arrange-Act-Assert, setup belongs to Arrange; teardown is handled separately, or in Arrange, when external resources need cleanup."}
{"aliases": ["The first prompt activates a generic distribution of marketing emails in the model's training data. The second activates a narrow, high-quality slice. Same model. Same parameters. Wildly different outputs.", "A prompt executes nothing — it conditions each next-token distribution."], "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "n_90deb2befe", "name": "A prompt conditions the next-token distribution", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "The first prompt activates a generic distribution of marketing emails in the model's training data. The second activates a narrow, high-quality slice.", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "Insight: a prompt executes nothing — it conditions each next-token distribution", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "Prompt text changes the model's next-token distribution rather than adding anything to its weights or invoking a subroutine: the same model with the same parameters yields a broad, generic distribution of continuations under a vague prompt and a narrow, high-quality slice of them under an engineered one."}
{"aliases": ["The model takes your prompt literally therefore means the model takes the conditioning the text implies seriously, not that it obeys instructions."], "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "n_988817be4e", "name": "Literal instruction-following means implied conditioning", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "The model takes your prompt literally therefore means the model takes the conditioning the text implies seriously, not that it obeys instructions.", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "Every token you send is an instruction, and the model follows instructions literally.", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "Saying a model takes a prompt literally is not a claim that it executes commands or obeys an imperative: it means the model acts on precisely the conditioning the prompt tokens imply, so a vague request is honored as vaguely as it is written and a precise description as precisely — the space of continuations the wording implies is what binds, not the grammatical mood."}
{"aliases": ["Every ambiguity in your prompt is a branch point where the model guesses. Sometimes it guesses right. Sometimes it does not."], "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "n_b591a7427e", "name": "Ambiguity is a fork with split probability mass", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "Every ambiguity in your prompt is a branch point where the model guesses.", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "a vague prompt spreads probability mass over many candidate continuations", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "Every choice a prompt leaves unresolved is a branch point: all continuations consistent with the text stay live as candidate arms, the probability mass splits across them in proportion to their plausibility, and the decoder emits whichever arm it samples, so an ambiguous prompt produces one of several possible answers rather than a predictable one."}
{"aliases": ["The specific version constrains the output space. Fewer valid outputs means higher probability of getting the one you want."], "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "n_c74c812b0a", "name": "Precision deletes fork arms and concentrates mass", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "The specific version constrains the output space. Fewer valid outputs means higher probability of getting the one you want.", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "a precise one deletes arms and piles the same mass onto a few survivors", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "Specifying format, length, audience, and what to include or exclude deletes whole regions of the continuation space, so the same probability mass piles onto the few surviving options and the chance that the emitted output is the desired one rises; the gain from specificity is this concentration, not extra obedience."}
{"aliases": ["\"You are a senior Python developer\" is not a magic spell. It is an activation function.", "When you say \"You are an expert,\" you are biasing the model's sampling distribution toward the expert end of its training data."], "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "n_4f86363788", "name": "Role prompts bias sampling toward expert training text", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "Role Prompting: Why \"You are an expert X\" Works", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "\"You are a senior X\" is not a magic spell but a bias on the sampling distribution: training data contains amateur and expert writing on the same subjects, and a role prompt shifts the next-token distributions toward the expert end of that text, so more specific roles activate narrower and higher-quality slices."}
{"aliases": ["If the role is so specific that few training examples match, the model will hallucinate."], "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "n_a54e93e026", "name": "Over-specific roles hallucinate", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "If the role is so specific that few training examples match, the model will hallucinate.", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "Over-specific roles hallucinate", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "Role specificity has a ceiling: once a role is narrow enough that few high-quality training examples lie at that intersection, the activated slice is thin or empty and the model fills it with confident fabrication, so \"You are the world's foremost expert on quantum gravity string topology\" produces nonsense rather than expertise."}
{"aliases": ["chain-of-thought prompting does not positively impact performance for small models, and only yields performance gains when used with models of ∼ 100B parameters", "chain of thought actually hurts performance for most models smaller than 10B parameters"], "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "n_65585a03a2", "name": "Chain-of-thought is an emergent ability of scale", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - cot-wei.md", "message": "3.2 Results: chain-of-thought prompting is an emergent ability of model scale", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - cot-wei.md", "message": "chain of thought actually hurts performance for most models smaller than 10B parameters", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "Eliciting intermediate reasoning steps before the final answer is an emergent ability of model scale: it yields gains only at roughly 100B parameters and above, while for most models under 10B it lowers accuracy relative to answering directly and those models emit fluent but illogical chains."}
{"aliases": ["Variable compute by itself is not the reason for the success of chain-of-thought prompting", "chain of thought helps the most when three conditions are met: (1) the task is challenging and requires multi-step reasoning, (2) a large language model is used, and (3) the scaling curve is relatively flat"], "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "n_33d9b881fb", "name": "Chain-of-thought gains come from the steps themselves", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - cot-wei.md", "message": "3.3 Ablation Study — Variable compute only", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - cot-wei.md", "message": "A.3 Will chain-of-thought prompting improve performance for my task of interest?", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "Chain-of-thought's advantage is the natural-language reasoning steps, not extra computation or merely reactivating knowledge: prompting a sequence of dots of comparable length performs about like the baseline, placing the chain after the answer performs about like the baseline, and the gains are largest on hard multi-step problems while being negligible or negative on the one- and two-step problems a large model already solves."}
{"aliases": ["This guide assumes that you have: A clear definition of the success criteria for your use case; Some ways to empirically test against those criteria; A first draft prompt you want to improve. If not, spend time establishing that first."], "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "n_75fff1d562", "name": "Prompt work presupposes criteria, tests, and a draft", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - anthropic.md", "message": "Before prompt engineering", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "Anthropic puts the ruler before the tweaking", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "Prompt engineering presupposes three things before any tweaking: a clear definition of the success criteria for the use case, some way to test empirically against those criteria, and a first draft prompt to improve; without them a prompt change cannot be judged, so quality ends up re-decided ad hoc on whatever case is in front."}
{"aliases": ["Implement a prompt testing harness that evaluates prompt changes against a set of expected outputs"], "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "n_113f51dedb", "name": "A prompt harness fixes cases and re-runs on every change", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "Learning Objectives: Implement a prompt testing harness that evaluates prompt changes against a set of expected outputs", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "Step 5: Test Suite Runner", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "without an eval harness that holds fixed cases and re-runs on every change", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "A prompt testing harness holds a fixed set of cases with expected outputs and scoring criteria (length limits, required keywords, forbidden phrases, expected format) and re-runs the whole set on every prompt change, so a change that fixes one case while regressing another is visible before it ships; tweaking only against the case in front yields versions that oscillate, each fixing the latest complaint and breaking a previously passing one."}
{"aliases": ["Temperature=0.0 makes the output deterministic -- the same input produces the same output every time. This is essential for testing and reproducibility.", "Test with temperature=0 first to isolate prompt quality from sampling randomness", "Temperature: a scaling factor on the logit distribution before softmax"], "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "n_fbece097e1", "name": "Determinism is repeatability, not quality", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "Temperature=0.0 makes the output deterministic -- the same input produces the same output every time.", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "Test with temperature=0 first to isolate prompt quality from sampling randomness", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "Determinism makes a run repeatable; it does not say the output is good.", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "Temperature rescales the logits before softmax, so at temperature 0 the distribution collapses onto the top-probability token and the same input always produces the same output; that makes runs reproducible and isolates prompt quality from sampling randomness, but it measures nothing about whether the output is good — a deterministic bad answer is still bad."}
{"aliases": ["Not every success criterion or failing eval is best solved by prompt engineering. For example, you can sometimes improve latency and cost more easily by choosing a different model."], "createdAt": "2026-10-28T23:04:53Z", "id": "n_bfc8d0d453", "name": "Not every failing eval is a prompt problem", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - anthropic.md", "message": "When to prompt engineer", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "Not every success criterion or failing eval is best solved by prompt engineering.", "session": "prompt-engineering-2026-10-28"}], "statement": "Prompt engineering is scoped to success criteria the prompt can control: some unmet criteria and failing evals are better fixed elsewhere, and latency or cost, for instance, are often improved more easily by choosing a different model than by rewriting the prompt."}
{"aliases": ["The scorer is not the ruler.", "the part that is still missing is what the human actually wants"], "createdAt": "2026-10-29T12:25:37Z", "id": "n_e03152dca5", "name": "Harness expected outputs must be authored outside the judged system", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "The scorer is not the ruler. The expected outputs must be human-authored and held outside the system being judged.", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Prompt Engineering — 2026-10-29.md", "message": "sabotage seam press: \"the model writes what it sees as a good, expected output … poisons the rest of the chain of versions\"", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Learning System/Lessons/Lesson — prompt-engineering — 2026-10-28.md", "message": "teammate replaces held expected outputs with the same model grading at temp 0", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}], "statement": "The expected outputs a harness compares against must be authored by the human and held outside the system being judged; if the judged model family writes them (for example by grading every version at temperature 0), the standard moves with the thing it is supposed to measure — what counts as good is re-decided on each run, the very drift the harness was built to catch reappears one level up, and a larger or different scorer does not repair it, because the missing element is what the human actually wants rather than judging capacity."}
{"aliases": ["fluent but illogical chains of thought", "pollutes the rest of the response"], "createdAt": "2026-10-29T12:25:37Z", "id": "n_31e625840e", "name": "An unfounded generated step pollutes every step after it", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - cot-wei.md", "message": "### 3.2 Results — models of smaller scale produced fluent but illogical chains of thought, leading to lower performance than standard prompting", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Learning System/Lessons/Lesson — prompt-engineering — 2026-10-28.md", "message": "Pollution trace produced at first press (pass): \"the next step is built on that shaky foundation … pollutes the rest of the response\"", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "Regime 2 — chain-of-thought can hurt on small models", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}], "statement": "Because each sampled token re-enters the context, a step-by-step answer whose early steps are fluent but unfounded becomes the conditioning for every later step: the error is not corrected downstream but amplified, so wherever chains come out fluent yet illogical — as they do below roughly 100B parameters — prompting for intermediate steps lowers accuracy relative to answering in one shot."}
{"aliases": ["For just a one-off prompt, we don't need it."], "createdAt": "2026-10-29T12:25:37Z", "id": "n_d51c37fce9", "name": "A fixed expected-output set is needed only once versions stack", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Learning System/Lessons/Lesson — prompt-engineering — 2026-10-28.md", "message": "Graduation — entailment check: one-off = a single judgment event, no versions stacking, no held set needed", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "When it is needed: more than one prompt version", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Prompt Engineering — 2026-10-29.md", "message": "one-off link completion (pass)", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}], "statement": "A held set of expected outputs earns its cost only when prompt versions accumulate: a single one-off response is one judgment event with no earlier version to regress against, so no fixed anchor is required, whereas deciding whether v2 beat v1 — and noticing that v2 fixed one case while breaking another — requires the standard to be fixed before either run, because a standard re-decided per run makes \"did this get better?\" undecidable."}
{"aliases": ["concentration measures how hard the model was squeezed, not whether the output is right"], "createdAt": "2026-10-29T12:25:37Z", "id": "n_00da07a1c2", "name": "Distribution concentration measures constraint, not correctness", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "Killed detour: \"the more the probability mass is piled onto a few arms, the better the output\"", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Learning System/Lessons/Lesson — prompt-engineering — 2026-10-28.md", "message": "First answer proposed a wrong ruler — pile/spread of output mass as the quality measure", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "If the role is so specific that few training examples match, the model will hallucinate", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}], "statement": "How much probability mass a prompt piles onto a few continuations measures how tightly the prompt constrained the model, not whether the surviving continuation is right: an over-specific role leaves the mass concentrated on a few likely-wrong arms and the model fabricates confidently, so peakedness cannot serve as a quality score."}
{"aliases": ["improves accuracy on math, logic, and multi-step problems by 10-40%", "chain of thought actually hurts performance for most models smaller than 10B parameters"], "createdAt": "2026-10-29T12:25:37Z", "id": "n_246ac52e58", "name": "A quoted technique gain is a model-regime average", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - rohit.md", "message": "## Key Terms — Chain-of-thought: improves accuracy on math, logic, and multi-step problems by 10-40%", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-10-28 - prompt-engineering - cot-wei.md", "message": "### 3.2 Results — chain-of-thought prompting does not positively impact performance for small models, and only yields performance gains when used with models of ~100B parameters", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Prompt Engineering.md", "message": "⚠️ Sources disagree — the CoT payoff is conditional, not a flat bonus", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}], "statement": "Reported prompting gains are averages over the model regime in which the technique works, not properties of the technique: the frequently quoted 10–40% chain-of-thought improvement is what models of roughly 100B parameters and above deliver on reasoning tasks, and the same step-by-step instruction lowers accuracy on smaller models, so the figure carried without its scale precondition is false exactly where small models are being deployed."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-29T12:25:37Z", "id": "n_113f51dedb", "op": "link", "provenance": [{"file": "Learning System/Lessons/Lesson — prompt-engineering — 2026-10-28.md", "message": "Resume session — row 4: harness assembled, learner words (pass)", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}], "statement": "The prompt harness holds the set of expected outputs and the outputs we actually received, allowing us to compare the two sets for any regression. When we make a change, the prompt runs."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-29T12:25:37Z", "id": "n_65585a03a2", "op": "link", "provenance": [{"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Prompt Engineering — 2026-10-29.md", "message": "Scope stitched (pass): ~100B+ line", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}], "statement": "the 10-40% figure holds for models greater than approximately 100 billion parameters; below that, mass piles on fluent unfounded arms and pollutes"}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-29T12:25:37Z", "id": "n_fbece097e1", "op": "link", "provenance": [{"file": "Learning System/Lessons/Lesson — prompt-engineering — 2026-10-28.md", "message": "row 4 push-down nudge: temp 0 vs quality distinction", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-10-29-prompt-engineering"}], "statement": "flipping it i think wont change whether the output is good or not"}
{"aliases": ["the part of software that is expensive and hard to change later", "the load-bearing structural commitments, judged by what it would cost to reverse them"], "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "n_80a3b1083b", "name": "Software architecture is defined by cost of reversal", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Software Architecture (intro).md", "message": "**Insight:** software architecture is the part of a system that is **expensive and hard to change later** — the load-bearing structural commitments, judged by what it would cost to reverse them.", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}, {"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Software Architecture (intro & non-functional) — 2026-11-06.md", "message": "my model is that software architecture is the part of software that is expensive and hard to change later", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "Software architecture is the part of a system that is expensive and hard to change later: what makes a decision architectural is the cost of reversing it, not its abstract importance."}
{"aliases": ["The discriminator is **cost of reversal**, not abstract importance", "A logo can be important and cheap to change"], "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "n_6bd781365f", "name": "Cost of reversal does not track importance", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Software Architecture (intro).md", "message": "The discriminator is **cost of reversal**, not abstract importance. A logo can be important and cheap to change; the database engine, the service boundaries, or a synchronous-vs-asynchronous integration are commitments whose reversal arrives as a migration.", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "The cost-of-reversal test does not track importance: a logo can be important and cheap to change, while the database engine, the service boundaries, or a synchronous-versus-asynchronous integration are commitments whose reversal arrives as a migration."}
{"aliases": ["The claim is a **prediction about cost**, not a compliment"], "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "n_3d76734b46", "name": "Architectural is a classification, not a compliment", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Software Architecture (intro).md", "message": "The claim is a **prediction about cost**, not a compliment. Calling a decision \"architectural\" says nothing about whether the decision is good; it says what class of decision it is.", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "Calling a decision architectural says what class of decision it is, not whether the decision is good: the claim is a prediction about cost, not a compliment."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "n_ea90687759", "name": "Cost of reversal does not make all structure architectural", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Software Architecture (intro).md", "message": "It does not say architecture is *everything structural* — code style and naming are structural and cheap to change.", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "The cost-of-reversal definition does not make architecture coextensive with everything structural: code style and naming are structural and cheap to change, so they fall outside architecture under this definition."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "n_25669effe2", "name": "The definition does not settle whether the system does its job", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Software Architecture (intro).md", "message": "It does not, on its own, say anything about whether the system **does its job**. That question is separate from whether the system's commitments are cheap or expensive to reverse.", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "The cost-of-reversal definition of architecture says nothing on its own about whether a system does its job: whether a system's commitments are cheap or expensive to reverse and whether the system fulfils its function are separate questions."}
{"aliases": ["a label for **how a running system behaves**, not failures/downtime", "an expectation about behaviour while running"], "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "n_2d3562299b", "name": "Non-functional is a requirements category, not a failure state", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Non-Functional (requirements label).md", "message": "\"non-functional\" is a **requirements category**, not a failure state. Functional requirements say *what the system does*; non-functional requirements say *how well it behaves while doing it*", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-06 - software-architecture - head-first-ch1.md", "message": "the term **\"non-functional\" is filed under requirements** — it is a label for **how a running system behaves**, not a label for failures or downtime.", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "\"Non-functional\" is a requirements category: functional requirements state what the system does, while non-functional requirements state how well it behaves while doing it — the quality attributes such as reliability, performance, availability, security, and scalability."}
{"aliases": ["A system with an unmet non-functional requirement is a system that **runs and behaves badly**, not one that never runs."], "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "n_0798381606", "name": "An unmet non-functional requirement yields a badly-behaving system", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Non-Functional (requirements label).md", "message": "A system with an unmet non-functional requirement is a system that **runs and behaves badly**, not one that never runs.", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "A system with an unmet non-functional requirement is one that runs and behaves badly, not one that never runs."}
{"aliases": ["\"nothing is functioning / the system never starts\"", "They share words, not objects."], "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "n_4f38dd58d1", "name": "Not-functioning and non-functional share words, not objects", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Non-Functional (requirements label).md", "message": "The collision is lexical: *not functioning* (a state of the system) vs *non-functional* (a class of requirement). They share words, not objects.", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "\"Nothing is functioning\" names a state of the system — a deployment failure, a dead system — while \"non-functional\" names a kind of requirement; the two phrases share words, not objects."}
{"aliases": ["Reliability is **one** non-functional requirement; the label covers the quality-attribute family", "\"Unreliable\" is a correct *instance* promoted to the definition."], "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "n_34af519f32", "name": "Reliability is one non-functional requirement, not the label", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Non-Functional (requirements label).md", "message": "**Missing piece (annotation, not a rewrite):** the learner equates *non-functional* with *unreliable*. Reliability is **one** non-functional requirement; the label covers the quality-attribute family", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "Reliability is a single member of the non-functional family — which also includes performance/latency, availability, security, scalability, maintainability, and observability — so \"unreliable\" names an instance of the label, not its definition."}
{"aliases": ["The index placement is the tell", "the index entry files it under **requirements**"], "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "n_2a988061f5", "name": "Filing under requirements identifies the label's kind", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-06 - software-architecture - head-first-ch1.md", "message": "an *index entry* from **Head First Software Architecture, Ch.1**: the term **\"non-functional\" is filed under requirements**", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Non-Functional (requirements label).md", "message": "The index placement is the tell: Head First Software Architecture Ch.1 files \"non-functional\" **under requirements** — which is where a *kind of requirement* files, not where an outage files.", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "Head First Software Architecture Ch.1 files \"non-functional\" under requirements, which is where a kind of requirement files rather than where an outage files — evidence that the label answers \"how well?\" and not \"is it up?\"."}
{"aliases": ["using \"non-functional\" to mean \"broken\" turns a missed quality target into an outage story"], "createdAt": "2026-11-06T01:07:01Z", "id": "n_e40fb3a681", "name": "Reading non-functional as broken hides an unmet requirement", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Non-Functional (requirements label).md", "message": "Why the misreading matters: using \"non-functional\" to mean \"broken\" turns a missed quality target into an outage story, and hides the real engineering situation", "session": "software-architecture-intro-non-functional-2026-11-06"}], "statement": "Using \"non-functional\" to mean \"broken\" turns a missed quality target into an outage story and hides the actual engineering situation: the requirement was never met, rather than the system having fallen over."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "JSON mode", "No schema guarantee", "The output will parse without errors. But it may not match your expected schema -- extra keys, wrong types, missing fields."], "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "n_80a56dc511", "name": "JSON mode guarantees parseable text, not a schema", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - rohit.md", "message": "**JSON mode**: the API guarantees the output is valid JSON. ... But it may not match your expected schema -- extra keys, wrong types, missing fields.", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Structured Outputs (JSON contract).md", "message": "## Where the constraint acts — the ladder", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "JSON mode — OpenAI's `response_format: {\"type\": \"json_object\"}` — guarantees only that the emitted text parses as JSON; it enforces nothing about the caller's schema, so extra keys, wrong types and missing required fields all remain possible, and the response must still be validated against the schema."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "The Structured Output Spectrum", "four levels of structured output control", "where the constraint acts", "The constraint is baked into the decoding process", "This is not a prompt engineering problem. It is a decoding problem."], "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "n_2e39589143", "name": "Three API tiers differ in where the constraint acts", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Structured Outputs (JSON contract).md", "message": "rung 4 is **how rung 3 is implemented**, not a fourth API you call separately", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - rohit.md", "message": "### The Structured Output Spectrum — 'There are four levels of structured output control, each more reliable than the last.'", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "Three tiers are visible from the API and they differ in where the constraint acts: a prompt asking for JSON guarantees nothing because it only shifts probability mass, `json_object` guarantees that the text parses, and `json_schema` with `strict: true` guarantees conformance to the schema that was sent. Constrained decoding is not a fourth tier above schema mode but the mechanism that implements it, so schema mode's guarantee and token masking are one thing seen from two sides."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "parser state", "in_string", "expect", "required_left", "literal", "legal set = `{ t ∈ V : δ(state, t) is defined }`", "a property of the state transition"], "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "n_f31f8dd1dd", "name": "The legal set is decided by parser state, not the last character", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Constrained Decoding (parser-state masking).md", "message": "## The decider, spelled out — legal set = { t ∈ V : δ(state, t) is defined }", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - xgrammar.md", "message": "Their logits are set to − ∞ -\\infty , effectively assigning them zero probability after the softmax operation and preserving the relative probabilities of other valid tokens.", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "The legal next-token set at a decoding step is `{t ∈ V : δ(state, t) is defined}`, with V the tokenizer's vocabulary of multi-character tokens and δ the parser's transition function, so what decides legality is the accumulated parser state rather than the last character: whether a string is open or closed, what is expected next (key-or-close, colon, value, separator-or-close), which required properties are still unwritten, how much of a literal (`t`/`tr`/`tru`) or a number is in progress, and the nesting stack. Each accepted token advances the state, so the next question is asked of a new state rather than of the new suffix, and the decoder applies the answer as a logit mask that sends the excluded tokens' logits to −∞ before the softmax."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "Token masking", "Filtering the vocabulary", "Guided generation", "p(t | s) = softmax( z + m_s )", "deletes arms from the distribution", "a safety net, not a rewriter"], "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "n_d14bda837f", "name": "Masking gives exactly zero probability, not a smaller one", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Constrained Decoding (parser-state masking).md", "message": "## The mechanism: mask, then renormalise", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - xgrammar.md", "message": "At each step, tokens that would violate the required structure are identified as invalid. Their logits are set to − ∞", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "Masked decoding is p(t | s) = softmax(z + m_s), where m_s[t] = 0 for a token the parser accepts and −∞ for one it rejects, so a rejected continuation has probability exactly 0 rather than a small one, the survivors are renormalised into a genuine distribution, and an arm that would leave the grammar is deleted rather than shrunk — which is why the argmax usually stays the token the unconstrained model would have chosen, making masking a safety net and not a rewriter."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "next_valid_tokens", "a last-character rule", "Simulate constrained decoding", "[\"any\"]"], "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "n_631d0704d1", "name": "The teaching next_valid_tokens is a last-character rule", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - rohit.md", "message": "### Step 3: Constrained Token Filter — def next_valid_tokens(partial_json, schema)", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Constrained Decoding (parser-state masking).md", "message": "## Why the rule cannot be the decider — three breakdowns", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "The teaching simulator `next_valid_tokens(partial_json, schema)` decides legality from two facts only — whether `json.loads` succeeds on the prefix, and what the final character is — returning `[\"<EOS>\"]` when the prefix parses and otherwise a category list (`['\"', \"}\"]` after `{`, digits/`true`/`false`/`null`/`[`/`{` after `:`, `[\"a-z\", '\"']` after a quote, `[\"0-9\", \".\", \",\", \"}\", \"]\"]` after a digit), with `[\"any\"]` in the else branch. It has no string-open flag, no required-key counter and no literal counter, and it never reads the schema argument it is given."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "rule vs decider", "the string **closed** — `in_string` flipped", "The lesson's rule is a much simpler machine"], "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "n_79ed0217b1", "name": "A last-character rule fails where two states share a final character", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Constrained Decoding (parser-state masking).md", "message": "the string **closed** — `in_string` flipped; letters are no longer 'inside a string'", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - rohit.md", "message": "partial_states list ending '{\"product\": \"Sony\", \"price\": 348}'", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "A rule keyed on the last character plus parse success returns the wrong legal set at exactly the positions where two different parser states share a final character: after a string value closes (`{\"product\": \"Sony\"` — the rule offers letters, but a complete value can only be followed by `,` or `}`), inside a literal (`{\"product\": tru` — the rule says `[\"any\"]`, the state allows only `e`), and after a prefix that parses while required properties remain unwritten (`{\"product\": \"Sony\", \"price\": 348}` — the rule returns `<EOS>`, the state allows only `,` until every required key exists). A prefix that `json.loads` accepts is therefore not a finished schema instance."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "Hallucinated values", "the type is correct, the value is wrong", "Schema validation cannot catch this"], "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "n_1f407a07c9", "name": "Value correctness is a relation to the source document", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - rohit.md", "message": "**Hallucinated values**: the output matches the schema but contains invented data. ... Schema validation cannot catch this -- the type is correct, the value is wrong.", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Structured Outputs (JSON contract).md", "message": "## Failure modes, and which tier can catch them", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "Whether an extracted value is correct is a relation between the output and the source document, not a property of the output string: `{\"price\": 299.99}` is correctly typed and wrong when the document says $348, and no check that inspects only the string can decide it."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "enforcement tiers catch grammar failures, not semantic ones", "nothing in the stack", "which tier can catch them"], "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "n_fc7be9212a", "name": "Enforcement tiers catch grammar failures, not semantic ones", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Structured Outputs (JSON contract).md", "message": "The one-line version: **enforcement tiers catch grammar failures, not semantic ones.**", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/JSON Schema Validation & Retry (Pydantic + Instructor).md", "message": "## Three guards for three failure classes", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "Enforcement tiers catch exactly the failures decidable from the output string — illegal syntax, a missing required key, extra keys when `additionalProperties: false`, a value outside an enum, array length where the provider enforces `minItems`/`maxItems` — and cannot catch a hallucinated value, whose type is right and value wrong; catching that needs an outside check: an eval harness of hand-labelled cases, a cross-field consistency rule, agreement across repeated extractions, or a human spot check."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "Structured Outputs supports a subset of the JSON Schema language.", "additionalProperties: false must always be set in objects", "Although all fields must be required", "Remove unsupported constraints", "the guarantee holds over the **schema actually sent**"], "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "n_6ac6b1e38e", "name": "The guarantee holds over the schema actually sent", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - openai.md", "message": "Structured Outputs supports a subset of the JSON Schema language.", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - anthropic-structured.md", "message": "Remove unsupported constraints (for example, minimum , maximum , minLength , maxLength )", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Structured Outputs (JSON contract).md", "message": "## What \"guaranteed\" covers — ⚠️ source disagreement, resolved", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "Every provider enforces only a subset of JSON Schema, so a schema-mode guarantee holds over the schema actually sent to the decoder, not over the one the caller wrote: OpenAI's strict mode rejects composition keywords (`allOf`, `not`, `if`/`then`/`else`, dependent schemas), requires the root to be an object with no top-level `anyOf`, requires `additionalProperties: false` on every object and requires every field to be `required`; Anthropic's native tier rejects numerical constraints (`minimum`, `maximum`, `multipleOf`), string constraints (`minLength`, `maxLength`), external `$ref` and recursive schemas, and its `transform_schema()` deletes the unsupported ones and moves them into field descriptions. A dropped `minimum: 0` therefore does not stop a negative price, and only a client-side validation against the original schema recovers the check."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "a validator is a predicate over the keywords it implements", "any keyword you do not implement is silently satisfied", "validate(data, schema) == []"], "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "n_bfc6012744", "name": "A validator is a predicate over the keywords it implements", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/JSON Schema Validation & Retry (Pydantic + Instructor).md", "message": "## The validator: a structural recursion", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - rohit.md", "message": "### Step 1: JSON Schema Validator — def validate_schema(data, schema)", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "A schema validator is a predicate over the keywords it implements, not a proof of correctness: `validate(data, schema) == []` means only that no rule the implementation knows about was violated, so any keyword it never reads is silently satisfied. The load-bearing families are type membership, presence (`required`, `minItems`, `maxItems`) and closure (`additionalProperties`, `patternProperties` — the only pruning family), and since JSON Schema allows unlisted keys unless closure is set, a schema without `additionalProperties: false` is looser than its author believes."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "It repairs, it does not guarantee.", "Automatic Retries", "The retry sometimes produces different data.", "max_retries"], "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "n_1e578247a2", "name": "A retry loop repairs; it does not guarantee, and it can change the data", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - instructor.md", "message": "Automatic Retries : Built-in retry logic when validation fails", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - rohit.md", "message": "The retry sometimes produces different data. Now you have a consistency problem on top of a parsing problem.", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/JSON Schema Validation & Retry (Pydantic + Instructor).md", "message": "## The retry loop (Instructor) and what it costs", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "Sending the validation error back into the conversation and resampling, up to `max_retries` (Instructor's example uses 3), repairs rather than guarantees: the terminal state after the retries is a failure the caller must still handle, and because each attempt is a fresh sample conditioned on an error message, a value that was right on the first attempt can come back different, converting a syntax problem into a consistency problem for everything downstream."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "an index over a language model's vocabulary", "𝒪(1)", "context-independent tokens", "context-dependent tokens", "under 40 µs per token", "adaptive token mask cache", "finite state machine"], "createdAt": "2026-11-09T11:47:58Z", "id": "n_7587309182", "name": "Token indexing is the cost of masked decoding", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - outlines-paper.md", "message": "allow the construction of an index with which the set of non-zero-probability tokens can be obtained efficiently at each step. The result is an algorithm that costs 𝒪(1) on average.", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-09 - structured-outputs - xgrammar.md", "message": "this technique reduces context-dependent tokens by 90% (from 1,134 to 120)", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Constrained Decoding (parser-state masking).md", "message": "## Characters vs tokens: the indexing problem", "session": "01a1bf90-c0bf-74e2-9e87-6014964ff653"}], "statement": "A grammar is defined over characters while generation emits tokens that can carry several structural characters (`\":{\"`) or straddle a character boundary, so validity has to be tested per vocabulary token, and rescanning all ~100k+ tokens against the automaton costs O(N) per generated token. Outlines compiles the schema to a finite-state machine and builds an index from FSM state to the vocabulary strings that leave it, giving O(1) average lookup; XGrammar handles context-free grammars with a byte-level pushdown automaton and splits the vocabulary into context-independent tokens, decided from the stack top alone and precomputed into an adaptive mask cache, and context-dependent tokens that need the whole stack — for Llama-3.1 with a JSON grammar the context-dependent share is under 1% (1,134 of 128k tokens, cut to 120 by context expansion) and mask generation costs under 40 µs per token for JSON Schema and CFG-JSON."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "the machine knows the schema and legal set. what it would have to know is the exact value for the price key of this particular json object. it slips through since it is a valid entry(number).", "Schema validation cannot catch this -- the type is correct, the value is wrong.", "legality, never truth"], "createdAt": "2026-11-10T11:37:17Z", "id": "n_c410102a30", "name": "A schema-side gate decides legality, never truth", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Structured Outputs (JSON, schema validation, constrained decoding) — 2026-11-10.md", "message": "\"the machine knows the schema and legal set. what it would have to know is the exact value for the price key of this particular json object. it slips through since it is a valid entry(number).\"", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-10 - structured-outputs - rohit.md", "message": "Hallucinated values: the output matches the schema but contains invented data. ... Schema validation cannot catch this -- the type is correct, the value is wrong.", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Structured Outputs (JSON contract).md", "message": "## Failure modes, and which tier can catch them", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}], "statement": "Every gate in a structured-output stack — prompt, JSON mode, schema mode, constrained decoding, validator, retry — answers its question from the emitted string and the schema alone: does it parse, are the required keys present, are the types right, is the value inside the enum, is the array length in range. Whether `299.99` is the price the source document stated is a relation between the output and a document that no gate in the loop holds, so a hallucinated value is legal by construction and passes every tier; catching it takes an outside check that holds the source — an eval harness of hand-labelled cases, a cross-field consistency rule, agreement across repeated extractions, or a human spot check."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "what it validates against is whether this breaks the schema or not. the info needed to flag this output doesnt exist anywhere in that loop.", "the loop validates legality, not truth"], "createdAt": "2026-11-10T11:37:17Z", "id": "n_495e8112ca", "name": "A valid-format hallucination never enters the retry loop", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Structured Outputs (JSON, schema validation, constrained decoding) — 2026-11-10.md", "message": "(Instructor) \"what it validates against is whether this breaks the schema or not. the info needed to flag this output doesnt exist anywhere in that loop.\"", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/JSON Schema Validation & Retry (Pydantic + Instructor).md", "message": "**Version 2 fragment — what the loop cannot see**", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}], "statement": "Instructor's validate-and-retry loop is entered only by a validation error, so an invented but schema-valid value raises none: the loop terminates on the first attempt and returns the hallucination as a validated object. Retrying cannot repair that, because the only message the loop feeds back names schema violations, so there is no error to resample on and nothing in the loop knows the number was invented."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "the prompt lowers \"available's probability", "lowered ≠ zero", "Good constrained decoding prevents this. Prompt-based approaches do not."], "createdAt": "2026-11-10T11:37:17Z", "id": "n_97c949704c", "name": "A prompt lowers an illegal continuation's probability; it does not delete it", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Structured Outputs (JSON, schema validation, constrained decoding) — 2026-11-10.md", "message": "the prompt lowers \"available's probability — patched in-session to lowered ≠ zero, so the sampler can still emit it; the absent component named: FSM/CFG", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-10 - structured-outputs - rohit.md", "message": "Enum confusion: ... Good constrained decoding prevents this. Prompt-based approaches do not.", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Constrained Decoding (parser-state masking).md", "message": "## Where the mask sits — the enum seam, rung by rung", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}], "statement": "At the prompting rung an off-enum string such as `\"available\"` keeps non-zero probability: a prompt re-weights the next-token distribution and never removes an arm, and the component that would remove it — an FSM or CFG acting on the decoder — is exactly what that rung lacks, so the sampler can still emit it. Lowering an illegal continuation's probability is not the same as making it impossible, which is why enum confusion survives prompting and is impossible under masking."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "raw distibuton → constrained decoding(fsm/cfg) → sampler", "constrained decoding is the one that hands the valid distribution to the sampler"], "createdAt": "2026-11-10T11:37:17Z", "id": "n_83606d1a83", "name": "The grammar mask modifies the distribution handed to the sampler", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Structured Outputs (JSON, schema validation, constrained decoding) — 2026-11-10.md", "message": "\"raw distibuton → constrained decoding(fsm/cfg) → sampler\"", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Constrained Decoding (parser-state masking).md", "message": "## Pipeline order — why the mask must precede the sampler", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}], "statement": "The decoding pipeline runs raw next-token distribution → constrained decoding (FSM/CFG) → sampler: the grammar constraint rewrites the distribution before any token is drawn, so what the sampler receives already has the illegal arms at zero rather than being checked against the grammar after it has drawn. The decoder masks; the sampler samples — neither does the other's job."}
{"aliases": ["Structured Outputs (JSON contract)", "its zeroing wouldnt be worth anything since the impure distribution has already been sampled from."], "createdAt": "2026-11-10T11:37:17Z", "id": "n_d00afefaaf", "name": "Zeroing a token after it has been sampled is a no-op", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Structured Outputs (JSON, schema validation, constrained decoding) — 2026-11-10.md", "message": "\"its zeroing wouldnt be worth anything since the impure distribution has already been sampled from.\"", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Constrained Decoding (parser-state masking).md", "message": "## Pipeline order — why the mask must precede the sampler", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}], "statement": "A sample is decided at the draw: once the sampler has picked a token from the unrestricted distribution, zeroing or deleting that token afterwards changes nothing about what was emitted. A constraint applied after sampling is therefore worthless rather than weak — it cannot withdraw an emission that has already happened."}
{"aliases": [], "createdAt": "2026-11-10T11:37:17Z", "id": "n_d14bda837f", "op": "link", "provenance": [{"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Structured Outputs (JSON, schema validation, constrained decoding) — 2026-11-10.md", "message": "the masking rung: enum consulted, `available` zeroed, mass handed to sampler — pass", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Constrained Decoding (parser-state masking).md", "message": "## Where the mask sits — the enum seam, rung by rung", "session": "01a1c4bb-1d4c-722e-a852-abe2ec18202d"}], "statement": "constrained decoding masks \"available\" and hence high probability for \"in_stock\", \"out_of_stock\", \"preorder\" to be sampled from. constrained decoding is the one that hands the valid distribution to the sampler"}
{"aliases": ["Schemas support minItems and maxItems but not all providers enforce them at the decoding level", "it is the provider's property"], "createdAt": "2026-11-11T14:07:51Z", "id": "n_4b76c3d5d2", "name": "Length enforcement is a provider property, decided at compile time", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-11 - structured-outputs - rohit.md", "message": "Array length : the model may produce too many or too few items in an array. Schemas support minItems and maxItems but not all providers enforce them at the decoding level.", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-11-11-structured-outputs-s3"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Structured Outputs (JSON contract).md", "message": "### Session 3 attack chain — the array-length seam (2026-11-11, CLOSED)", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-11-11-structured-outputs-s3"}, {"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Structured Outputs (JSON, schema validation, constrained decoding) — 2026-11-11.md", "message": "4. **Whose property the failure is**", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-11-11-structured-outputs-s3"}], "statement": "Whether `minItems` and `maxItems` are enforced during decoding is a property of the provider, not of structured-output mode: a schema keyword the provider does not compile into grammar state never exists at decode time, so no component of the decoder can enforce it. Two applications sending the same schema through the same mode can therefore differ in enforcement purely by provider or model tier — a fine-tuned deployment drops more keywords than the base model, and a tier that supports only `minItems` of 0 or 1 silently loses the constraint — which is why `minItems`/`maxItems` support in a schema is not evidence that an array's length is enforced."}
{"aliases": ["a number, not a boolean", "the counter says 2 which means , is invalid while ] is valid"], "createdAt": "2026-11-11T14:07:51Z", "id": "n_7f0e2b20d8", "name": "Length legality needs an item counter, not a flag set", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Constrained Decoding (parser-state masking).md", "message": "## Array length: why flags × schema is not enough (2026-11-11, CLOSED)", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-11-11-structured-outputs-s3"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/raw/sources/2026-11-11 - structured-outputs - rohit.md", "message": "Array length : the model may produce too many or too few items in an array.", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-11-11-structured-outputs-s3"}, {"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Structured Outputs (JSON, schema validation, constrained decoding) — 2026-11-11.md", "message": "3. **What the enforcing mask must carry**", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-11-11-structured-outputs-s3"}], "statement": "Enforcing `minItems`/`maxItems` needs state that no boolean flag set can supply: the mask keeps a counter of items emitted in the current array and makes `]` legal iff `count ≥ minItems`, `,` legal only while `count < maxItems`. The reason is that the flags are identical at the positions where length legality diverges — after items 1, 2 and 3 of an array declared `{minItems: 3, maxItems: 5}`, `in_string` is closed, `expect` is separator-or-close, `required_left` is unchanged and no literal is in progress at any of the three, yet `]` becomes legal only at the third — so length legality is a function of how many items this array has already emitted, and not of any tuple of booleans."}
{"aliases": ["enforced path, non-enforced", "the validator is idle on the enforced path and catching on the non-enforced path"], "createdAt": "2026-11-11T14:07:51Z", "id": "n_0e20a8fe58", "name": "What the validator has left to catch is a property of the path", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/JSON Schema Validation & Retry (Pydantic + Instructor).md", "message": "## Where the validator's verdict lands — path-dependent (2026-11-11)", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-11-11-structured-outputs-s3"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Structured Outputs (JSON contract).md", "message": "**Path-dependence is not another mechanism.** The two-blank fill is a *locational* claim: the validator is the same code on both paths. What differs is whether anything is left for it to catch.", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-11-11-structured-outputs-s3"}, {"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Structured Outputs (JSON, schema validation, constrained decoding) — 2026-11-11.md", "message": "the no-op is a property of the PATH, not of the validator (mask guarantees → validator idle; mask absent → the validator is the only catcher of length legality)", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-11-11-structured-outputs-s3"}], "statement": "Whether a post-sampler legality validator has anything to catch is a property of the path, not of the validator: on the enforced path, where the provider compiled the length keywords and the mask already deleted every illegal continuation, it is idle; on the non-enforced path it is the only thing in the stack that will ever see a length violation. \"The validator is null and void\" is therefore true of only one of the two paths, and because the no-op is redundancy by construction rather than a defect, reading that redundancy as the layer being pointless inverts which side of the condition it sits on — the same code is load-bearing the moment provider coverage drops."}
{"aliases": ["the ground truth comparison will have to sit after the post sampler validator step"], "createdAt": "2026-11-11T14:07:51Z", "id": "n_d588e4cf30", "name": "Value truth lives outside the output path", "op": "define", "provenance": [{"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/JSON Schema Validation & Retry (Pydantic + Instructor).md", "message": "**Version 3 — where the loop's verdict lands** (2026-11-11, verbatim)", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-11-11-structured-outputs-s3"}, {"file": "Knowledge Wiki/wiki/Structured Outputs (JSON contract).md", "message": "On the enforcing path the post-sampler legality validator is a **no-op**; value truth is checked, if at all, **outside the output path**", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-11-11-structured-outputs-s3"}, {"file": "Learning System/Sessions/Session — Structured Outputs (JSON, schema validation, constrained decoding) — 2026-11-11.md", "message": "(repair 1) *\"the ground truth comparison will have to sit after the post sampler validator step\"* — on the enforcing path the validator is a no-op for legality; truth-checking lives outside the pipe.", "session": "clerk-ingest-2026-11-11-structured-outputs-s3"}], "statement": "Value truth has no home inside the output path: the mask never sees the source document and the validator's oracle is the schema, so no component between the sampler and the caller can distinguish a legal value that is true from a legal value that was invented. A ground-truth comparison must therefore sit after the post-sampler validator step — outside the pipeline rather than as one more stage of it — which is why placing the detector at the constrained decoder is a category error even on the enforced path."}
